window.KB_EXPLAIN_EN_ADD({"L3-T3A.1.1":"An LLM inference request travels from the client through the API server, the scheduler, the GPU engine's main loop, and detokenization before streaming back. Mapping out this path is how you figure out which stage is actually \"slow\".\n\n**How it works**\n- The client sends the request over HTTP / SSE (the OpenAI-compatible API is the de facto standard).\n- The API server handles authentication, rate limiting, applying the chat template, and tokenization, all on the CPU.\n- The scheduler maintains a waiting queue and a running set and decides which requests to process in this iteration and how many tokens for each.\n- Each iteration of the engine's main loop is one forward pass: prefill runs the prompt's N tokens through all layers in parallel and writes the KV cache; decode feeds only 1 new token per sequence; sampling goes logits → (optional grammar mask) → temperature / top-p → token id.\n- After incremental detokenization (handling multi-byte characters), the output is streamed back, and the KV cache is released on EOS / stop / max_tokens.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nOnly the engine's main loop runs on the GPU; everything else is on the CPU. Under heavy load, tokenization, detokenization, JSON serialization, and tool-call parsing can all become bottlenecks: vLLM measured about 15 ms of CPU time just to tokenize a 9k-token prompt. That is why 2026 inference stacks began splitting out the \"rendering layer\" to scale horizontally on its own.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to draw the full path \"gateway → scheduling → prefill → decode → sampling → streaming\" and say whether each stage affects TTFT or TPOT.\n- When asked about \"low GPU utilization but still slow\", think of the CPU-side stages.","L3-T3A.1.2":"Prefill and decode are the two phases of inference. They use the same weights but have completely different compute profiles, and almost every inference optimization can be classified as \"targeting one of the two\".\n\n**How it works**\n- **Prefill**: the input is the whole prompt (N tokens), parallel within the sequence, processing N positions in one forward pass. The matrix multiplications are large and the bottleneck is **compute**; it determines TTFT. Main optimizations: prefix caching, chunked prefill, FlashAttention, P/D disaggregation.\n- **Decode**: each sequence has only 1 token per step, so generating M tokens takes M forward passes. Each step reads all the weights plus that sequence's KV cache yet computes only one position, so the bottleneck is **memory bandwidth**; it determines TPOT / ITL. Main optimizations: batching, quantization, speculative decoding, KV compression.\n- Back-of-the-envelope: an 8B model's BF16 weights are about 16 GB and H100 bandwidth is about 3.35 TB/s, so the lower bound per step for a single stream is about 16 GB ÷ 3.35 TB/s ≈ 4.8 ms, i.e. a single-stream ceiling of about 200 tok/s.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nPutting both phases in the same batch causes interference: a long prefill stalls decode. Chunked prefill and P/D disaggregation emerged to solve exactly this.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Standard phrasing: prefill is compute-bound and determines TTFT; decode is memory-bandwidth-bound and determines TPOT.\n- Follow-up \"does the bottleneck change as batch grows\": past the roofline knee (about batch 300 for H100 BF16), decode becomes compute-bound too.\n- MoE models must fit all experts in memory, but bandwidth is consumed only by the activated ones.","L3-T3A.1.3":"The fact that \"decode is memory-bandwidth-bound\" leads to a chain of engineering conclusions, and interviewers often use them to test whether you truly understand this bottleneck.\n\n**How it works**\nFor every token decode generates, all the weights (plus this sequence's KV) are read from memory once; step time ≈ \"bytes read ÷ bandwidth\", and compute sits mostly idle. From this it follows:\n1. **Batching is nearly free**: concatenating B sequences still reads the weights only once but produces B tokens. Until you hit the compute bottleneck, throughput grows roughly linearly with batch while per-step latency rises only slowly.\n2. **Quantization speeds up decode**: 4-bit weights take about 1/4 the bytes of BF16, so less data is read per step. This is especially pronounced on-device, where bandwidth is only tens to hundreds of GB/s.\n3. **Long context slows decode**: each step must also read the entire KV cache, so the longer the context, the higher the TPOT.\n4. **Output costs more than input**: input can be prefilled in parallel, while output must be generated serially. API output prices are typically 4–5x input prices, and there is a physical reason for it.\n5. **Macs can run models but not fast**: unified memory can hold the weights, but bandwidth is far below data-center GPUs, and token speed is roughly proportional to \"bandwidth ÷ model bytes\".\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThese map respectively to continuous batching, quantization, KV compression, API pricing, and on-device hardware choices. \"Free\" only holds in the bandwidth-bound regime; past the knee, batching is no longer free.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to estimate the single-stream tok/s ceiling on the spot with \"weight bytes ÷ bandwidth\".\n- Explain why output tokens are priced higher than input tokens.","L3-T3A.1.4":"TTFT, TPOT, ITL, and E2E latency are the four basic metrics for LLM serving latency, corresponding to \"how fast is the first token\", \"how fast are the rest\", \"does it stutter\", and \"how long overall\".\n\n**How it works**\n- **TTFT** (Time To First Token): from sending the request to receiving the first output token. Determined by network, queueing, tokenization, prefill (related to input length and cache hit rate), and the first decode step.\n- **TPOT** (Time Per Output Token): average time per token after the first. Determined by per-step decode time, affected by model size, batch, context length, and interruptions from prefill.\n- **ITL** (Inter-Token Latency): the interval between consecutive tokens, a distribution. ITL p99 exposes \"stutter\" in streamed output, which the TPOT average smooths away.\n- **E2E latency**: total request time, E2E ≈ TTFT + TPOT × (number of output tokens − 1), where TTFT ≈ queueing + prefill(N_in).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe four metrics point to different optimizations: TTFT relies on reducing queueing, prefix caching, chunked prefill; TPOT / ITL rely on controlling batch size, quantization, speculative decoding. E2E alone can't localize problems, and averages alone miss tail stutter.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Write the E2E decomposition and name the dominant factor of each term.\n- In streamed responses one chunk may contain several tokens, so compute TPOT from the token count returned in usage, not by counting chunks.\n- Distinguish TPOT (an average) from ITL (a distribution).","L3-T3A.1.5":"Throughput can be measured in several ways, so before quoting a number, ask which one. A reasoning model's \"first token\" also needs its own definition, or latency metrics will mislead.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Throughput**: how much the system processes per unit time, either tokens/s (distinguish output-only from input + output) or requests/s, determined by batch size, GPU compute, and bandwidth.\n- **Interactivity**: the speed an individual user perceives = 1 / TPOT (tok/s/user), which trades off against system throughput.\n- From a single user's perspective tok/s = 1/TPOT; from the system's perspective throughput ≈ concurrency / TPOT. Vendors' \"tens of thousands of tok/s per GPU\" is usually total system throughput at high concurrency, often counting input tokens too, while an individual user still experiences only tens of tok/s.\n- **First answer token**: a model with reasoning may first generate thousands of thinking tokens; what the user is really waiting for is time-to-first-answer-token, which is not TTFT. Track the two separately in monitoring.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nUnclear definitions lead to wrong hardware choices and capacity plans: estimating single-user experience from system throughput badly overestimates it. A reasoning model's thinking tokens can make TTFT look great while the user experience is poor.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"TPOT and tok/s aren't simply reciprocals\" — distinguish the single-user and system perspectives.\n- When you see a vendor benchmark, first ask: total throughput or per-user speed, are input tokens counted, and at what concurrency.\n- Add latency to the first visible answer token to monitoring for reasoning models.","L3-T3A.1.6":"Raising concurrency increases both throughput and latency, forming a throughput–latency curve. Goodput answers \"where on this curve to stop\": the maximum load you can sustain while meeting latency SLOs.\n\n**How it works**\n- The curve's x-axis is total system throughput (tok/s/GPU) and its y-axis is per-user speed (tok/s/user). At low concurrency every user is fast but most bandwidth is wasted; at high concurrency total throughput peaks and per-user experience is worst; in between is a \"knee\", past which extra concurrency gives smaller throughput gains and markedly worse latency.\n- **Goodput**: DistServe (OSDI 2024) defines it as the maximum request rate the system can sustain while the fraction of requests meeting both TTFT and TPOT SLOs stays at target (e.g., 90%).\n- SemiAnalysis's InferenceX (formerly InferenceMAX) benchmark compares hardware and engines using \"interactivity vs. throughput\" Pareto curves rather than a single number.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nProduction systems shouldn't chase either end of the curve. Chasing throughput alone makes many requests time out, and timed-out requests are wasted compute; chasing low latency alone leaves GPUs underutilized and unit cost high. Goodput ties \"useful output\" to SLOs and is the right basis for capacity planning and engine comparisons.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Define goodput in one sentence: throughput that meets SLOs.\n- Compare engines or hardware by the whole Pareto curve, not a single peak-throughput point.\n- The optimization target of P/D disaggregation (DistServe) is goodput.","L3-T3A.1.7":"SLOs should be set by product type. Different products guard different metrics, and a single set of engine parameters can't satisfy every scenario at once.\n\n**How it works**\nThese are rule-of-thumb magnitudes; in an interview, state that they are assumptions:\n- **Chat**: TTFT p95 < 1 s, TPOT corresponding to ≥ 20–50 tok/s (faster than human reading speed). Guard TTFT and TPOT.\n- **Code completion**: stricter first-token requirement, TTFT < 200–300 ms.\n- **Voice conversation**: end-to-end first response < 500 ms.\n- **Offline batch processing**: only throughput, unit price, and completion deadline matter; you can use a Batch API for a discount.\n- **Enterprise APIs**: besides latency, manage rate limits (RPM / TPM), tenant fairness, failure retries, and peak capacity.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nSLOs determine how large the batch can be, whether to enable speculative decoding, how large to make chunks, and whether to use P/D disaggregation. Interactive scenarios sacrifice throughput for latency, while offline scenarios max out the batch for the lowest unit price. When both kinds of traffic share a cluster, separate them with priority scheduling.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When asked \"how do you set SLOs\", first ask about the product type, then give magnitudes, and stress p95 / p99 rather than averages.\n- Once SLOs are set, what you actually optimize is goodput.\n- When co-located, give interactive requests high priority and offline batch jobs low priority.","L3-T3A.1.8":"LLM serving should watch tail latency such as p99, not averages. There are three reasons: latency distributions are heavy-tailed, agents amplify the tail, and queueing time diverges at high utilization.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Heavy-tailed distribution**: input and output lengths span several orders of magnitude, and a 100k-token prefill stalls other requests in the same batch — something averages completely miss.\n- **Agents amplify tail latency**: if a task calls the model 20 times in sequence, the probability that at least one call falls beyond the single-call p99 is 1 − 0.99^20 ≈ 18%. For agents, the single-call p99 is close to the task-level p80.\n- **Queueing intuition**: as utilization ρ approaches 1, queueing time diverges as 1/(1−ρ). So capacity planning must leave headroom; a common practice is targeting 60–70% utilization rather than saturating GPUs based on average throughput.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nAverages get pulled down by large numbers of short requests, while the 1% stragglers are often exactly the most important long requests. Leaving utilization headroom means buying more GPUs, trading cost for tail latency. The alternative is admission control: reject quickly under overload to protect requests already admitted.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Compute 1 − 0.99^20 ≈ 18% on the spot to show why agents are especially sensitive to tail latency.\n- Explain why the target utilization is 60–70% rather than 100%.\n- Set alerts on p95 / p99, not averages.","L3-T3A.1.9":"Open-loop load testing sends requests at a fixed arrival rate regardless of whether earlier requests have finished. It faithfully exposes queueing time and avoids the coordinated omission bias of closed-loop testing.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Closed-loop**: fixed concurrency; the next request is sent only after the previous one finishes. As soon as the system slows, the offered load drops automatically, masking queueing time, so measured latency is optimistic. This is coordinated omission.\n- **Open-loop**: send requests at a set arrival rate (e.g., a Poisson process), which faithfully shows how queues grow under overload.\n- **Computing TPOT**: in streamed responses one chunk may contain several tokens. Enable `stream_options={\"include_usage\": True}` on the client so the last chunk carries usage, then compute (t_last − t_first) / (completion_tokens − 1) rather than counting chunks.\n- **Also collect engine-side metrics**: vLLM exposes Prometheus metrics at `/metrics`, including TTFT / ITL / E2E histograms, running / waiting counts, KV usage, and prefix cache hits. Client TTFT minus engine TTFT is the network and gateway overhead.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nCapacity measured with closed-loop testing is overstated and collapses at the first production peak. Open-loop testing requires sweeping multiple arrival rates to draw the full throughput–latency curve.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain what coordinated omission is and why it underestimates latency.\n- Compute TPOT from the token count returned by the server.\n- Subtracting engine metrics from client metrics localizes latency sources layer by layer.","L3-T3A.1.10":"Whether load-test numbers mean anything depends on whether the workload resembles real traffic. The industry has several dedicated tools that send load at a set arrival rate and report percentiles.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Features the workload must reproduce**: input / output length distributions, prefix reuse rate, and whether it is multi-turn. Numbers from fixed short prompts are useless for agent workloads, because agents can have input-to-output ratios above 100:1 and depend heavily on prefix caching.\n- **Common tools**: `vllm bench serve` (bundled with vLLM), GuideLLM, NVIDIA's genai-perf / AIPerf, and llmperf. All support sending load at a set arrival rate and report TTFT / ITL percentiles.\n- **Best data source**: replay real production traces (de-identified), or synthesize workloads from production statistics on length distributions and prefix-sharing structure.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nPrefix reuse rate has a huge effect: on the same hardware, whether prefix caching is on and how high the hit rate is can change cost by an order of magnitude. The variance of the length distribution also affects conclusions: the higher the variance of output lengths, the worse static batching does and the more chunked prefill matters. Synthetic workloads make it easy to control variables but may miss the long tail of real traffic.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Name the four load-testing essentials: open-loop, realistic workload, counting tokens from usage, collecting engine metrics at the same time.\n- Name two or three tools and their common capabilities.\n- Stress that agent workloads must reproduce multi-turn conversations and prefix sharing.","L3-T3A.1.11":"\"Where is this API request slow\" is a frequent question. The key to answering well is connecting the application-layer path and the engine-layer path into one, and for each stage stating which metric it affects, the common causes, and what data to look at.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Gateway**: authentication, quotas, RPM / TPM rate limiting, context-length validation. Affects TTFT; look at the 429 rate.\n- **Application layer / LLM gateway**: prompt assembly, RAG retrieval, guardrails, fallback. Look at span durations for each stage.\n- **Replica selection**: picking a busy replica, or missing the prefix cache. Look at per-replica queue depth and hit rate.\n- **Queueing and scheduling**: the main variable in TTFT. Peaks, long requests blocking the head of the queue, and insufficient KV all cause queueing. Look at queueing time p99 and the waiting count.\n- **Prefill**: long inputs, cache misses, recomputation after preemption. Look at the input token distribution and preemption count.\n- **Decode**: batch too large, context too long, interrupted by long prefills. Look at ITL p99 and KV utilization.\n- **Streaming**: buffering by proxies or CDNs, client disconnects. Compare client first-token time minus server TTFT.\n- **Wrap-up and monitoring**: record token counts, TTFT, TPOT, retries, timeouts, instance ID. Look at timeout rate, retry rate, unit cost.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nTroubleshooting rule of thumb: slow first token → check queueing first, then prefill; choppy output afterward → check decode and preemption; rising failure rate → check rate limits, memory, timeouts; client experience much worse than server metrics → check the network and proxy buffering.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to narrate the whole path in about 45 seconds.\n- TTFT p99 worsens but ITL is normal: check queueing, falling prefix hit rate, KV near full, tokenization. The reverse: check whether batch is past the knee, chunks are too large, speculative decoding acceptance rate, context length, and GPU throttling.\n- Put change logs and metrics on the same timeline; most \"sudden degradations\" line up with some change.","L3-T3A.2.1":"From the serving side, the KV cache is the scarcest resource in an inference service. Weights are a fixed cost shared by all requests; the KV cache is a variable cost that every in-flight sequence occupies separately.\n\n**How it works**\n- Formula: KV bytes = 2 × L × n_kv × d_head × bytes × T × B, where 2 means one copy each of K and V, L is the number of layers, n_kv the number of KV heads, d_head the head dimension, bytes the bytes per element, T the context length, and B the number of concurrent sequences.\n- The concurrency one GPU can serve ≈ (memory − weights − overhead) ÷ per-sequence KV size.\n- Causal chain: concurrency determines batch size, batch determines throughput, throughput determines cost per token.\n- Example: Llama-3.1-8B (32 layers, 8 KV heads, d_head = 128, BF16) has 128 KiB of KV per token.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nPer-token KV size is a core parameter of a model's \"serving cost\". Architectural designs such as GQA, MLA, sliding windows, and sparse attention are all motivated by compressing KV. On the serving side you can also quantize KV (FP8 doubles capacity) and do architecture-aware hybrid KV management (in 2026 both vLLM and SGLang implemented unified hybrid KV managers).\n\n**Interview notes**\n- The formula uses the number of KV heads n_kv, not query heads. Under GQA, n_kv is far smaller than the number of query heads.\n- Be able to compute a model's per-token KV size and maximum concurrency on the spot.\n- Be able to explain \"why the KV cache determines unit price\".","L3-T3A.2.2":"KV cache lifecycle management is a core responsibility of an inference engine, answering four questions: how to allocate, how to reuse, what to do when full, and how to go across machines.\n\n**How it works**\n- **How to allocate**: the naive approach reserves contiguous memory per sequence based on `max_tokens`, causing lots of internal fragmentation and limiting concurrency. Modern engines allocate fixed-size blocks on demand, i.e. PagedAttention.\n- **How to reuse**: the naive approach prefills every request from scratch. Modern engines use prefix hashing (vLLM) or a radix tree (SGLang) so requests with the same prefix share blocks.\n- **What to do when full**: the naive approach simply OOMs. Modern engines queue, preempt (and recompute afterward), evict idle blocks by LRU, or offload KV to CPU memory or SSD.\n- **How to go across machines**: the naive approach can't. Modern engines transfer KV via P/D disaggregation or use distributed KV stores (e.g., Mooncake, LMCache).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThese four questions map to PagedAttention, prefix caching, scheduling and preemption, and tiered KV storage, covering nearly all the main modules of an inference engine. Each approach has a cost: preemption wastes compute on recomputation, offloading is limited by PCIe bandwidth, and cross-machine transfer depends on a high-speed network.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Using these four questions as a framework, you can lay out an inference engine's design as a table.\n- Distinguish \"request finished\" from \"cache invalidated\": a returned block keeps its contents until it is overwritten.\n- KV compression has two paths: the architecture layer (GQA / MLA) and the serving layer (quantization, hybrid layouts).","L3-T3A.2.3":"GPU memory estimation is a must-do calculation in system design interviews: VRAM ≈ weights + KV cache + activations and runtime overhead.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Weights** = parameter count × bits per weight ÷ 8. BF16 is 2 bytes per parameter (about 16 GB for an 8B model); FP8 / INT8 is 1 byte; 4-bit is about 4.5–5 bits per parameter including quantization metadata.\n- **KV cache** = 2 × L × n_kv × d_head × bytes × T × B.\n- **Activations and temporary tensors**: inference doesn't need to keep activations for backprop, so these are far smaller than in training. Mainly hidden states, logits (batch × vocab × 4 bytes, about 0.6 MB per sequence for a 150k vocabulary), and attention intermediates. Prefill with long sequences and large batches can produce transient peaks in the GiB range.\n- **Runtime overhead**: CUDA context, kernel workspace, CUDA graphs, fragmentation, typically 1–3 GiB.\n- **MoE**: all experts' weights must reside in memory, even though each token activates only a small subset.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nTake a 70B model: BF16 weights are 140 GB, which doesn't fit on a single 80 GB card, so you need 2–4 cards with TP, or quantize to FP8 (about 70 GB) or INT4 (about 37–40 GB), and then add the KV budget. Llama-3.1-70B has about 320 KB of KV per token, so 64 concurrent × 4k context ≈ 80 GB.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Calculate in the order \"weights → KV → overhead → round up to the number of cards\", then back out TPOT from bandwidth to check whether latency meets the target.\n- Validate estimates against the `GPU KV cache size: N tokens` line in vLLM's startup log.\n- For MoE, weights are counted by total parameters and bandwidth by activated parameters.","L3-T3A.2.4":"At startup, vLLM pre-allocates GPU memory all at once according to `--gpu-memory-utilization` (default 0.9), and after loading the weights it gives everything left to the KV cache block pool.\n\n**How it works**\n1. Determine the total usable memory from the utilization cap.\n2. Load the weights.\n3. Run a dummy forward pass to probe peak activation memory.\n4. After subtracting weights, peak activations, and runtime overhead, split all remaining memory into fixed-size KV blocks forming the block pool.\nSize of one block per layer = 2 × block_size (default 16) × n_kv_heads × head_size × bytes per element.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- Seeing vLLM use 90% of memory in `nvidia-smi` is normal; it doesn't mean the model is that big — the extra is all the KV pool.\n- The benefit of pre-allocation is that the runtime doesn't repeatedly request and free memory from CUDA, the engine fully controls block management, and concurrency capacity is deterministic. The cost is that it's hard to run other processes on the same card; co-locating multiple models requires manually lowering utilization or partitioning with MIG and the like.\n- Lowering `--max-model-len` doesn't save memory; it only caps how many blocks a single sequence can occupy.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain \"why vLLM eats 90% of memory as soon as it starts\".\n- Be able to explain where the KV cache token capacity in the startup log comes from.\n- For OOMs or frequent preemption, the tunable parameters are gpu-memory-utilization, max-num-seqs, and KV dtype.","L3-T3A.2.5":"Running the memory estimation formula on a few concrete examples yields four conclusions commonly used in interviews. The core point: once concurrency rises, the KV cache often takes more memory than the weights.\n\n**How it works**\nUsing Llama-3.1-8B (32 layers, 8 KV heads, d_head = 128) as the example:\n1. **KV can exceed the weights**: BF16 weights are about 15 GiB; with 32 concurrent sequences of 8k context each, KV is about 32 GiB, more than twice the weights. \"The model is 16 GB, so a 24 GB card is enough\" only holds for a single user with short context.\n2. **On small cards, quantizing weights is mainly about freeing room for KV**: on a 24 GiB card at 0.9 utilization with 8k context per sequence, BF16 weights allow only 3 concurrent sequences; with about 4.5-bit weights, 15.\n3. **On large cards, quantizing KV directly doubles concurrency**: an 80 GiB H100 supports 54 sequences with BF16 KV and 108 with FP8 KV. Here the marginal benefit of weight quantization shrinks.\n4. **Compute for the worst case, plan for p95**: the figures above assume every sequence fills its context. Real workloads vary in length and PagedAttention allocates on demand, so actual concurrency is usually higher. But capacity planning should use the p95 context length, not the average.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThese conclusions directly determine quantization strategy: with little memory, quantize weights first; with plenty of memory, quantize KV first.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to compute \"128 KiB per token, about 1 GiB per sequence at 8k context\" on the spot.\n- Explain why a 24 GB card running an 8B BF16 model has almost no concurrency.\n- Find the bottleneck first, then choose what to quantize.","L3-T3A.2.6":"`max-model-len` sets the maximum context allowed for a single sequence. It is a safety valve for capacity planning, not a switch for saving memory.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Capacity planning answers for the worst case**: if you allow 1M context, a single request may eat tens to hundreds of GB of KV, crowding out all other concurrent requests on the instance or triggering heavy preemption.\n- **Practice**: set `--max-model-len` according to business needs, say 32k. At the gateway, route very long requests separately (to dedicated long-context replicas), or rate-limit or reject them outright.\n- **Common misconception**: lowering `--max-model-len` in vLLM doesn't reduce memory usage. The KV pool's size is determined by gpu-memory-utilization; this parameter only caps how many blocks a single sequence can occupy.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe larger the context, the better a single request's experience, but the worse the system's worst case; long requests also block the head of the queue and raise others' TTFT. Tiered deployment (regular replicas plus dedicated long-context replicas) balances both, at the cost of more complex operations and possibly lower resource utilization.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When asked \"why not set the context to the maximum the model supports\", answer with worst-case KV usage and head-of-line blocking.\n- Clarify the relationship between max-model-len and memory usage.\n- Ways to handle very long requests: gateway routing, rate limiting, a dedicated long-context pool.","L3-T3A.3.1":"Batching has gone through three generations: static, dynamic, and continuous. Continuous batching uses one forward iteration as the scheduling unit and is standard in modern LLM engines.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Static batching**: collect a fixed number of requests, pad them to the same length, and run the whole batch until the longest finishes. Early requests wait for the batch to fill; after short requests finish, their slots sit idle.\n- **Dynamic batching**: set \"max batch + max wait time\" and launch when either condition is met first (how Triton and TorchServe handle traditional models). This fixes waiting to fill a batch but is still request-level: everything in a batch enters and leaves together.\n- **Continuous batching** (iteration-level scheduling, from Orca, OSDI 2022): after each iteration, finished sequences leave immediately and requests from the waiting queue fill in immediately. It works because autoregressive generation already proceeds step by step, so every step boundary is a natural scheduling point, and each sequence's KV is independent.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nSimulation results: on the same hardware with a 3 QPS load, continuous batching steadily handles all traffic (475 tok/s) with TTFT p50 around 40 ms; static batching's effective capacity is only about 260 tok/s, the system is already overloaded, and TTFT reaches tens of seconds to minutes. The gap comes from batch slots idling after short requests finish; the higher the variance of output lengths, the worse static does. Continuous batching's cost is implementation complexity, requiring per-sequence KV management. vLLM, TGI, SGLang, TensorRT-LLM, and llama.cpp server all use it.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain the difference in one sentence: the scheduling granularity is the batch, the batch plus a timeout, and the iteration, respectively.\n- Explain why continuous batching works.\n- The new problem it introduces is prefill and decode running together, which leads to chunked prefill.","L3-T3A.3.2":"Chunked prefill (from Sarathi-Serve) splits the prefill of a long prompt into small chunks and runs them in the same iteration as decode, solving the problem of a long prefill stalling every decode request.\n\n**How it works**\n- **The problem**: under continuous batching, an iteration contains both old requests in decode and new requests needing prefill. A 50k-token prefill may take hundreds of milliseconds or even seconds, during which all decodes stall, ITL spikes, and users see the streamed output \"hiccup\".\n- **The approach**: give each iteration a token budget. First allocate 1 token to every decode request, then fill the remaining budget with a chunk carved off a prefill. Long prompts complete over multiple iterations, and decode is never starved (stall-free).\n- **Effect**: the paper reports 2.6x higher serving capacity for Mistral-7B compared with vLLM at the time.\n- **vLLM parameters**: `--max-num-batched-tokens` (the per-iteration token budget, defaulting to 16384 since v0.28.0), `--max-num-seqs`, `long_prefill_token_threshold`.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- Smaller chunks give steadier ITL but higher TTFT for long prompts, because prefill is stretched out and each chunk must re-read the earlier KV.\n- Larger chunks give better TTFT but spiky ITL.\n- When no tuning can meet the SLOs, the next step is P/D disaggregation.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain the order of token-budget allocation: decode first, then carve off prefill.\n- Explain the trade-off of chunk size between TTFT and ITL.\n- The division of labor with P/D disaggregation: the former time-slices within one instance, the latter splits the two phases onto different instances.","L3-T3A.3.3":"Preemption is the safety valve of on-demand KV cache allocation. When a sequence needs a new block mid-decode but the pool is empty, the engine evicts some running request and restores it once space frees up.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Who gets evicted**: vLLM uses LIFO, evicting the most recently admitted request. It has had the least compute invested, so recomputation is cheapest, and this also respects first-come-first-served. The evicted request goes back to the front of the waiting queue and is restored first once space is freed.\n- **Recompute**: free all its blocks; on restore, treat \"original prompt + tokens generated so far\" as a new prompt and prefill it again. Prefill is compute-intensive and parallel, much faster than decoding token by token.\n- **Swap**: copy the KV to CPU memory and copy it back on restore, at a cost of 2 × KV bytes ÷ PCIe bandwidth. vLLM V1 defaults to RECOMPUTE, and KV swapping between GPU and CPU has been removed. CPU offload in V1 goes through the KV connector and is used to offload the prefix cache, unrelated to preemption.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nFrequent preemption causes thrashing, making throughput fall rather than rise. In a simulator, shrinking the KV pool to 2,000 blocks caused 594 preemptions, TTFT p99 rose from 0.71 s to 18 s, and throughput dropped from 2,287 to 1,395 tok/s. Tokens already streamed are not taken back; the user sees the output pause for a while and then resume.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why evict the latest arrival rather than the longest: the latest has the least invested; evicting the longest is the most expensive to recompute, and it is often close to finishing.\n- Choosing recompute vs swap: compare the compute time of prefill(L) with 2 × KV(L) ÷ PCIe bandwidth.\n- Remedies: lower max_num_seqs, use FP8 KV, limit max_model_len, add replicas.","L3-T3A.3.4":"Admission control decides \"whether to let a request in\", and fairness decides \"whom to serve first\". Together they prevent overload cascades and stop a single tenant from crowding out others.\n\n**How it works**\n- **The problem with FCFS**: the default first-come-first-served suffers head-of-line blocking — one very long request blocks all the short ones behind it. In theory SJF minimizes average waiting time, but output length isn't known in advance. One work (NeurIPS 2024) used learning to rank to predict relative request lengths and approximate SJF, cutting latency by 2.8x in chat scenarios; it needs aging to prevent starvation of long requests.\n- **Admission control**: the queue length must be capped. Under overload, return 429 quickly rather than letting requests queue for tens of seconds and then time out, which wastes GPU time and makes things worse as clients retry.\n- **Priority**: enable it in vLLM with `--scheduling-policy priority`; smaller values are processed first, and since v0.28.0 the priority can be read from an HTTP header. Give interactive requests high priority and offline batch jobs low priority.\n- **Tenant fairness**: rate-limit or weight scheduling per tenant. VTC (OSDI 2024) keeps a counter per tenant accumulating the token service it has received and always serves the tenant with the smallest count first. Metering by tokens is more reasonable than by request count (RPM).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nFair scheduling lowers throughput for large customers, so align SLAs with the business. When priority and preemption are combined, low-priority requests may be preempted repeatedly, causing lots of recomputation.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Under overload, failing fast beats queueing until timeout.\n- Fairness should be metered in tokens, with input and output weighted separately and output costing more.\n- Agent workloads should be scheduled per whole task, not queued per individual call.","L3-T3A.4.1":"PagedAttention is the core technique of vLLM (SOSP 2023). It brings operating-system virtual memory paging to the KV cache, solving the fragmentation and duplicate storage caused by reserving contiguous memory.\n\n**How it works**\n- **The problem**: a sequence's final length is unknown before generation. Early systems reserved contiguous memory per sequence for the maximum length, causing three kinds of waste: internal fragmentation, external fragmentation, and duplicate storage of identical prefixes. The paper measured that only 20%–38% of KV memory in systems of the time actually held token state.\n- **The solution**:\n  - Split KV into fixed-size blocks; by default each vLLM block holds 16 tokens.\n  - Each sequence maintains a block table mapping logical blocks to physical blocks, which need not be contiguous.\n  - Blocks are requested on demand as the sequence grows and returned when it ends.\n  - The attention kernel reads KV via indirect addressing through the block table.\n  - Multiple sequences can point to the same physical block, sharing prefixes via reference counting; continuing to write on a shared block triggers copy-on-write.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nInternal fragmentation is confined to the last block of each sequence, external fragmentation disappears, memory utilization approaches 100%, and at the same latency throughput is 2–4x higher than FasterTransformer, Orca, and others at the time. Note: PagedAttention itself doesn't speed up a single attention computation, and indirect addressing even adds slight overhead. It is an innovation in memory management, not in kernels.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain by analogy: blocks are pages, the block table is the page table, prefix sharing is shared pages plus copy-on-write.\n- Stress that it improves concurrency and throughput, not single-request speed.\n- Be able to explain what the 20%–38% figure means.","L3-T3A.4.2":"A minimal paged KV pool has three parts: fixed-size blocks, reference counts, and reuse based on prefix hashes. The hash chain guarantees that \"equal hashes mean the entire prefix is identical\".\n\n**How it works**\n- Each block's key = H(previous block's hash + this block's token ids), computed as a chain block by block from the start.\n- When a new request arrives, look up the hash table block by block from the start: on a hit, reference the existing physical block (reference count + 1); only on a miss allocate a new block and prefill.\n- **Only full blocks register hashes and can be shared**. A partially filled block's contents will still change, so its key is unstable. Hits are therefore block-granular: with a 10-token common prefix and block size 4, only 8 tokens hit.\n- In real systems the key must also include everything that affects the KV: LoRA adapter ID, hashes of multimodal inputs (e.g., images), and the cache salt.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe hash chain maps one-to-one onto the causal property that the KV cache \"can only reuse prefixes\", and lookups are O(1). The cost is coarse hit granularity (vLLM defaults to 16 tokens per block), so the tail that doesn't fill a block is always recomputed. Omitting the LoRA ID or image hash from the key causes wrong KV to be read. Hashes must be collision-resistant; otherwise one request may read another's KV, i.e. data crosstalk.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Write the hash-chain formula and explain why the previous block's hash is included.\n- Why only full blocks are cached.\n- Correctness pitfalls (LoRA, multimodal) and security issues (side channels, collisions).","L3-T3A.4.3":"In a paged KV pool, a request ending doesn't mean its cache is invalidated. Blocks returned to the free queue keep their contents and are truly invalidated only when overwritten by a new allocation. The order of the free queue is therefore the eviction policy.\n\n**How it works**\n- When a request ends, its blocks' reference counts drop to 0 and the blocks go back to the free queue. Their hash registrations are not cleared and their contents are untouched.\n- If a new request's prefix hits a block that is currently free, it is taken back out of the free queue (reference count back to 1) and reused directly.\n- Only when the block is handed out by `_alloc` for other content is the old hash registration deleted and the cache truly invalidated.\n- Freed blocks are placed where they will \"be reused last\", so the longest-unused ones are overwritten first — approximately LRU. SGLang instead does LRU eviction starting from the leaves of its radix tree.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThis deferred invalidation enables reuse across requests and turns at no extra storage cost: between two agent calls, as long as the blocks haven't been overwritten, the next round can hit. The cost is that under high concurrency, free blocks are reused quickly and the cache gets squeezed out. Between two agent calls there is tool execution (median 5.2 seconds, p99 up to 81 seconds), and by the time the agent returns the cache may have been evicted; you then need to offload KV to a cheaper storage tier such as CPU memory or SSD.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Distinguish \"released\" from \"invalidated\".\n- The eviction policy is determined by the order of the free queue.\n- Cache thrashing under high concurrency, and tiered KV storage as the remedy.","L3-T3A.4.4":"vLLM V1 is the rewrite of the core engine released in January 2025. Its two main changes: CPU work and the GPU loop are split into different processes, and each step forms a padding-free batch based on a token budget.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Two processes**: the API server process (FastAPI plus AsyncLLM) handles tokenization, applying templates, incremental detokenization, streaming, and tool-call and reasoning parsing; the EngineCore process runs step() in a loop. They communicate via ZMQ. In the V0 era this work shared one event loop with scheduling, and GIL contention often left the GPU waiting on the CPU.\n- **The four parts of step()**: the Scheduler moves requests from waiting to running, allocates the token budget, and decides how to chunk prefills; the KVCacheManager handles block allocation, prefix-hash lookup, and eviction; the Executor has GPU workers run one forward pass; finally, sampling updates each request's state.\n- **The scheduler prioritizes decode**: first give each request in the running queue 1 token, then use the remaining budget to pull new requests from the waiting queue for prefill. This is where chunked prefill is implemented.\n- **Flattened batches**: one iteration concatenates all sequences into one very long sequence, and the attention kernel finds each sequence's KV through its own block table, so differing lengths need no padding.\n- With multiple GPUs, the MultiProcExecutor launches one worker process per card.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe faster the GPU, the larger the share of total latency taken by CPU-side overhead. V1's split was the first step in this direction of optimization, and Model Runner V2 is the second.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Draw the three-layer structure: API server, EngineCore (Scheduler, KVCacheManager, Executor).\n- Explain why separate processes are needed: GIL contention.\n- Explain why no padding is needed: flattened sequences plus block-table addressing.","L3-T3A.4.5":"Model Runner V2 (MRV2) is a rewrite of vLLM's execution core announced in March 2026, aiming to eliminate CPU–GPU synchronization overhead within each step. It became the default executor for all models in v0.29.0 (2026-09-09).\n\n**How it works**\n- **Motivation**: GPUs keep getting faster, and small models and speculative decoding make each step's compute shorter, so the few milliseconds per step spent on the CPU preparing inputs, building block tables, synchronizing, and sampling became the dominant overhead.\n- **The approach**:\n  - Persist batch state instead of rebuilding it every step;\n  - Move input preparation onto the GPU with Triton kernels;\n  - Make the CPU and GPU fully asynchronous;\n  - Do sampling on the GPU as well.\n- **Effect**: officially reported 56% higher throughput in a small-model scenario (Qwen3-0.6B, GB200).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThis reflects a trend: the focus of inference optimization is shifting from \"kernels\" to \"eliminating CPU–GPU synchronization\". There's a matching troubleshooting lesson: if GPU utilization looks high but tok/s won't rise, first look at the gaps between kernels in Nsight — CPU-side scheduling, Python overhead, and sync points are often the real bottleneck. SGLang takes a similar direction (a zero-overhead CPU scheduler overlapped with GPU compute).\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain the four points of the MRV2 rewrite and its motivation.\n- Explain why small models benefit most: their per-step compute is short, so CPU overhead takes the largest share.\n- Group it with CUDA graphs and overlap scheduling under \"eliminating CPU overhead\".","L3-T3A.4.6":"FlashAttention is an IO-aware attention kernel. On the serving side, attention comes in two forms, prefill and decode, each needing a different kernel.\n\n**How it works**\n- **The essence of FlashAttention**: instead of writing the N×N attention matrix back to HBM, it loads tiles into SRAM and uses online softmax to normalize as it computes, turning attention from memory-bandwidth-bound into compute-bound. FA2 optimized parallelization and warp partitioning; FA3 (2024) pipelines for Hopper's asynchronous Tensor Cores (WGMMA, TMA) and supports FP8.\n- **Prefill attention**: the query is long and the work is matmul-heavy, so FlashAttention-style kernels fit best.\n- **Decode attention**: each sequence has only 1 query yet must read its entire paged KV, making it bandwidth-heavy. The PagedAttention kernel, FlashDecoding (split KV along its length, compute in parallel, then reduce), and FlashInfer (a customizable library of paged / sparse attention kernels used by both SGLang and vLLM) address exactly this.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nNew architectures such as MLA, sliding windows, and hybrid linear attention all need dedicated kernels. By 2026, vLLM's attention backends are a pluggable set (FlashAttention, FlashInfer, Triton, FlexAttention, etc.), selected automatically by model and hardware.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain what IO-aware means: reducing HBM reads and writes, not reducing FLOPs.\n- Explain why decode attention needs separate optimization: a single query but a very long KV to read.\n- Describe the idea of FlashDecoding: split along the KV length in parallel, then reduce.","L3-T3A.4.7":"CUDA graphs and torch.compile are both ways to cut the fixed per-step overhead of decode: the former eliminates kernel launch overhead, the latter does graph-level operator fusion.\n\n**How it works**\n- **CUDA graphs**: each decode step launches hundreds of small kernels, each with a few microseconds of launch overhead, which can be a sizable share of total time at small batch sizes. A CUDA graph records the kernel sequence of a forward pass as a graph and then submits the whole graph at once. The cost is that input shapes must be fixed, so engines pre-capture multiple graphs for a set of batch sizes and pad to the nearest size at runtime. This slows startup and uses more memory. vLLM V1 enables it by default; `--enforce-eager` turns it off for easier debugging.\n- **torch.compile**: vLLM V1 uses it for graph-level optimization, including operator fusion and removing redundant reshape / cast operations, and caches compiled artifacts on disk to shorten later startups.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nBoth trade startup time and memory for runtime latency. Cold-start-sensitive serverless scenarios must weigh whether to capture graphs and how many. A lesson for reading metrics: if GPU utilization is high but tok/s won't rise, first use Nsight to look at the gaps between kernels; CPU-side scheduling and sync points are often the real bottleneck.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why CUDA graphs need fixed shapes, and how engines handle variable batches (pre-capture a set of sizes plus padding).\n- How to debug problems: turn off graphs with `--enforce-eager` and compare behavior.\n- Summarize in one line: the bottleneck of small-batch decode is often the CPU and kernel launches, not the kernels themselves.","L3-T3A.5.1":"Server-side prefix caching lets requests that share the same prefix reuse already computed KV, skipping repeated prefill. The mainstream implementations use two index structures: vLLM's block-level hash table and SGLang's radix tree.\n\n**How it works**\n- **vLLM Automatic Prefix Caching**: each full block is registered in a hash table keyed by its prefix hash. A new request looks up the table block by block from the start; hit blocks are referenced directly, and only the missed portion is prefilled. Advantages: simple, O(1) lookups, and a natural fit with paged memory; the cost is that hit granularity is a whole 16-token block.\n- **SGLang RadixAttention**: organizes all cached token sequences into a radix tree, where each edge is a run of tokens and nodes point to the corresponding KV. A new request does longest-prefix matching along the tree, nodes split automatically at branch points, and eviction is LRU starting from the leaves. It naturally supports tree-shaped reuse patterns such as multi-turn chat, shared few-shot examples, self-consistency multi-sampling, tree-of-thought, and agent fork branches. The scheduler can also sort requests by \"match length against existing cache\" to raise the hit rate.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nBoth essentially do the same thing. The hash table is simple to implement and unified with block management; the radix tree is more natural for branching reuse. In 2026, SGLang extended the radix tree to hybrid architectures such as sliding windows, Mamba, and sparse attention, and added reference-aware caching for agent sessions.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to explain the difference between \"hashed block table vs prefix tree\" and where each fits.\n- Their eviction policies: vLLM uses free-queue LRU, SGLang uses LRU from the leaves.\n- Cache-aware scheduling can raise the hit rate.","L3-T3A.5.2":"Agent workloads depend especially heavily on prefix caching. Every step resends \"system prompt + tool definitions + full history\" and appends only a small piece at the end, so the vast majority of input tokens are a repeated prefix.\n\n**How it works**\nPublic workload analysis data:\n- The vLLM team analyzed 610 coding-agent trajectories: an input-to-output token ratio of about 131:1, a median context of about 80K at turn 30, and a theoretical cacheable share of 94.2%.\n- SemiAnalysis's AgentX benchmark (from real coding-agent trajectories): a median of 43 turns per session, median input of 142K tokens and median output of 444 tokens per turn, and a prefix cache hit rate above 96%.\nWithout prefix caching, every turn must re-prefill over a hundred thousand tokens; with it, each turn only prefills the few thousand newly added tokens.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThis is the fundamental reason the same hardware can differ in cost by more than an order of magnitude on agent workloads. It also shows that agent cost is dominated by input, and input cost is dominated by the cache hit rate. So context engineering should be designed around the cache: keep the prefix stable, append rather than modify, leave the tool list untouched. Once the cache is invalidated by truncation or routing problems, cost and TTFT explode together.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Remember the magnitudes 131:1 and 96%.\n- Explain why agents depend on caching more than ordinary chat: many turns, long context, short outputs.\n- When designing agent systems, actively ensure cache hits, e.g. session-affinity routing and avoiding sliding truncation.","L3-T3A.5.3":"Prefix caching is easy to break unintentionally. Its failure modes are a frequent interview question, and a good answer covers five layers: prompt design, context management, routing, eviction, and security.\n\n**How it works**\n1. **Volatile content in the prefix**: timestamps, request IDs, randomly ordered tool lists, few-shot examples that differ each time. If any token changes, the entire cache after it is invalidated.\n2. **Context truncation or compaction**: dropping the earliest messages in a sliding-window fashion changes the whole prefix. The LMCache paper notes from production observations that truncation can halve the hit rate. The right approach is to compact in chunks and as rarely as possible.\n3. **Load balancing scatters sessions**: each replica's cache is independent, and round-robin makes the hit rate drop linearly with the number of replicas. You need session affinity or cache-aware routing.\n4. **Eviction between calls**: tool execution has a median of 5.2 seconds and a p99 of 81 seconds, and by the time the agent returns the blocks may have been reused. The fix is offloading KV to a cheaper storage tier.\n5. **Multi-tenant side channels**: a shared cache makes TTFT an observable signal, so an attacker can use timing to probe whether someone else has sent a given prefix. Commercial APIs isolate caches per organization or workspace; self-hosted systems can use vLLM's cache salt to salt per request.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe first four affect cost and TTFT; the fifth affects security. In addition, omitting the LoRA ID or a hash of multimodal content from the hash key causes correctness problems, and hash collisions cause data crosstalk.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to list these five categories with a fix for each.\n- How to debug a low hit rate: diff the rendered output of two requests and find the first differing token.\n- For security, isolate tenants with a salt, and use a collision-resistant hash function.","L3-T3A.5.4":"Prompt caching in commercial APIs is \"server-side prefix caching + routing affinity + a set of billing rules\". Anthropic, OpenAI, Google, and DeepSeek differ in trigger method, minimum length, price multipliers, TTL, and isolation scope (per their documentation as of 2026-09).\n\n**How it works**\n- **Trigger method**: Anthropic is explicit — add `cache_control` to content blocks (up to 4 breakpoints), or enable automatic caching at the top level of the request. OpenAI uses implicit caching by default, with GPT-5.6 and later models also supporting explicit breakpoints. Gemini 2.5 and above enable implicit caching by default, plus explicit caching that requires creating a cache object first and then referencing it. DeepSeek is fully automatic.\n- **Minimum length**: 512 tokens for Anthropic's latest generation, 1,024 / 2,048 / 4,096 for other models; 1,024 for OpenAI; 2,048 for the Gemini 2.5 series and 4,096 for the 3.x series.\n- **Price**: Anthropic charges 1.25x for writes with a 5-minute TTL and 2x with a 1-hour TTL, and 0.1x for reads (lower on some models); OpenAI GPT-5.6+ charges 1.25x for writes and 0.1x for reads; Gemini's implicit caching has no write fee, while explicit caching is billed by storage duration; DeepSeek's hit price is about 1/30–1/50 of the miss price.\n- **TTL**: Anthropic is 5 minutes or 1 hour, renewed for free on every hit; OpenAI GPT-5.6+ is 30 minutes.\n- **Isolation**: Anthropic per workspace (per organization on some cloud platforms); OpenAI per organization, not across regions.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nImplicit caching requires no changes but offers no control; explicit caching gives precise control over breakpoints but carries a write premium, so you need to compute the break-even point.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain the \"implicit vs explicit\" trade-off.\n- Remember the magnitudes \"writes about 1.25x, reads about 0.1x\".\n- Confirm hits using the usage fields: `cache_read_input_tokens` for Anthropic, `cached_tokens` for OpenAI.","L3-T3A.5.5":"API caches match on the rendered prefix, and the layer order is fixed. Changing one layer invalidates the cache for that layer and every layer after it.\n\n**How it works**\nTaking Anthropic as an example, the prefix order is tools → system → messages:\n- **Changing tool definitions**: all three layers (tools, system, messages) are invalidated.\n- **Toggling web search / citations or switching fast mode**: tools survive; system and messages are invalidated.\n- **Changing `tool_choice` or adding/removing images**: tools and system survive; messages are invalidated.\n- **Changing the thinking config or effort**: messages are invalidated; whether the two layers above are also invalidated depends on the model.\nA few easy-to-miss points: the effort value is rendered into the prompt, so changing effort mid-conversation invalidates the message-level cache; fast mode and standard speed do not share a cache; and switching the structured-output schema (`output_config.format`) also invalidates that conversation's cache.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThis dictates how you organize a prompt: put the most stable content first (tool definitions, system prompt) and volatile content at the end, and avoid changing the tool list, effort, or schema mid-conversation. Adding and removing tools dynamically is flexible, but every change forces a rewrite of the cache for the whole prefix.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Know the layer order by heart, plus the rule \"change an upper layer and everything below it is gone.\"\n- Name a few subtle invalidation causes: effort, fast mode, schema.\n- State the design principles: stable content first, append-only, leave the tool list alone.","L3-T3A.5.6":"Prompt caching charges a premium on writes and gives a discount on reads. Whether it pays off comes down to a simple break-even inequality.\n\n**How it works**\n- Let the write multiplier be w and the read multiplier be r. If a prefix is written once and then read n times, the cost relative to no caching is (w + n·r) / (1 + n).\n- Break-even condition: n ≥ (w − 1) / (1 − r).\n- Anthropic 5-minute cache: (1.25 − 1) / (1 − 0.1) ≈ 0.28, so a single read is enough to break even.\n- Anthropic 1-hour cache: (2 − 1) / 0.9 ≈ 1.11, so it takes 2 reads.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe only question to answer is: will this prefix be used at least once more within the TTL? Conversely, don't put cache breakpoints on prefixes that differ every time; you would just be paying the 25% premium for nothing. When calls are spaced more than 5 minutes apart (for example, an agent waiting on tool execution or a user thinking), consider the 1-hour TTL, but it needs one extra read to break even. For agent workloads, the cache hit rate determines input cost and typically saves 70–90%.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Derive the break-even formula on the spot and plug in 1.25 / 0.1 to get \"one read to break even.\"\n- How to compute the hit rate: cache_read / (cache_read + cache_creation + input_tokens), bucketed by request type with alerts.\n- How to pick a TTL: compare the TTL with the interval between calls.","L3-T3A.5.7":"Caches live on specific machines. Actually getting hits requires routing affinity, the right warm-up order, and confirmation via the usage fields; otherwise it is easy to think you are cached when you are not.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Routing affinity**: OpenAI's docs say routing is based on a hash of the beginning of the prefix; once traffic for the same prefix exceeds roughly 15 requests/minute, it overflows to other machines and the hit rate drops. On older models, passing a stable `prompt_cache_key` (such as a user ID or session ID) helps route related requests together. The self-hosted equivalent is session affinity or cache-aware routing.\n- **Concurrency and warm-up**: a cache entry only becomes available after the first request finishes processing. If you fire 50 concurrent requests sharing a long prefix, they will most likely all miss and each write the cache separately. The right approach is to send one request to warm the cache, then fan out the rest. Anthropic supports pure warm-up with `max_tokens: 0` (write the cache, generate nothing).\n- **Hit verification**: prefixes below the minimum length are not cached, and no error is raised. Always log `cache_read_input_tokens` or `cached_tokens` before going live to confirm.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nAll of these are silent failures: no error, just a higher bill and longer TTFT. Warm-up adds one serial wait, in exchange for every subsequent concurrent request hitting the cache.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Answer \"why did 50 concurrent requests all miss\": the cache only takes effect after the first request finishes.\n- How to verify hits: check the usage fields and monitor hit rate per request type.\n- The self-hosted counterpart: prefix-aware routing plus offloaded KV.","L3-T3A.5.8":"A worked example shows that agent cost is dominated by input, and input cost is dominated by the cache hit rate.\n\n**How it works**\nSetup: one turn of a coding agent with 60k input tokens, of which 95% are identical to the previous turn and can hit the cache, 3% are a delta that must be newly written to cache, and 2% is an uncached tail; output is 600 tokens; prices are $2 input and $10 output (per million tokens); read multiplier 0.1, write multiplier 1.25.\n- Without caching: input 60k × $2/M = $0.12, output 600 × $10/M = $0.006, total $0.1260 per call, with input at 95%.\n- With caching: input cost = 60k × $2/M × (0.02 + 0.95 × 0.1 + 0.03 × 1.25) ≈ $0.018; adding output gives $0.0243 per call, a saving of about 81%.\n- Once caching is in place, output ($0.006) becomes a non-negligible share.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThis structural conclusion sets the priorities for agent cost optimization: first push up the hit rate (stable prefix, append-only, fixed tool list, session affinity), then think about cutting output or switching to a smaller model. If the cache hit rate drops from 95% to 50%, cost multiplies, an effect far larger than switching to a slightly cheaper model.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to redo this example on the spot and arrive at the roughly 81% saving.\n- Takeaway: agent cost is dominated by input, and input cost is dominated by the hit rate.\n- Give the optimization order: raise the hit rate first, then control output and model choice.","L3-T3A.6.1":"Quantization represents numbers with fewer bits. By what gets quantized, there are three categories: weight-only (W4A16, W8A16), weights plus activations (W8A8, W4A4), and KV cache quantization. Each gets its gains from a different place.\n\n**How it works**\n- Decode is bandwidth-bound; the lower bound on step time ≈ weight bytes / memory bandwidth. Example: Llama-3.1-70B in BF16 (140 GB) on an H100 (3.35 TB/s) takes about 42 ms per step, at most about 24 tok/s for a single request; in INT4 (about 37 GB) it drops to about 11 ms, at most about 90 tok/s.\n- **Weight-only (W4A16 / W8A16)**: reduces memory and bandwidth, but weights must be dequantized back to BF16 before compute, so there is no compute gain. Suited to small batches, memory-constrained setups, and on-device.\n- **Weights + activations (W8A8 INT8 / FP8, W4A4 NVFP4 / MXFP4)**: runs directly on low-precision Tensor Cores and increases compute. H100 FP8 throughput is 2× BF16, and Blackwell FP4 doubles it again. Suited to large-batch, throughput-first serving.\n- **KV quantization (KV8 / KV4)**: reduces KV memory and read volume; suited to long context and high concurrency.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThis is the trade-off most worth articulating in an interview: once the batch is large enough that compute is the bottleneck, weight-only quantization not only fails to speed things up but can slow them down due to dequantization overhead.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Start model selection by asking about the bottleneck: weight-only for small-batch, latency-sensitive workloads; W8A8 / W4A4 for throughput-sensitive ones; KV quantization when concurrency is KV-bound. The three can be stacked.\n- Use \"weight bytes ÷ bandwidth\" to explain why quantization lowers TPOT.\n- W8A8 must deal with activation outliers, e.g. with SmoothQuant.","L3-T3A.6.2":"By when quantization happens, there is PTQ (post-training quantization) and QAT (quantization-aware training). PTQ is the production mainstream; QAT gives better results but costs more.\n\n**How it works**\n- **PTQ**: take a trained model and use a small calibration set (a few hundred samples) to determine scale factors per tensor, channel, or group, without updating weights or with only light updates. It finishes in minutes to hours.\n- **QAT**: insert fake quantization (quantize then dequantize) into the forward pass during training or fine-tuning, and use a straight-through estimator so gradients bypass the rounding, letting the model learn to tolerate quantization noise. It works better below 4 bits or for reasoning models, but needs training compute and data.\n- **Basic formula for symmetric integer quantization**: s = max(|W|) / (2^(b−1) − 1); q = clamp(round(W / s), −2^(b−1), 2^(b−1) − 1); dequantize as W_hat = q × s.\n- **Granularity**: per-tensor → per-channel → per-group (group size 128 is common). Finer granularity means smaller error but more storage for scales. INT4 is usable largely because group-wise scales confine the impact of an outlier to 128 values.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe 2025–2026 trend is native low-precision training (FP8, NVFP4 training). Models ship with low-precision weights, such as DeepSeek-V3 in FP8 and gpt-oss in MXFP4, so the deployment side no longer needs to quantize them.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain the difference between PTQ and QAT and when to use each.\n- The calibration set must match the production distribution; calibrating on the wrong domain is the most common cause of failure.\n- Explain how group size affects accuracy and overhead.","L3-T3A.6.3":"The core difficulty in LLM quantization is outliers: activations in a few channels are tens of times larger than in the others. Naive RTN is fine at 8 bits but loses noticeable accuracy at 4 bits. GPTQ takes the error-compensation route.\n\n**How it works**\n- **RTN (round-to-nearest)**: round directly according to the scale, ignoring how each weight affects the output.\n- **GPTQ** (2022): quantizes weights column by column. After each column is quantized, it uses second-order information (the inverse Hessian, with H = 2XXᵀ computed from calibration data) to spread that column's quantization error across the not-yet-quantized columns as compensation. It is essentially an efficient approximation of OBS / OBQ and can quantize a 175B model within a few hours.\n- Quantized weights are stored as packed INT4 plus FP16 scale / zero, and dedicated kernels such as Marlin, ExLlama, and Machete dequantize them on load.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nGPTQ is one of the two major server-side INT4 formats (the other is AWQ). Its downside is the dependence on the calibration set: to compensate error it modifies other weights, so the quantized weights diverge more from the originals and occasionally perform poorly on out-of-distribution data. AWQ, by contrast, does no regression and generalizes more robustly.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Answer \"what is the essential difference between GPTQ and AWQ\": GPTQ quantizes column by column and uses second-order information to push error onto unquantized columns (modifying other weights); AWQ scales up salient channels based on activation statistics and then quantizes, with no regression.\n- Explain why RTN drops accuracy at 4 bits: outliers inflate the scale, leaving too little precision for ordinary values.\n- GPTQ's Hessian comes from the calibration data.","L3-T3A.6.4":"AWQ (2023) is about protecting important weights: find the small fraction of weight channels with the most influence on the output, scale them up, then quantize, reducing their relative error.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Observation**: about 1% of \"salient weights\" determine most of the quality, and they correspond to input channels with large activations.\n- **Approach**: rather than keeping these weights in mixed precision, multiply the corresponding channels by a scale factor s greater than 1: the weights grow and the corresponding activations shrink, which is mathematically equivalent. Quantizing after scaling reduces the relative error.\n- **Finding s**: use activation statistics s = mean(|X|)^α and grid-search α ∈ [0, 1], picking the α that minimizes output error after quantization.\n- **Implementation**: s is folded into the preceding layer (LayerNorm or the previous linear layer), so inference needs no extra multiplication.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nAWQ does no regression and leaves other weights untouched, so it depends less on the calibration set than GPTQ and generalizes more robustly. It was the most common format for open-source INT4 models in 2024–2025. On the tooling side, AutoAWQ has been archived and its functionality merged into LLM Compressor. Like all weight-only INT4, it speeds up small batches noticeably but not large ones.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- One sentence: scale up the ~1% salient channels by activation magnitude, then quantize.\n- Explain why an equivalent transform reduces error: after scaling, those weights have smaller relative error on the quantization grid.\n- Contrast with GPTQ: whether other weights are modified, and how well each generalizes.","L3-T3A.6.5":"SmoothQuant (2022) targets W8A8: it uses per-channel scaling to migrate part of the activations' quantization difficulty onto the weights, so both weights and activations can be represented in INT8 and actually use INT8 Tensor Cores.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Problem**: activations are hard to quantize because outliers consistently appear in certain channels; weights are comparatively easy. Applying per-tensor INT8 directly to activations lets outliers inflate the scale, leaving ordinary values with badly insufficient precision.\n- **Approach**: use the equivalent transform X·W = (X·diag(s)⁻¹)·(diag(s)·W). Divide activations by s per channel to flatten the outlier channels, and multiply the weights by s correspondingly. After smoothing, both sides can be quantized with per-tensor INT8.\n- s is determined jointly by the per-channel magnitudes of activations and weights, balancing quantization difficulty between the two.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nWeight-only quantization must dequantize back to BF16 before compute, so it doesn't speed up large batches. W8A8 makes matrix multiplies actually run on low-precision Tensor Cores, so throughput-first, large-batch workloads also speed up. The cost is that activations are quantized too, making the model more sensitive to outliers and dependent on good calibration. Doing W8A8 while ignoring activation outliers will break the model. SmoothQuant's idea applies equally to 4-bit activation quantization (e.g. NVFP4 W4A4).\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Write out the equivalent-transform formula and describe it as \"migrating difficulty from activations to weights.\"\n- Explain why W8A8 needs it and weight-only does not.\n- Contrast with AWQ: both use per-channel scaling, but AWQ does it to protect salient weights (weight-only), while SmoothQuant does it to make activations quantizable (W8A8).","L3-T3A.6.6":"FP8, NVFP4, and MXFP4 are the low-precision floating-point formats of the Hopper and Blackwell era. FP8 has become the default for serving, and NVFP4 and MXFP4 push the battleground down to 4 bits.\n\n**How it works**\n- **FP8**: comes in E4M3 (4 exponent bits, 3 mantissa bits; higher precision, used for weights and activations) and E5M2 (wider range, mostly used for gradients). Natively supported from Hopper (H100) onward and nearly lossless, it is the serving default from 2025 on, effectively the \"new BF16.\" On H100 and above there is almost no reason to serve BF16 weights anymore.\n- **NVFP4**: elements are E2M1, with every 16 sharing one FP8 (E4M3) scale, plus a tensor-level FP32 scale. The two-level scaling makes it noticeably more accurate than MXFP4. Natively supported on Blackwell, enabling W4A4 + KV4.\n- **MXFP4** (OCP MX spec): elements are also E2M1, with every 32 sharing one E8M0 (power-of-two) scale. Natively supported on Blackwell; on Hopper it can only be emulated with W4A16 kernels. gpt-oss ships its MoE weights in MXFP4.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nNVFP4 W4A4 on Blackwell captures all three gains at once: memory, bandwidth, and compute. In 2026 both SGLang and TensorRT-LLM treat it as a first-class citizen. The risks: 4-bit activations are the most outlier-sensitive, reasoning models and long-chain outputs tend to accumulate error, and kernel coverage for a given model architecture has to be verified case by case.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Distinguish NVFP4 from MXFP4: group size (16 vs 32), scale type (FP8 vs power of two), and NVFP4's extra tensor-level scale.\n- How to migrate from FP8 to NVFP4: quantize weights only first, then gradually add activations and KV; keep attention and lm_head at higher precision; set an accuracy-drop threshold relative to the FP8 baseline.\n- If a model ships in MXFP4, use MXFP4 as is; when quantizing yourself on Blackwell, prefer NVFP4.","L3-T3A.6.7":"There are many quantization formats and tools; which to use depends mainly on the deployment target: GGUF for on-device and CPU, GPTQ / AWQ weights for server-side INT4, and LLM Compressor or NVIDIA ModelOpt for a unified quantization pipeline.\n\n**How it works**\n- **GGUF** (llama.cpp): the de facto standard on-device, on CPU, and on Apple Silicon. A single file contains weights, metadata, and tokenizer. It has many quant types, e.g. `Q8_0`, `Q4_K_M` (k-quants, block-wise two-level scales), and i-quants such as `IQ2_XXS` (non-uniform quantization based on an importance matrix, imatrix), and supports per-layer mixed precision.\n- **bitsandbytes**: a one-line switch in Hugging Face, mainly for training and experiments.\n- **GPTQ / AWQ weights** (AutoGPTQ, GPTQModel, AutoAWQ): the two major server-side INT4 formats, loaded by vLLM / SGLang through kernels like Marlin and Machete.\n- **LLM Compressor + compressed-tensors**: the unified quantization pipeline under the vLLM project.\n- **NVIDIA ModelOpt**: the quantization tool of the TensorRT-LLM ecosystem.\n- **EXL3, MLX**: targeting single consumer GPUs and Apple Silicon respectively.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe format must match the inference engine and hardware: GGUF mainly serves llama.cpp / Ollama and is not optimal on vLLM; GPTQ / AWQ rely on dedicated kernels. The ecosystem is converging on a few unified pipelines (AutoAWQ was archived and merged into LLM Compressor), where a single recipe can produce multiple precisions.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Choose a format by deployment scenario: on-device, serving, experimentation.\n- Be able to explain `Q4_K_M`: k-quants, 4 bits, block-wise two-level scales.\n- Benefit of a unified pipeline: one toolchain covers weight, activation, and KV quantization.","L3-T3A.6.8":"bitsandbytes is the quantization library you can turn on with a single line in Hugging Face Transformers. It offers two schemes, LLM.int8() and NF4, and is aimed at training and experimentation rather than high-performance serving.\n\n**How it works**\n- **LLM.int8()**: splits outlier channels in the activations out and computes them in FP16, computes the rest in INT8, then merges the two results. This handles outliers while saving memory.\n- **NF4** (4-bit NormalFloat): assumes weights are roughly normally distributed and places 16 quantization levels at normal-distribution quantiles, fitting the weight distribution better than uniform INT4. Combined with double quantization (quantizing the scales themselves to save more memory), it is the foundation of QLoRA: the base model is frozen in NF4 and only BF16 LoRA adapters are trained, making it possible to fine-tune large models on a single GPU.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIts strengths are ease of use and seamless integration with PEFT / QLoRA, which makes it ideal for fine-tuning under limited memory and for quick experiments. The cost is that its inference kernels are slower than dedicated formats like Marlin, so it is not suited to high-throughput production serving. Production deployments usually switch to AWQ / GPTQ / FP8 weights loaded by engines like vLLM. After QLoRA training, the adapter is typically merged back into a BF16 base and then re-quantized for serving.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain how LLM.int8() handles outliers: outlier channels go through FP16.\n- NF4 and double quantization are components of QLoRA.\n- Distinguish quantization for training from quantization for serving, and why bitsandbytes is not used directly for production serving.","L3-T3A.6.9":"LLM Compressor (with the compressed-tensors format) and NVIDIA ModelOpt are the two mainstream unified quantization pipelines, serving the vLLM and TensorRT-LLM ecosystems respectively.\n\n**How it works**\n- **LLM Compressor + compressed-tensors**: part of the vLLM project, a unified quantization pipeline for vLLM. One recipe covers W8A8 FP8 / INT8, W4A16, NVFP4, MXFP4, plus KV cache and attention quantization. Version 0.9.0 (2026-01) added attention quantization and MXFP4 support. The compressed-tensors checkpoints it produces load directly in vLLM. After AutoAWQ was archived, its functionality was merged in here as well. It can also generate calibrated scales for FP8 KV.\n- **NVIDIA ModelOpt**: the quantization tool for TensorRT-LLM, supporting FP8, NVFP4, and INT4 AWQ; its HF checkpoints can be used by both TRT-LLM and vLLM.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe value of a unified pipeline is that one set of calibration data and one process can produce multiple precisions, which makes A/B evaluation easy (e.g. FP8 vs NVFP4) and guarantees compatibility with engine kernels. The choice depends on the target engine: prefer LLM Compressor for vLLM, and ModelOpt for TensorRT-LLM or when squeezing maximum NVFP4 performance out of Blackwell. Either way, the calibration set should resemble production data, and quantized results must be signed off on downstream tasks.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- State each tool's positioning and which ecosystem it belongs to.\n- Describe a migration: run PTQ with the tool, gradually widen the quantization scope, and finally sign off on your own eval set.\n- Calibrated scales for FP8 KV can be generated by LLM Compressor.","L3-T3A.6.10":"EXL3 and MLX quantization are formats for specific hardware: the former pursues extreme compression on a single consumer NVIDIA GPU, the latter serves Apple Silicon.\n\n**How it works**\n- **EXL3** (ExLlamaV3): a new format based on QTIP (trellis quantization) that emphasizes preserving quality at very low bit widths of 2–4 bits, with the goal of fitting large models onto a single consumer GPU. The community migrated from EXL2 to EXL3 in 2025 (check the official sources for details). Trellis quantization is a vector / codebook-style quantization that no longer rounds each weight independently, so its error at low bit widths is smaller than element-wise quantization.\n- **MLX quantization**: Apple's MLX framework uses `mlx_lm.convert -q` to produce 4/8-bit affine-quantized weights with adjustable group size, running on unified memory.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nBoth belong to on-device or single-GPU scenarios. These are batch=1 and bandwidth-bound, so token speed is roughly proportional to bandwidth ÷ model bytes, and the compression ratio translates directly into speed and the largest model you can run. The cost is a relatively closed ecosystem: EXL3 mainly works with ExLlama-family runtimes, and MLX only runs on Apple hardware; neither suits high-concurrency data-center serving. On a Mac, MLX and GGUF (llama.cpp) are the two main routes.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why on-device and single-GPU setups favor very low bit widths: small batches, bandwidth bottleneck.\n- Apple Silicon can run large models but not quickly: unified memory is large, but bandwidth is limited.\n- Be able to choose by mapping hardware to format.","L3-T3A.6.11":"KV cache quantization directly compresses the main bottleneck on concurrency. FP8 KV doubles capacity and is nearly lossless. Low-bit schemes like TurboQuant have impressive paper results, but in engineering measurements they lose accuracy and throughput.\n\n**How it works**\n- **FP8 KV**: in vLLM, `--kv-cache-dtype fp8` (E4M3 or E5M2) doubles capacity with almost no quality impact; the attention kernel dequantizes on load. The most robust approach is calibrated scales, which can be generated with LLM Compressor.\n- **4-bit KV**: KIVI (2024) found that K's outliers concentrate in fixed channels, so K should be quantized per channel and V per token.\n- **TurboQuant** (Google Research, ICLR 2026): first applies a random orthogonal rotation to make vector distributions isotropic (PolarQuant), then applies a 1-bit QJL correction to the residual. The paper reports lossless quality at about 3.5 bits per channel and about 6× overall compression.\n- **vLLM's measurements (2026-05-11)**: the k8v4 and 4-bit variants were close to baseline on AIME25 and on 128k–256k long-context retrieval; the 3-bit variant dropped about 20 points on AIME25 and about 30% (relative) on long-context retrieval. All TurboQuant variants delivered only about 66–80% of BF16 throughput, while FP8 KV had no throughput loss.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe conclusion is that FP8 is still the best default for KV quantization. Use 4 bits only under extreme memory pressure, and only after validation. The gap between paper claims and engineering measurements shows that any quantization scheme must be measured on your own workload for both accuracy and throughput.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain why rotation helps: it spreads energy evenly across dimensions and shrinks the maximum (QuaRot and SpinQuant follow the same idea).\n- KV quantization and MLA are compatible: DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash stores KV in MXFP8 in vLLM.\n- FP8 KV and BF16 KV prefix caches are not interchangeable; the cache key must include the precision.","L3-T3A.6.12":"Whether a quantization scheme can ship must be decided on downstream tasks, not perplexity alone. The industry has also accumulated a set of rules of thumb for accuracy loss that you can cite directly.\n\n**How it works**\n- **How to evaluate**: perplexity is only a smoke alarm; it is very insensitive to quantization. You must run downstream tasks, especially those sensitive to error accumulation such as long outputs, reasoning, and tool calls. Typical failure: PPL rises only 1%, but GSM8K drops 5 points, the JSON validity rate falls, and long reasoning chains start repeating after token 3000.\n- **Rules of thumb**:\n  1. FP8 W8A8 is nearly lossless; turn it on by default.\n  2. INT4 W4A16 (AWQ / GPTQ, g128) typically loses 0.5–2 points on 7B–70B models; larger models tolerate quantization better, and small models ≤3B lose noticeably.\n  3. Reasoning models and long chain outputs are more fragile than ordinary chat models.\n  4. MoE expert weights quantize well; attention and shared layers should stay at higher precision.\n  5. Ask where the bottleneck is first, then choose the quantization method.\n- **Failure modes**: calibration set doesn't match production distribution; quantizing lm_head or embeddings; calibrating a chat model with base-model data; doing W8A8 while ignoring activation outliers; shipping after validating on PPL alone.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nPost-quantization evaluation should reuse your regular eval sets with an accuracy-drop threshold (e.g. no more than 1 point below baseline), followed by load testing to confirm the throughput and unit-cost gains actually materialize.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"How much does INT4 lose?\": 0.5–2 points, and explain the factors (model size, task type).\n- Why PPL is not enough.\n- Be able to list three common causes of quantization failures.","L3-T3A.7.1":"Speculative decoding has a cheap draft guess γ tokens, which the target model then verifies in a single forward pass. It uses the compute left idle during decode to reduce latency.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Motivation**: decode is bandwidth-bound, and at small batch sizes GPU compute sits largely idle. Verifying 1 token and verifying γ tokens cost the target model almost the same, because both are limited by reading the weights.\n- **Flow**: the draft model autoregressively guesses γ tokens; the target model runs one forward pass over those γ positions plus one extra position to get the distribution at each; it then verifies position by position, accepts a prefix, and discards all drafts after the rejection point.\n- Each step produces at least 1 token and at most γ + 1.\n- Sources: Leviathan et al. 2023, Chen et al. 2023.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIt does not change the output distribution (guaranteed by speculative sampling), so quality loss is zero; the only cost is extra compute. That makes it best for interactive, small-batch, TPOT-sensitive scenarios. At large, throughput-first batch sizes compute is no longer idle, and the gain disappears or even turns negative. Drafts can come from a separate small model, n-gram lookup, EAGLE heads, or the model's built-in MTP.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- One sentence: trade idle compute for latency, because verifying γ tokens costs about the same as generating 1.\n- Each step yields 1 to γ+1 tokens.\n- When not to enable it: large batches, low acceptance rate, overly expensive drafts.","L3-T3A.7.2":"Speculative sampling is the verification rule for speculative decoding. It mathematically guarantees that the final output distribution is identical to sampling token by token from the target model, which is why speculative decoding loses no quality.\n\n**How it works**\nLet the draft distribution be q and the target distribution p. For each draft token x:\n- accept x with probability min(1, p(x) / q(x));\n- if rejected, resample a token from the corrected distribution norm(max(0, p − q)) and discard all subsequent drafts;\n- if all γ are accepted, sample 1 extra token from the target model's distribution p at the last position.\n\nIntuition: when p(x) ≥ q(x), the draft \"under-guessed\" and x is always accepted; when p(x) < q(x), the draft \"over-guessed\" and x is accepted proportionally. After a rejection, you sample from the probability mass where p exceeds q. Together, the two parts are exactly sampling from p.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThis property makes speculative decoding a lossless speedup, fundamentally different from methods like quantization or distillation that change model behavior. It requires verification against the full probability distribution; sampling parameters such as temperature and top-p must be applied consistently to p and q. When combined with constrained decoding, draft tokens must also go through the grammar mask, or the acceptance rate collapses.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Write out the acceptance probability and the residual distribution.\n- Answer \"why is the output distribution unchanged\": the accepted part plus the residual resampling sum to exactly p.\n- Why discard later drafts after a rejection: they were generated conditioned on the rejected token.","L3-T3A.7.3":"How much speculative decoding speeds things up depends on just two quantities: the acceptance rate α of draft tokens, and the cost c of the draft relative to the target model. Every later method is about improving these two numbers.\n\n**How it works**\n- Assuming each draft token is accepted independently with probability α, the expected yield per step is E[tokens/step] = (1 − α^(γ+1)) / (1 − α).\n- Example: with α = 0.8 and γ = 4, about 3.36 tokens per step.\n- Speedup ≈ E[tokens/step] / (1 + γ·c), where c is the cost of one draft step relative to one target step.\n- In practice acceptance falls with position: vLLM's 2026 evaluation shows the first draft position is typically accepted 85–95% of the time, with a significant drop at each subsequent position. So γ beyond 7 brings almost no extra benefit.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nA larger γ means more potential output, but also more draft cost and more wasted verification compute. When α is low, a large γ is pure waste. An overly expensive draft also cancels the gains; for example, drafting for a 13B model with a 7B model is not worth it. EAGLE-style draft heads cost roughly one layer of the target model, which is the fundamental reason they win.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to derive or recall the expected-yield formula and plug in numbers.\n- Production practice: measure α first, then set γ, usually 3–7.\n- Monitor per-position acceptance rates and the actual tokens produced per step.","L3-T3A.7.4":"Drafting for speculative decoding has grown into a whole family of methods: from zero-cost n-gram lookup, to trained EAGLE-3 heads, to MTP shipped with the model, to the parallel drafting that emerged in 2026.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Separate small model**: draft with a small model sharing the vocabulary (e.g. 1B drafting for 70B); the tokenizer must be identical, and the draft itself takes memory and compute.\n- **n-gram / prompt lookup**: find a matching n-gram in the context and use what follows it as the draft. Zero cost, with high acceptance on \"copy-heavy\" tasks such as RAG, code editing, and summarization; try it first.\n- **SuffixDecoding**: caches prompts and past outputs in suffix trees (one tree per request plus a global tree) and adaptively decides how far to guess. Up to 5.3× speedup on agent tasks such as SWE-Bench; suited to code editing, agent loops, and RL rollouts.\n- **Medusa**: adds multiple prediction heads to the target model; superseded by EAGLE.\n- **EAGLE-3**: a lightweight feature-level autoregressive head that takes hidden states from early, middle, and late layers of the target model and builds a draft tree, so one verification covers multiple paths. The paper reports 3–6.5× speedup; it is the mainstream choice for serving.\n- **MTP**: multi-token prediction modules trained into the model itself (DeepSeek-V3 family, Qwen3-Next, GLM, etc.), using almost no extra memory.\n- **Parallel drafting** (P-EAGLE, DFlash, DSpark): produces all draft tokens in a single forward pass, cutting draft cost. P-EAGLE is up to 1.69× faster than EAGLE-3 at concurrency 1, narrowing to 1.05–1.25× at concurrency 64.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nSelection rule: if the model ships with MTP, use MTP; otherwise use EAGLE-3; for copy-heavy workloads, try n-gram or SuffixDecoding first.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why EAGLE beats a separate draft model: it uses the target model's hidden states for higher acceptance, costs only about one layer, and adds tree drafting.\n- Why n-gram works well for RAG: the output copies heavily from the context.\n- Parallel drafting addresses the serial overhead on the draft side.","L3-T3A.7.5":"Speculative decoding does not always speed things up. With large batches, low acceptance rates, or overly expensive drafts, it actually makes the system slower, so it needs to be turned on and off adaptively based on load.\n\n**How it works**\n1. **Large batches**: speculative decoding assumes the target model has idle compute during verification. Once continuous batching has filled the batch close to the compute bottleneck, verifying γ+1 positions really does cost γ times more compute, and every rejected draft is waste. Rule of thumb: leave it off for throughput-first offline workloads with batches in the dozens or more; turn it on for interactive, small-batch workloads.\n2. **Low acceptance rate**: when the draft and target distributions diverge (a domain shift, high temperature, creative writing), α falls below 0.5 and the gain disappears.\n3. **Overly expensive drafts**: drafting for a 13B model with a 7B model is not worth it.\n4. **Interaction with other features**: structured-output masks must apply to both drafting and verification; maturity varies across engines when combined with prefix caching and chunked prefill.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nProduction load is dynamic, and leaving speculative decoding permanently on can tank throughput at peak. The scheduler should decide whether to draft, and how many tokens, based on the current batch size and historical acceptance rate. vLLM v0.29.0 added adaptive verification extensions and supports returning acceptance statistics per request. Acceptance rates are available from vLLM's `/metrics` and in OpenAI's responses.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- The three slowdown cases and the roofline reasoning behind them.\n- Metrics to monitor: per-position acceptance rate, tokens produced per step, actual TPOT and throughput.\n- Rollout process: measure α on real traffic first, then set γ, and finally load-test to confirm neither TPOT nor throughput regresses.","L3-T3A.8.1":"Constrained decoding sets the logits of tokens that violate the grammar to −∞ before each sampling step. The JSON or tool-call arguments the model emits therefore cannot be malformed by construction.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Why it's needed**: every agent tool call requires valid, schema-conforming JSON. Relying on the prompt alone (\"output only JSON\") gives a validity rate of 95–99%. For a system with a million calls a day, a 1% failure rate means ten thousand retries or crashes.\n- **Pipeline**: JSON schema → regex or CFG (context-free grammar) → automaton. At each step, the automaton's current state gives the set of allowed next characters, which is mapped onto the vocabulary to get the set of allowed tokens (the mask); then logits + mask → softmax → sample.\n- **The hard part**: there is a mismatch between the character-level grammar and the token-level vocabulary. A single token can span several grammar states, and with a vocabulary of over a hundred thousand tokens, computing which ones are legal online at every step is too slow.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIt turns validity from \"probably right\" into \"guaranteed right,\" but syntactic validity is not semantic correctness. Constraints also change the sampling distribution and can force the model down paths it would not otherwise take. Wrapping the entire response in JSON degrades reasoning quality; for tool calls, best practice is a structural tag or strict tool parameters. Also, if mask generation runs serially on the CPU, it slows down the whole batch.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Mechanism: grammar → automaton → token mask → set illegal token logits to −∞.\n- Quality impact and mitigations: order schema fields to match the model's natural output, put a reasoning field first, and leave the thinking part of reasoning models unconstrained.\n- When combined with speculative decoding, draft tokens must also pass through the mask.","L3-T3A.8.2":"Outlines, llguidance, and XGrammar are three generations of constrained decoding. They differ in how they make \"computing the legal-token mask at each step\" fast.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Outlines** (2023): compiles a regex into an FSM, precomputes the allowed-token list for each FSM state offline, and does an O(1) table lookup at runtime. Pros: simple, with near-zero runtime overhead. Cons: slow compilation, where complex schemas can take seconds to tens of seconds; and JSON nesting and recursion need a CFG, which a pure FSM cannot express.\n- **llguidance** (Microsoft, written in Rust): handles CFGs with an Earley parser plus a lexer layer, computes masks on demand at runtime, and speeds this up with a token trie. It supports full CFGs (Lark grammar) without long compilation, at roughly tens of microseconds per step; the cost is implementation complexity.\n- **XGrammar** (CMU / MLC): handles CFGs with a pushdown automaton (PDA). It splits tokens into two classes: context-independent ones, which can be precomputed and make up the vast majority, and context-dependent ones, which are checked at runtime. Mask generation overlaps with GPU compute, so per-step overhead is close to zero. It is the default backend for vLLM, SGLang, TensorRT-LLM, and MLC. XGrammar-2 (2026-01) added dynamic grammars and faster compilation for agent scenarios.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nvLLM supports two backends, xgrammar and guidance, with `auto` selection by default, and five kinds of constraints: JSON schema, regex, EBNF, choice, and structural tag. A structural tag lets constrained segments be interleaved with free text, for example enforcing the schema only inside `<tool_call>` tags, which matches the shape of agent tool calls. The first use of a given schema incurs compilation latency, so warm it up and cache it.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Core differences: offline FSM lookup, on-demand Earley parsing, PDA plus token classification.\n- Why an FSM isn't enough: nesting and recursion need a CFG.\n- What a structural tag is for: constrain only the tool-call portion and leave the rest free.","L3-T3A.8.3":"A structural tag is a \"local constraint\" form of constrained decoding: it lets the model interleave constrained segments with free text, for example enforcing a JSON schema only inside `<tool_call>` tags while generating freely outside them. It resolves the tension between wanting tool arguments to be 100% valid and not wanting to lock the whole response into JSON.\n\n**How it works**\nThe underlying machinery is the same: JSON schema → regex / CFG → automaton, computing the allowed token set at each step and setting the other logits to −∞. A structural tag adds a trigger on top: no mask is applied during free generation, but once the model emits the opening tag (e.g. `<tool_call>`), decoding switches to the grammar for the corresponding schema, and it returns to free mode on the closing tag. vLLM supports five kinds of constraints (JSON schema, regex, EBNF, choice, and structural tag), passed via `extra_body={\"structured_outputs\": {...}}`.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThis is exactly how agent tool calls actually look: the model first reasons freely and states its intent, then emits a structured call. Compared with turning the whole response into JSON, it preserves the model's room to think and avoids over-constraining that hurts reasoning quality. The costs are that the engine must support it and the tag format must match the chat template the model was trained with.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Best practice for tool calls is a structural tag or strict tool parameters, not JSON-ifying everything.\n- Explain how it differs from a plain `response_format: json_schema`: the former constrains only segments, the latter the entire output.\n- A relevant experiment: compare validity and argument accuracy across \"unconstrained + retry,\" \"JSON schema,\" and \"structural tag.\"","L3-T3A.8.4":"API-side structured outputs are how commercial models expose constrained decoding as a request parameter: the developer passes a JSON schema and the server guarantees the output strictly conforms. The application layer no longer needs \"retry on parse failure\" fallback logic.\n\n**How it works**\nMain interfaces: OpenAI's `response_format: {type: \"json_schema\", strict: true}` and `strict: true` for function calling; Anthropic's `output_config.format` (JSON schema) and strict tool use; Gemini's `response_schema`. The server compiles the schema into a grammar, caches it, and generates token masks step by step during decoding.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- **First-use latency**: a schema must be compiled the first time it is used, so that request has higher latency; later requests hit the cache.\n- **Cache invalidation**: changing the schema invalidates the prompt cache, so keep schemas stable and don't change fields dynamically every turn.\n- **Subset limits**: each provider supports a different subset of JSON schema, e.g. requiring `additionalProperties: false`, not supporting some `format` keywords, or capping recursion depth; validate against the docs before launch.\n- Syntactic validity is not semantic correctness; argument meanings still need validation.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"Are commercial API structured outputs the same as self-hosted constrained decoding?\" → Essentially yes; the server compiles and caches the grammar.\n- Name three engineering details: first-compile latency, schema changes breaking the prompt cache, and restricted schema subsets.","L3-T3A.8.5":"Constrained decoding turns output validity from \"probably right\" into \"impossible to get wrong by construction,\" but it isn't free: it affects quality and performance, doesn't guarantee semantics, and interacts with optimizations like speculative decoding.\n\n**How it works**\nAt each step the logits of illegal tokens are set to −∞, and the masked-out probability mass is redistributed to legal tokens, which changes the model's original sampling distribution. Three generations of implementations: Outlines compiles a regex into an FSM and precomputes each state's allowed-token table offline (table lookup at runtime, but slow compilation and no recursion); llguidance computes masks on demand at runtime with an Earley parser; XGrammar uses a pushdown automaton and splits tokens into context-independent (precomputable, the vast majority) and context-dependent classes, overlapping mask generation with GPU compute, and is the default backend for vLLM / SGLang / TensorRT-LLM.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- **Quality**: if the schema requires a `\"reason\"` field first but the model tends to state its conclusion first, it gets forced into low-probability paths. Mitigations: order fields to match natural output, put a free-text `reasoning` field first, and for reasoning models constrain only the final answer (vLLM's `enable_in_reasoning` leaves the thinking part unconstrained by default).\n- **Performance**: if masks are computed serially on the CPU, one slow schema drags down the whole batch; first-time compilation requires warming the cache.\n- **Semantics**: syntactically valid ≠ correct content; argument semantics still rely on prompting, validation, and retries.\n- **Speculative decoding**: draft tokens must also pass through the mask, or the acceptance rate collapses.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to explain the implementation challenge of the mismatch between character-level grammar and token-level vocabulary.\n- List the four kinds of trade-offs (quality, performance, semantics, speculative decoding) and the mitigation for each.","L3-T3A.8.6":"Over-constraining means forcing the model's entire response into a JSON schema, which makes it \"think less\" and degrades reasoning quality. It is the most overlooked failure mode of constrained decoding: the output is valid, but the answer is worse.\n\n**How it works**\nConstrained decoding changes the sampling distribution, and the wider the constraint's scope, the more generation steps get distorted. When the whole response must be JSON, the model loses room to reason in natural language, or its reasoning gets squeezed into a field whose order conflicts with its habits. The right approach is to narrow the scope of constraints:\n- for tool calls, use a structural tag (constraining only the inside of `<tool_call>`) or strict tool parameters (constraining only the argument object);\n- when the whole output must be structured, put a free-text `reasoning` field at the very start of the schema;\n- for reasoning models, constrain only the final answer, not the thinking part.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIn agent systems, tool-call validity and reasoning quality matter equally. Full JSON-ification is fine for simple extraction tasks but clearly hurts quality in decision-making scenarios that require multi-step reasoning. The rule: constrain what must be machine-readable, leave free what requires thinking.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"Why not make the whole response JSON?\" → Over-constraining lowers reasoning quality; constrain only the tool-call segments.\n- Propose validating it experimentally: compare argument accuracy under full JSON vs a structural tag, and the effect of reversing field order.","L3-T3A.8.7":"Multi-LoRA serving lets one base model serve hundreds of LoRA adapters at once: all requests share a single copy of the base weights, and each request specifies its adapter as needed. It solves the deployment problem of \"100 customers each with their own fine-tune, and you can't run 100 instances.\"\n\n**How it works**\nFor each adapted linear layer: `y = W x + (B A) x · (α / r)`. `W x` is the same for every request in the batch and can still be done as one big matmul; `B A x` is tiny (rank r is usually 8–64), and each request uses its own A and B. Merging an adapter into the weights would require a full copy per adapter and defeat the purpose of sharing, so the server does **not merge**.\n- **Punica**: introduced the SGMV (Segmented Gather Matrix-Vector multiplication) kernel, which segments requests in a batch by adapter and computes each segment's `B A x` in a single kernel, avoiding a per-request loop.\n- **S-LoRA**: puts adapter weights in a unified paged memory pool shared with the KV cache, swapping them in and out from CPU on demand; it can serve thousands of adapters at once and schedules requests grouped by adapter to reduce swaps.\n- **vLLM**: `--enable-lora --max-loras --max-lora-rank --max-cpu-loras`; the request's `model` field names the adapter, and `/v1/load_lora_adapter` supports dynamic loading.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nOne base plus a number of adapters of a few tens of MB each suits multi-tenant customization, A/B experiments, and per-task routing; a single high-traffic model is better merged and deployed on its own.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why not merge: after merging, each adapter needs a full copy of the weights.\n- Punica solves compute (SGMV); S-LoRA solves memory management (unified paged pool + swapping).","L3-T3A.8.8":"Sharing an instance across many LoRAs saves money, but it costs performance and isolation, and has two often-overlooked correctness pitfalls.\n\n**How it works**\nComparing \"multi-LoRA shared instance\" with \"each adapter merged and deployed separately\":\n- **Cost**: the former has one base and tiny adapters (an 8B model at r=16 is a few tens of MB); the latter needs a full copy of the weights and a set of GPUs per adapter.\n- **Latency**: the former adds one small matmul per layer, and the overhead grows with the number of adapters in the batch, typically 10–30%; the latter has no extra overhead.\n- **Isolation / canarying**: weak for the former, since batches are shared and one tenant's traffic affects others; strong for the latter.\n- **Hot updates**: the former loads a new adapter in seconds; the latter requires a redeploy.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nCorrectness pitfalls:\n1. The base an adapter was trained on must exactly match the serving base, including the tokenizer and chat template; serving QLoRA outputs on a quantized base requires the engine to support that combination (or merge into BF16 and then re-quantize).\n2. **The prefix cache cannot be shared across adapters**: the same prefix produces different KV under different adapters, so the cache key must include the adapter ID, or data leaks across adapters and outputs are wrong.\nWith many adapters, use `max_loras` to cap how many stay resident on the GPU and keep the rest on CPU, with hot ones resident and cold ones evicted by LRU. When a single adapter has heavy traffic or needs strong isolation and its own SLO, deploy it separately.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- The extra overhead is typically 10–30%, in exchange for one base serving hundreds of customizations.\n- Including the adapter ID in the prefix cache key is the detail that earns the most credit.","L3-T3A.9.1":"Inference-side multi-GPU parallelism comes in five kinds: TP, PP, DP, EP, and CP. Unlike training, inference has no backward pass or optimizer state, so the choice is driven by latency, memory, and interconnect bandwidth.\n\n**How it works**\n- **TP (tensor parallelism)**: splits each linear layer by column / row across GPUs, with 2 all-reduces per Transformer block and communication on every decode step. Weight reads are spread across N GPUs, lowering per-request latency; it only pays off within NVLink (a single 8-GPU node) and gets dragged down by the network across machines.\n- **PP (pipeline parallelism)**: splits by layer and only passes inter-layer activations, so communication is small; used across machines when a model doesn't fit on one, but it doesn't help latency and needs multiple micro-batches to fill the pipeline.\n- **DP (data parallelism)**: replicates the whole model with no communication, the simplest way to add throughput; each replica has its own KV cache, so a routing layer is needed.\n- **EP (expert parallelism)**: distributes MoE experts across GPUs, with 2 all-to-alls per layer (dispatch / combine); large MoEs (DeepSeek-V3 class) commonly use attention DP + expert EP with DeepEP, and the main risk is expert load imbalance.\n- **CP / DCP**: shards the KV of a single very long sequence across GPUs, for contexts of hundreds of thousands of tokens that don't fit on one GPU.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nA classic judgment call: serve a 70B model with TP=2 on two H100s, or on one H200 (141 GB, 4.8 TB/s)? TP=2 gives lower per-request latency but adds all-reduce overhead and the cost of two GPUs; the H200 avoids communication, is simpler to operate, and has similar throughput. Choose TP for small-batch, latency-sensitive workloads; choose fewer GPUs with larger memory plus DP for throughput- or cost-sensitive ones.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- TP only within an NVLink domain; PP spans machines but doesn't reduce latency.\n- NVL72 lets EP for large MoEs avoid crossing InfiniBand.\n- At small batch sizes TP is dominated by link latency, with diminishing returns as the degree grows.","L3-T3A.9.2":"P/D disaggregation (prefill / decode disaggregation) runs prefill and decode on different instances (even different machines or GPU models), solving the interference between the two phases when colocated and the fact that each phase has a different optimal configuration.\n\n**How it works**\nWhen colocated, a long prefill entering the batch causes ITL spikes for every decode request; chunked prefill only mitigates this. The two phases also behave differently: prefill is compute-intensive and suits small TP and large chunks; decode is bandwidth-intensive and suits large batches and more aggressive quantization. After disaggregation, the prefill instance computes the KV and sends it over RDMA / NVLink / NIXL to the decode instance, which continues generation.\n- **DistServe** and **Splitwise** (2024) proposed the idea; **Mooncake** (Kimi's serving architecture) was the first published production-grade implementation. It is KV-cache-centric, building a distributed KV pool from CPU memory and SSD that nodes access via RDMA.\n- vLLM supports transport backends such as NIXL, LMCache, Mooncake, and FlexKV through its **KV connector** abstraction; NVIDIA's **NIXL** is a unified library for inference KV transfer, running over UCX (RDMA) or TCP. TensorRT-LLM has a KV Cache Connector API since 1.1, and SGLang has its own P/D implementation.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIt fits large models (≥70B or large MoE), long inputs, strict ITL SLOs, and clusters with NVLink / InfiniBand; small models, short inputs, single-node or PCIe setups should stick with chunked prefill. The P/D ratio is set by the input-to-output ratio and can be adjusted dynamically by an orchestration layer such as Dynamo's Planner.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Two motivations: eliminate interference, and optimize each phase's configuration separately.\n- In multi-turn conversations the prefix KV sits on the decode side, so you need bidirectional transfer or shared KV storage, or the prefix cache hit rate drops to zero.","L3-T3A.9.3":"P/D disaggregation is not a free upgrade: it introduces the hard cost of KV transfer and significant operational complexity, and its gains show up mainly in tail latency rather than averages. Knowing when not to disaggregate is where this topic best shows engineering judgment.\n\n**How it works**\n1. **KV transfer is a hard cost**: the KV for a 10k-token Llama-3.1-70B request is about 3 GB, which takes tens of milliseconds over NVLink / RDMA and may exceed 1 second over PCIe. vLLM's 2026-09 measurements: on PCIe-connected GPUs, the roughly 470 MB of KV for an 8k prompt took about 1.3 s to reach the decode side, giving a median TTFT of 2.2 s after disaggregation versus only 0.7 s colocated.\n2. **Operational complexity**: one service becomes three or four (prefill, decode, router / proxy, KV transfer layer), and you must handle new failures such as prefill succeeding but decode failing, KV leases expiring (vLLM's `kv_lease_duration` defaults to 30 s), and whether to error out or recompute after a transfer failure (`kv_load_failure_policy`).\n3. **Gains are in tail latency**: on 2× L40S (Qwen2.5-7B, 8k prompt), colocated p99 ITL jumps from 23 ms to 169 ms at just 0.4 req/s, while disaggregated stays at 25–52 ms across loads; on MI300X goodput is about 2.4×.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe conclusion: **don't disaggregate without a high-speed interconnect**. Compared with chunked prefill, which chunks and colocates within a single instance at zero infrastructure cost at the price of longer TTFT for long prompts, disaggregation is worth it only when colocation can't meet the SLO however you tune it, or when the two phases' optimal configurations differ greatly.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Cite \"TTFT of 2.2 s vs 0.7 s over PCIe\" to show disaggregation can be slower.\n- List the new failure modes: lease expiry, transfer failure, sending multi-turn KV back.","L3-T3A.9.4":"Cache-aware routing is the routing strategy for multi-replica deployments that tries to send each request to a replica that already has its prefix cached. With DP replicas, each replica's prefix cache is independent, and round-robin makes the hit rate fall linearly with the number of replicas.\n\n**How it works**\nThree strategies:\n1. **Session affinity (sticky sessions)**: consistent-hash by session / user ID to a fixed replica. Simple, but load can be uneven, and when scaling changes the hash ring you get widespread misses.\n2. **Prefix-aware routing**: the router hashes request prefixes in blocks, records which replica recently received them, and sends new requests to the replica with the longest prefix match and acceptable load. SGLang Router, vLLM production-stack, the llm-d scheduler, and Dynamo Router all take this approach.\n3. **KV-event-driven**: the engine publishes events when blocks are created / evicted, and the router maintains an exact view; the cost is event traffic and consistency.\n\nThe scoring function is usually \"expected hit tokens × weight − queue length × weight,\" optionally adding predicted TTFT. llm-d's endpoint picker goes \"filter → score → select,\" with scoring terms including queue depth, KV utilization, prefix hits, in-flight tokens, and LoRA affinity; Dynamo's cost is \"prefix-hit weight × number of prefill blocks to compute + estimated decode load.\"\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nLLM request costs vary by orders of magnitude, so you can't round-robin by request count. Looking only at the cache overwhelms hot replicas; looking only at load throws away the cache. Multiple router instances may also stampede toward the same idle replica, which can be countered with power of two choices or softmax sampling over cost to add randomness; when overloaded, use admission control to return 429 quickly.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- The three strategies and the cost of each.\n- Multi-turn agent sessions: session affinity first, falling back to the next-best replica by prefix similarity under overload or failure.","L3-T3A.9.5":"KV cache tiering and offloading store KV in tiers like a storage system, so the prefix cache in GPU memory isn't evicted constantly. Under high concurrency, GPU-resident cache gets pushed out within minutes or even seconds, while agent calls are separated by tool execution time (median about 5 seconds, p99 over 80 seconds).\n\n**How it works**\nTiers: GPU HBM (tens of GB, TB/s) → CPU DRAM (hundreds of GB to TB, tens of GB/s) → local NVMe (TB scale) → remote distributed storage (Mooncake Store, object storage).\n- **LMCache**: the KV caching layer of the vLLM ecosystem, supporting CPU / disk / remote backends, non-prefix reuse (CacheBlend), P/D transfer, and cross-instance sharing.\n- **Mooncake Store**: Kimi's open-source distributed KV store, accessed via RDMA.\n- The retrieval path pivots on host memory: on a GPU miss → check host memory → then the file system, object storage, or peer nodes in order; hit chunks are asynchronously promoted to host memory and then copied H2D into newly allocated GPU blocks, during which the request is suspended without blocking others.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nWorth doing when inputs are much longer than outputs, conversations are multi-turn, calls are spaced far apart (agents), multiple replicas need to share a cache, and prefill dominates cost. Costs: CPU memory and PCIe bandwidth become new bottlenecks; storage consistency and eviction policy; almost no benefit for short-input, single-turn workloads. Fetching KV from CPU is typically several to tens of times faster than re-running prefill, but it depends on prompt length and interconnect; for short prompts recomputing may be cheaper.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- The cache key must include the model version, LoRA ID, and KV precision, falling back to recompute on validation failure.\n- When SSD retrieval is slow: prefetch during tool calls, sink only cold prefixes, and pipeline loading by chunk.","L3-T3A.9.6":"CacheBlend is a non-prefix KV reuse technique. In RAG, the same document chunk often appears at different positions in different requests; ordinary prefix caching can only reuse what is identical from the very beginning and can't exploit this repetition. CacheBlend makes these chunks' KV reusable too.\n\n**How it works**\nThe difficulty is that KV depends on more than the tokens themselves: positional encodings differ, and tokens within a chunk should attend to the content before it (cross-chunk attention), which separately precomputed KV lacks. CacheBlend directly reuses each chunk's precomputed KV, then selects a small number of the most affected tokens to recompute, correcting the deviation from cross-chunk attention and approximating a full prefill with very little recomputation. It is provided by LMCache; the LMCache paper groups KV reuse into three forms: prefix reuse, non-prefix reuse (CacheBlend), and P/D transfer.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nRAG inputs are typically assembled as \"system prompt + several retrieved chunks + question,\" with a different combination and order of chunks each time, so prefix cache hit rates are low even though the chunks themselves repeat heavily. CacheBlend can significantly cut prefill cost and TTFT for such workloads. The cost is that the result is approximate (only some tokens are recomputed), so quality must be validated on your own tasks, and the implementation depends on an external cache layer integrated with the engine.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Difference from prefix caching: the latter requires an exact match from the start; CacheBlend allows chunks at any position.\n- One of the alternatives for long-context RAG, alongside retrieval compression and hierarchical summarization.","L3-T3A.9.7":"These are two built-in implementations of KV tiering: vLLM's native CPU offloading and NVIDIA Dynamo's KVBM (KV Block Manager). The former lets vLLM put evicted KV in host memory without external components; the latter is Dynamo's built-in tiered KV manager.\n\n**How it works**\n- **vLLM native CPU offloading**: the V1 engine added tiered offloading in 2025–2026. Blocks evicted from GPU memory are not discarded but offloaded to host memory and copied back to the GPU on a later hit. Since v0.28.0 tiered KV offloading also supports disk; vLLM's post on tiered offloading says that with more than 128 concurrent sessions, storage hits more than double throughput.\n- **NVIDIA Dynamo KVBM**: Dynamo's built-in tiered KV manager. However, the Dynamo v1.5.0 (2026-09-21) notes mark KVBM for removal in v1.6.0, with its KV index moving to Mooncake shared-cache events and a disk tier.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nKVBM's exit shows the KV storage ecosystem converging on a few storage layers (such as LMCache and Mooncake), with engines and orchestration layers no longer each reinventing the wheel. When choosing: to extend cache lifetime on a single node and instance, vLLM's native offloading is the least effort; bring in LMCache or Mooncake Store when you need cross-instance sharing or remote storage. The cost is still that PCIe bandwidth and host memory become new bottlenecks.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain the difference between \"native offloading needs no external components\" and \"external caches support cross-instance sharing.\"\n- Keep up with the ecosystem: the planned removal of KVBM reflects the convergence of KV storage layers.","L3-T3A.9.8":"The orchestration layer handles how to schedule across dozens or hundreds of machines; a single engine handles how to run fast on one GPU or one machine. The orchestration layer manages routing, autoscaling, the P/D ratio, and KV sharing.\n\n**How it works**\nSeveral solutions took shape in 2025–2026:\n- **NVIDIA Dynamo**: a data-center-scale distributed inference framework with a KV-aware router, a Planner that adjusts the P/D ratio to load, and NIXL transfer, supporting vLLM / SGLang / TensorRT-LLM backends.\n- **llm-d**: a Kubernetes-native stack backed by Red Hat, IBM, Google, CoreWeave, NVIDIA, and others, using vLLM as the engine and the Gateway API Inference Extension in place of a plain Service for AI-aware routing (prefix-aware, predicted-latency scheduling), with support for P/D disaggregation and KV events; it entered the CNCF Sandbox in 2026-03.\n- **Gateway API Inference Extension (GAIE)**: a Kubernetes SIG standard for inference routing that defines CRDs such as InferencePool and an Endpoint Picker (EPP) plugin framework, letting the gateway choose endpoints by model, priority, and KV state.\n- There are also vLLM production-stack, AIBrix, and KServe / Ray Serve.\nLarge MoE deployments also involve wide-EP: spreading experts across dozens or hundreds of GPUs and using EPLB to replicate hot experts and bin-pack for balanced load.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nEngines solve single-instance efficiency and the orchestration layer solves cluster-level problems; the two connect through an OpenAI-compatible API and KV connectors. Without an orchestration layer, Nginx round-robin plus session affinity can work, but cache hit rates under agent workloads will look bad.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Division of labor in one sentence: the engine owns the single instance; the orchestration layer owns routing, autoscaling, the P/D ratio, and KV sharing.\n- Be able to contrast the positioning of Dynamo, llm-d, and GAIE.","L3-T3A.9.9":"NVIDIA Dynamo is NVIDIA's data-center-scale distributed inference framework, positioned as the successor to Triton. It bundles KV-aware routing, P/D disaggregation, KV transfer, and dynamic autoscaling into one package.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Rust core**, with vLLM, SGLang, or TensorRT-LLM as backends.\n- **KV-aware router**: pluggable worker-selection policies and session affinity; the cost function is \"prefix-hit weight × number of prefill blocks to compute + estimated decode load.\" Setting the weight to 0 reduces it to pure load balancing, and a temperature parameter enables softmax sampling to avoid herding.\n- **Planner**: dynamically adjusts the ratio of prefill to decode instances based on load, and is part of the autoscaling logic.\n- **NIXL**: a unified library for inference KV transfer, running over UCX (RDMA) or TCP.\n- Timeline: announced at GTC 2025; 1.0 declared production-ready on 2026-03-16 (GTC 2026); the latest as of 2026-09-21 is v1.5.0, which added pluggable worker-selection policies and plans to remove KVBM in 1.6.0.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIt suits teams already in the NVIDIA ecosystem that need large-scale P/D disaggregation and KV-aware routing; by comparison, llm-d is more Kubernetes-native and vLLM-centric. The trade-off is many components and complex operations, which small deployments may not need.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Remember the trio: KV-aware router, Planner (dynamic P/D ratio), NIXL transfer.\n- When describing \"the division of labor between engine and orchestration layer\" in an interview, Dynamo is the representative example of the orchestration layer.","L3-T3A.9.10":"vLLM production-stack and AIBrix are both reference solutions / control planes for deploying LLM inference on Kubernetes, filling the gap between \"a single vLLM process\" and \"a production-grade cluster.\"\n\n**How it works**\n- **vLLM production-stack** (vLLM community): the reference deployment of vLLM on K8s, made up of a Helm chart + router (session / prefix-aware) + LMCache + observability. Its KEDA example scales on `vllm:num_requests_waiting` and supports scale-to-zero.\n- **AIBrix** (ByteDance): an LLM inference control plane on K8s, offering multi-LoRA management, distributed KV, heterogeneous GPU scheduling, and autoscaling.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThey address similar problems with different emphases: production-stack is the \"officially recommended out-of-the-box combo\" for vLLM, focused on prefix-aware routing and offloaded KV caching; AIBrix is more of a platform control plane, emphasizing multi-tenant and resource-scheduling capabilities such as multi-LoRA and heterogeneous GPUs. Compared with Dynamo and llm-d, they are smaller in scope and easier to adopt, suiting teams that have already chosen vLLM and want to stand up an observable, autoscaling cluster quickly; for very large-scale P/D disaggregation and cross-engine support, look at Dynamo or llm-d.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Name production-stack's components: Helm + router + LMCache + observability.\n- AIBrix's selling points: multi-LoRA management and heterogeneous GPU scheduling.\n- Use waiting-queue length, not GPU utilization, as the autoscaling signal.","L3-T3A.10.1":"Autoscaling signals determine when an inference cluster adds or removes replicas. The core conclusion: GPU utilization from `nvidia-smi` is **the wrong signal**; use the metrics exposed by the engine and the queue instead.\n\n**How it works**\nGPU utilization is defined as the fraction of the sampling period during which a kernel is running. Decode is bandwidth-bound and kernels run continuously, so it is almost always near 100% regardless of whether the system is overloaded. The right signals:\n- `num_requests_waiting` (waiting-queue length): the most direct overload signal; if it stays > 0, scale up;\n- KV cache usage: above 90%, preemption starts and throughput drops;\n- TTFT / ITL p95: what users feel, used as SLO targets, but they lag, so they suit verification rather than triggering;\n- `num_requests_running`: compare against the knee of the throughput–latency curve from load tests;\n- request arrival rate: a leading signal, usable for predictive scaling on daily / weekly cycles.\nIn practice: Prometheus scrapes vLLM's `/metrics` → KEDA / HPA custom metrics → scale replicas.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nUsing the wrong signal means either scaling up forever or not scaling up under overload. GKE also warns against using memory usage for scale-down decisions, because vLLM pre-allocates all GPU memory. Estimating replica count: load-test to find the per-replica concurrency knee C* within SLO, use Little's law to get required concurrency = arrival rate × mean E2E latency, and replicas = concurrency / (C* × target utilization of 0.6–0.7).\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"Why is GPU utilization a poor autoscaling signal?\" is a frequently asked question.\n- Trigger scale-up when waiting > 0 persists for 30–60 s, and leave headroom for cold starts.","L3-T3A.10.2":"The real difficulty in autoscaling isn't when to scale up but how slow scaling up is and how safe scaling down is: new replicas take minutes to start serving, and careless scale-down truncates streaming responses.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Slow scale-up**: a new replica goes through scheduling, image pull, weight download, loading, compilation, and warm-up, taking minutes from scheduling to serving. Two ways to cope: scale ahead (predictive scaling on traffic cycles, keeping headroom), or accept queuing during overload, combined with admission control and degradation (rate-limit with 429, shorten max_tokens, switch to a smaller model, disable speculative decoding or reasoning mode).\n- **Scale-down draining**: first remove the replica from routing so it receives no new requests; wait for all in-flight requests to finish before killing the process, or responses mid-stream will be cut off.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nLLM requests can last tens of seconds or even minutes, so the traditional web-service approach of killing processes immediately directly hurts user experience. Predictive scaling and headroom mean paying for idle capacity, while purely reactive scaling means SLO violations at peak; weigh this by how latency-sensitive the business is. The admission-control principle: better to return 429 quickly than let a request queue for tens of seconds and then time out.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Degradation under overload: rate limiting, shorter outputs, a smaller model, disabling speculative decoding.\n- Scale-down must remove traffic first, then drain.\n- Cold-start time directly determines how much headroom you need.","L3-T3A.10.3":"Cold start is the time from a new inference replica being scheduled to it serving requests. It determines scale-up speed and serverless first-request latency.\n\n**How it works**\nCold-start breakdown for a 70B model replica (orders of magnitude; varies by environment):\n- Image pull (10–20 GB): 30 s to a few minutes, negligible once the image is cached;\n- Weight download (70B FP8 is about 70 GB): 1–5 minutes from object storage, under 1 minute with a local NVMe cache;\n- Loading onto GPU: safetensors mmap + multithreaded copy, tens of seconds;\n- Initialization / compilation: torch.compile, CUDA graph capture, memory profiling, 30 s to a few minutes;\n- Warm-up: the first requests trigger kernel autotuning, grammar compilation, and so on.\nMitigations:\n1. Keep weights close: node-local NVMe, in-cluster object storage, streaming loaders (e.g. Run:ai Model Streamer);\n2. Cache compilation artifacts and shrink the set of batch sizes captured as CUDA graphs;\n3. vLLM **sleep mode**: level 1 offloads weights to CPU while keeping the process; level 2 discards weights but keeps the CUDA context; waking takes only seconds;\n4. Weight-cache daemons: vLLM v0.30.0's Fast Start maps weights already in GPU memory directly via CUDA IPC, and SGLang v0.5.20 also has a weight-cache daemon;\n5. Snapshot / restore: Modal's memory snapshots, which it says cut cold start for some models from nearly 2 minutes to about 10 seconds.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nCold-start time determines how much headroom scale-up needs, and whether serverless is viable for online serving.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Break it down into image, weights, loading, compilation, and warm-up, then give a mitigation for each.\n- For large-scale scale-up, distribute weights via P2P / tree-based forwarding.","L3-T3A.10.4":"Large-scale weight distribution is a system design question of the form \"distribute 500 GB of weights to 1,000 GPU workers\": first state the time lower bound, then design a scheme that approaches it.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Lower bound**: each node must receive at least the data it needs, so time ≥ per-node data ÷ per-node ingress bandwidth, independent of the number of nodes. At 100 Gbps ingress (12.5 GB/s), 500 GB takes about 40 s; at 400 Gbps, about 10 s.\n- **Single-source fan-out is infeasible**: the source would have to send 500 GB × 1,000 = 500 TB, which takes about 11 hours from a 100 Gbps source.\n- **Design**: split the weights into fixed-size chunks (e.g. 64–256 MB) with hashes; pull from multiple object-storage replicas in parallel; between nodes, use BitTorrent-style P2P or tree / ring forwarding, forwarding while receiving in a pipeline, so aggregate bandwidth grows with node count and total time approaches \"lower bound + startup overhead tied to tree depth\"; be rack-aware and prefer same-rack peers to avoid ToR oversubscription; persist to a local NVMe cache; overlap download with loading onto the GPU.\n- **TP sharding**: with TP=8, each rank pulls only its own shard (about 62.5 GB), cutting the lower bound by another order of magnitude.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThis question tests the ability to quantify first and design second. In practice you also have to handle slow nodes, source failures, re-fetching on checksum failure, and atomic version switching; the bottleneck may be source egress, switch oversubscription ratio, local disk writes, or deserialization, so measure the throughput of each stage first.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- State your bandwidth assumptions first, then give the lower bound.\n- When scaling out, new nodes should pull from existing nodes rather than going back to the source.","L3-T3A.10.5":"Serverless GPU (Modal, RunPod Serverless, Baseten, on-demand endpoints from Fireworks / Together, etc.) bills per second, scales to zero when idle, and leaves autoscaling and cold-start optimization to the platform. It addresses the waste of renting whole GPUs that sit idle most of the time under low or bursty traffic.\n\n**How it works**\nDecision logic: **serverless costs 20–50% more per unit, but total cost is lower when utilization is low**. A third-party pricing page from 2026-09 shows RunPod Secure Cloud's H100 SXM at about $3.49/h versus about $4.79/h for its Serverless H100; Modal's H100 is about $3.95/h (check the official pricing pages).\n- Steady traffic with utilization > 50–60%: renting whole GPUs or reserved instances yourself is cheaper;\n- Sparse, bursty traffic, internal tools, no traffic overnight: serverless is more cost-effective;\n- Hybrid setups are the norm: reserved instances for the baseline, with peaks overflowing to serverless or APIs.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nCold start is the core constraint of serverless; the first request may wait 1–5 minutes. Latency-sensitive services need a minimum number of reserved instances (min replicas ≥ 1) or keep-warm, at which point it is **no longer pay-per-use**, which should be stated clearly in design reviews. Platforms mitigate cold starts with techniques such as memory snapshots.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"When is serverless GPU worth it?\" → When utilization is below about 50% and traffic is bursty.\n- Losing the pay-per-use advantage once keep-warm is on is a common follow-up.","L3-T3A.10.6":"Kubernetes natively only understands CPU and memory; GPUs need an extra stack to be schedulable. Understanding this layer is the prerequisite for answering any K8s GPU scheduling question.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Extended resources**: GPUs are exposed as `nvidia.com/gpu` through the NVIDIA device plugin. Extended resources **can only be requested in whole numbers, cannot be overcommitted, and cannot be shared between containers**. A Pod requests a whole GPU with `resources.limits: {nvidia.com/gpu: 1}` (if requests is also set, they must be equal), and uses taints / tolerations and nodeSelector to pin work to GPU nodes.\n- The **GPU Operator** manages node GPU software as a unit: driver, Container Toolkit, device plugin, GPU Feature Discovery (GFD, which labels nodes with model, memory, MIG, etc.), DCGM and DCGM Exporter (monitoring), MIG Manager, validators, and in newer versions a DRA driver; it depends on Node Feature Discovery.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n\"Whole numbers, no overcommit, no sharing\" leads to four kinds of engineering problems: small low-traffic models monopolizing a whole GPU is wasteful (→ GPU partitioning / sharing); multi-GPU, multi-node jobs can only start when they get every GPU (→ gang scheduling); cross-node inference must land in the same NVLink domain (→ topology awareness, LWS); and `nvidia-smi` utilization doesn't tell you how busy the GPU really is (→ DCGM profiling metrics).\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Open a 30-second answer with: \"In K8s, a GPU is an integer extended resource exposed by the device plugin; it can't be overcommitted or shared.\"\n- Be able to list the components the GPU Operator installs, especially what GFD and DCGM Exporter are for.","L3-T3A.10.7":"Time-slicing, MPS, and MIG are three ways to split one GPU across multiple workloads; they differ in degree of isolation and flexibility.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Time-slicing**: the device plugin advertises one GPU as N (`replicas`), and CUDA rotates time slices between processes. No memory isolation (every process can use all the memory) and no fault isolation (one crash affects everyone). Requesting 2 slices doesn't get you more compute, and the official advice is to enable `failRequestsGreaterThanOne`. Suited to development, notebooks, and bursty low traffic.\n- **MPS**: kernels from multiple processes truly run concurrently (spatial sharing), with per-client limits on memory and thread share; fault isolation is weak, since a fatal error in one client affects all clients on the GPU; at most 60 client contexts per GPU, and it cannot be combined with MIG (in the NVIDIA plugin). Suited to several small inference services.\n- **MIG**: hardware partitioning where each instance has its own SMs, L2, memory controllers, and memory bandwidth, giving the best isolation. A100 / H100 / H200 / B200 / GB200 support up to 7 instances; the H100 80GB profiles include 1g.10gb, 2g.20gb, 3g.40gb, 7g.80gb, etc.; repartitioning requires no running jobs on the GPU.\nIn the device plugin, time-slicing and MPS are mutually exclusive and apply to all GPUs on the node; MIG has two exposure strategies, `single` and `mixed`.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nFor production multi-tenancy with QoS, choose MIG, but profiles are fixed and smaller slices shrink KV space and bandwidth proportionally; choose time-slicing for development; MPS sits in between.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- First ask whether the models share a base: if so, use multi-LoRA instead of partitioning the GPU.\n- Worked example: split an H100 into three 2g.20gb slices for 7B models (about 15 GB in BF16), and each has only a few GB left for KV, suitable only for short contexts and low concurrency.","L3-T3A.10.8":"Running vLLM on shared GPUs (time-slicing, MPS, MIG) has a few specific pitfalls, all rooted in vLLM pre-allocating most of the GPU memory at startup.\n\n**How it works**\nvLLM grabs most of the GPU memory up front for the KV cache according to `gpu_memory_utilization` (default 0.9).\n- **Time-slicing / MPS**: there is no hard memory isolation, so several vLLM processes started with the default will crowd each other out; you must manually lower each process's fraction so they sum to no more than 1. For example, to put three 7B models on one H100, set `gpu_memory_utilization≈0.28` for each. KV space is larger than with MIG, but one process OOMing or crashing takes the others down, and they compete for compute at peak.\n- **MIG**: has a hard limit, but the smaller the slice, the less room for the KV cache, the smaller the batch, and the higher the unit cost.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nAsk first: are these models multiple fine-tunes of the same base? If so, serving several LoRAs from one vLLM process is usually better: the KV cache is shared across adapters, and throughput and cost beat any partitioning scheme. When traffic is very sparse, time-sharing via sleep mode or serverless is also cheaper than partitioning. And for monitoring, vLLM's pre-allocation means memory usage can't be used as a scale-down signal.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Remember the 0.9 default and that the fractions must sum to no more than 1 when sharing.\n- \"Should several fine-tunes of the same base get partitioned GPUs?\" → No, use multi-LoRA.","L3-T3A.10.9":"DRA (Dynamic Resource Allocation) is Kubernetes' next-generation device allocation API. It replaces the device plugin's coarse \"integer count\" model, letting devices such as GPUs be selected by attribute, shared, and partitioned dynamically. The core went GA and on by default in Kubernetes v1.34 (2025-08-27).\n\n**How it works**\nDevices are described by a few kinds of objects: DeviceClass, ResourceClaim / ResourceClaimTemplate, and ResourceSlice.\n- Use **CEL** to select devices by attribute, e.g. \"an H100 with ≥ 80GB of memory\";\n- multiple containers or Pods can reference **the same claim** to share a device;\n- vendor configuration can be passed per claim;\n- dynamically partitionable devices (PartitionableDevices, similar to on-demand MIG slicing) reached Beta in v1.36.\nNVIDIA's DRA driver (donated to the CNCF) first went GA in 2025-08 with a focus on ComputeDomain, managing IMEX channels for multi-node NVLink systems like GB200; v25.12.0 in 2026-02 announced GA for GPU allocation and dynamic MIG in alpha.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nA device plugin can only say \"give me 1 GPU\"; it can't express requirements such as model, memory, or topology, and it can't share. DRA fills these gaps and is the direction for fine-grained GPU scheduling. Current limitations: the scheduler does not yet preempt other Pods for DRA resources, and the ecosystem and drivers are still evolving.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"What problem does DRA solve over the device plugin?\" → CEL device selection, shared claims, vendor configuration, on-demand partitioning.\n- Remember: GA in v1.34.","L3-T3A.10.10":"Gang scheduling and queues solve resource allocation for multi-GPU, multi-node jobs: an 8-GPU training job that gets only 6 GPUs starts waiting, those 6 GPUs sit idle, and several such jobs can deadlock each other.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Gang scheduling**: all or nothing; Pods start together only when all of them can be scheduled.\n- **Queues and quotas**: manage who goes first, how much they get, and whether they can borrow or preempt.\nMain options:\n- **Native Kubernetes**: v1.35 (2025-12) added a Workload API with gang policies (alpha), which went Beta in v1.37 (2026-08), but the feature gate is off by default.\n- **Kueue**: ClusterQueue (cluster-level quota pool) + LocalQueue (namespace-level, per tenant); queues in the same cohort can borrow each other's idle quota; supports fair sharing and preemption by priority or borrowing status; topology-aware scheduling (TAS) went Beta in v0.14.\n- **Volcano**: a batch scheduler based on kube-batch, a CNCF incubating project.\n- **KAI Scheduler**: open-sourced by NVIDIA from Run:ai, supporting gang scheduling, hierarchical queues, DRF fair sharing, bin-packing, and topology awareness.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nGPUs are expensive and scarce: without gang scheduling you get partial allocation and deadlock; without queues one team can monopolize the cluster. Borrowing raises utilization and preemption protects high-priority jobs, but preemption interrupts training and needs checkpointing to go with it.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why gang scheduling is needed: partial allocation holds GPUs and can deadlock.\n- Kueue's ClusterQueue / LocalQueue / cohort borrowing is a common follow-up.","L3-T3A.10.11":"KAI Scheduler and HAMi provide software GPU partitioning: splitting one GPU among multiple workloads by memory / compute share, which is more flexible than MIG's fixed profiles.\n\n**How it works**\n- **How software partitioning works**: the scheduler allocates GPUs by share and can optionally intercept CUDA calls to enforce a memory cap. Whether isolation is real depends on enforcement: KAI's default mode does not prevent overuse, and there is no fault isolation.\n- **HAMi**: a GPU virtualization and partitioning project that became a CNCF incubating project in 2026-07.\n- **KAI Scheduler**: open-sourced by NVIDIA from Run:ai in 2025-04 and entered the CNCF Sandbox in 2025-12. Besides sharing GPUs by memory share (GPU fractions), it supports gang scheduling, hierarchical queues, DRF (Dominant Resource Fairness) fair sharing, bin-packing and consolidation, and topology awareness. KAI can enforce memory caps via HAMi-core or NVIDIA CDI.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nMIG profiles are fixed and repartitioning requires an empty GPU, while time-slicing has no memory isolation at all; software partitioning offers a middle ground of \"arbitrary shares + optional enforcement,\" suited to cases that need more flexible shares than MIG, such as development platforms and several low-traffic services. The cost is weaker isolation than MIG's hardware partitions and no fault isolation.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When comparing GPU-sharing methods, software partitioning is the fourth: flexible, but isolation depends on enforcement.\n- KAI's distinguishing feature is combining GPU-share sharing with gang scheduling, hierarchical queues, and DRF in a single scheduler.","L3-T3A.10.12":"LeaderWorkerSet (LWS) is the Kubernetes workload API for multi-node inference: when a model is deployed across several machines (e.g. TP=8 within a node, PP across nodes), the Pods on those machines together form one model replica and must be managed as a unit.\n\n**How it works**\n- LWS treats a group of Pods (1 leader + several workers) as **the unit of replication**, creating, rolling-upgrading, and scaling by group;\n- the default restart policy recreates the whole group if any Pod in it fails;\n- a group can be required to occupy a topology domain exclusively;\n- v0.9.0 (2026-06) added DisaggregatedSet for P/D disaggregation.\nvLLM, SGLang, llm-d, and NVIDIA Dynamo all use it for multi-node inference. To place a group of Pods in the same NVLink domain, use GFD's `nvidia.com/gpu.clique` node label, the DRA driver's ComputeDomain, or topology-aware scheduling in Kueue or KAI; Dynamo's Grove provides hierarchical gang scheduling and topology-aware placement (still pre-release).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nA plain Deployment treats each Pod as an independent replica and recreates only the one that died, but in multi-node inference the whole replica is unusable if any Pod fails, and recreating a single Pod can't restore the communication group. TP must stay within an NVLink domain or all-reduce gets slowed by the network, so topology-aware placement is just as critical.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"Why can't multi-node inference use a plain Deployment?\" → It must be created, upgraded, and restarted as a group, which is exactly LWS's semantics.\n- Mention ways to place within an NVLink domain: the gpu.clique label, ComputeDomain, topology-aware scheduling.","L3-T3A.10.13":"GPU fragmentation means the cluster has enough idle GPUs in total, but they are scattered across nodes and can't be assembled into the whole-node resources a multi-GPU job needs. For example, if 3 single-GPU jobs are scattered across an 8-GPU node, an 8-GPU job can no longer fit there.\n\n**How it works**\nThe countermeasure is to make resources \"used together, idle together\":\n- **Bin-packing**: fill one GPU node before using the next, instead of the default spread scheduling, leaving whole nodes free for large jobs;\n- **Separate node pools**: keep training and inference in different node pools, so long-running single-GPU inference jobs don't fragment the whole nodes training needs;\n- **Standardize MIG profiles**: plan MIG partitioning uniformly by workload rather than slicing each GPU differently, or the scheduler will struggle to match;\n- Schedulers such as KAI Scheduler also support bin-packing and consolidation.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nGPUs are expensive, and fragmentation causes the waste of \"there seem to be free GPUs, but large jobs can't be scheduled.\" Another easy trap: **Cluster Autoscaler's GPU scale-down threshold is based on GPU requests, not actual utilization**, so a Pod that requests a GPU but barely uses it prevents the node from scaling down. The cost of bin-packing is that a single node failure affects more jobs and hot spots become more concentrated.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Three countermeasures to fragmentation: bin-packing, separate node pools, standardized MIG profiles.\n- Cluster Autoscaler looking at requests rather than utilization is a detail that signals hands-on experience.","L3-T3A.10.14":"The core takeaway for GPU monitoring: don't use `nvidia-smi` GPU utilization to judge how busy a GPU is; look at DCGM profiling metrics instead, and watch for Xid hardware errors.\n\n**How it works**\nGPU utilization is defined as the fraction of the sampling period during which a kernel is running; a tiny kernel running continuously counts as 100%, so it doesn't measure how much work is done. More useful DCGM metrics:\n- `DCGM_FI_PROF_SM_ACTIVE`: the fraction of SMs with at least one warp running; ≥ 0.8 is \"necessary but not sufficient,\" and < 0.5 likely means low efficiency;\n- `DCGM_FI_PROF_SM_OCCUPANCY`: resident warps relative to the maximum, read together with SM_ACTIVE;\n- `DCGM_FI_PROF_PIPE_TENSOR_ACTIVE`: the fraction of time Tensor Cores are busy, which should be high for prefill and training;\n- `DCGM_FI_PROF_DRAM_ACTIVE`: memory bandwidth utilization, where about 0.8 is already the practical peak; this is the main metric for decode.\nNote that in dcgm-exporter's default config, SM_ACTIVE and SM_OCCUPANCY are commented out and must be enabled manually; with time-slicing on, DCGM can't attribute metrics to specific Pods.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nLLM decode is bandwidth-bound, so DRAM_ACTIVE tells you how far you are from the ceiling; prefill is compute-bound, so look at Tensor activity. For hardware faults, watch Xid errors (e.g. 48 double-bit ECC, 79 GPU fallen off the bus, 94 / 95 memory errors); when they appear, cordon and drain the node.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- State the definition of GPU utilization and why it is misleading.\n- Decode: look at DRAM_ACTIVE; prefill: look at Tensor activity.","L3-T3A.11.1":"There are three main server-side inference engines: vLLM, SGLang, and TensorRT-LLM. The choice determines which optimizations you can use and which models and hardware are supported.\n\n**How it works**\n- **vLLM**: supports NVIDIA / AMD / Intel / TPU and more; core techniques include PagedAttention, continuous batching, prefix caching, chunked prefill, speculative decoding, structured outputs, multi-LoRA, P/D disaggregation, and KV connectors. It has the broadest model coverage and the largest ecosystem (with the Transformers backend as a fallback) and a mature OpenAI-compatible API; limitations are fast iteration with behavior changing across versions, and slightly lower peak performance than TRT-LLM. The latest as of 2026-09 is v0.30.0, with MRV2 as the default.\n- **SGLang**: RadixAttention (radix-tree prefix caching), zero-overhead CPU scheduling, FlashInfer kernels, XGrammar structured outputs, HiCache tiered caching, and large-scale EP. It stands out on multi-turn / tree-shaped reuse, agent, and RL rollout workloads; model coverage is slightly narrower than vLLM.\n- **TensorRT-LLM**: NVIDIA-only, chasing peak performance; since 1.2 PyTorch is the sole execution backend; FP8 / NVFP4, speculative decoding, P/D disaggregation. It has the highest performance ceiling on Blackwell and pairs with Dynamo / NIM, but is NVIDIA-only and complex to set up and tune.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nRule of thumb: vLLM by default for serving; seriously evaluate SGLang for agent / multi-turn / RL rollout workloads or when you need large-scale EP; choose TensorRT-LLM for pure NVIDIA setups, chasing the last 20% of performance, or when using NIM. Before quoting any \"X is N times faster than Y,\" check the assumptions behind the SemiAnalysis InferenceX benchmark.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- One-sentence positioning for each of the three.\n- Why SGLang's RadixAttention suits multi-turn and tree-shaped reuse.","L3-T3A.11.2":"On-device and local engines target single users, personal devices, or local development, a different goal from server-side engines built for high concurrency.\n\n**How it works**\n- **llama.cpp**: C/C++, supporting CPU, Apple Metal, CUDA, Vulkan, and almost every other platform; GGUF format with all kinds of low-bit quantization, CPU–GPU hybrid offloading, an OpenAI-compatible `llama-server`, and speculative decoding. Zero dependencies and the first choice on-device, but throughput and concurrency are far below server engines, and support for new architectures lags.\n- **MLX / mlx-lm**: Apple's official framework for Apple Silicon, with zero-copy unified memory, Metal kernels, 4/8-bit quantization, LoRA fine-tuning, and `mlx_lm.server`; the best performance on Mac, but Apple hardware only.\n- **Ollama**: an easy-to-use wrapper around llama.cpp plus a model library; the 0.19 preview in 2026-03 added an MLX backend on Apple Silicon. The best local development experience, but designed for a single user and not suited to production concurrency.\n- **TGI** (Hugging Face): once one of the big three serving engines; its README states it is in maintenance mode, the repository is archived, and it officially recommends vLLM / SGLang / llama.cpp / MLX.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nSelection: llama.cpp for cross-platform, MLX for performance on Apple, Ollama for local development; for running large models on a single consumer GPU, ExLlama or low-bit GGUF with llama.cpp. **Don't put TGI in new project designs anymore**; this is a point that shows your information is current.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Limitations of local engines: single user, poor concurrency.\n- TGI is in maintenance mode; the serving landscape has converged on vLLM / SGLang / TensorRT-LLM.","L3-T3A.11.3":"ExLlamaV2 / V3 are extreme-quantization inference engines focused on consumer NVIDIA GPUs, aiming to run 70B-class models on a single 24 GB card with the best possible quality.\n\n**How it works**\n- They use their own quantization formats: **EXL2** (V2) and **EXL3** (V3). EXL3 is based on QTIP's trellis quantization, an advanced vector / trellis-code method that retains more information at very low bit widths than simple element-wise quantization.\n- These formats support non-integer average bit widths, so you can tune \"bits per weight\" precisely to a memory budget and keep as much precision as possible within the memory limit.\n- A 70B model is about 140 GB in BF16; fitting it into 24 GB of memory requires compressing to roughly 2–2.5 bits per weight (while leaving room for KV), which is exactly ExLlama's sweet spot.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIn the narrow scenario of \"a 70B-class model on a single 24 GB card,\" ExLlama has the best quality. Limitations: it is a community project with limited serving features (concurrency, multi-tenancy, and production-grade APIs fall short of vLLM), and it only supports NVIDIA. It and low-bit GGUF with llama.cpp are two choices for the same scenario: the latter is cross-platform with a larger ecosystem, the former has better quality on NVIDIA consumer cards.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Remember the positioning: single consumer NVIDIA GPU, extreme quantization, EXL2 / EXL3.\n- Be able to explain why running 70B on 24 GB requires compressing to the 2–3 bit range.","L3-T3A.11.4":"MLC-LLM is a TVM-based cross-platform LLM compilation and deployment solution that compiles the same model to CUDA, Metal, Vulkan, WebGPU, and phones; WebLLM is its browser version, running models directly in the browser via WebGPU.\n\n**How it works**\n- **The compilation approach**: rather than hand-writing kernels for each platform, use an ML compiler like TVM to automatically generate and optimize code for the target hardware from the model's computation graph, so one model definition covers many backends.\n- **WebLLM**: runs 1–8B-class models in the browser via WebGPU, with no installation and no data leaving the user's device.\n- Part of the same on-device stack: small models (0.5–4B) + 4-bit quantization + speculative decoding + KV cache quantization + distillation.\n- The MLC team is also one of the origins of XGrammar (a structured-output backend).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIts advantage is reach: browsers and mobile, platforms server engines can't touch. It suits privacy-first lightweight scenarios, offline apps, and demos with zero server cost. Limitations: a smaller ecosystem, and on-device inference is limited by memory bandwidth, so only small models run, with limited speed and quality. Compared with llama.cpp, MLC's selling points are its compilation approach and WebGPU support; llama.cpp's are its ecosystem and ease of use.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- One sentence: TVM compilation, focused on browsers (WebLLM) and mobile.\n- On-device selection: MLX on Mac, llama.cpp for cross-platform, WebLLM in the browser.","L3-T3A.11.5":"Choosing a GPU comes down to three numbers: memory, memory bandwidth, and interconnect. First compute memory to set the GPU count, then look at bandwidth to set decode latency, and finally check the interconnect to decide whether TP is viable.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Memory**: total requirement = weights + KV cache at target concurrency + activations and overhead, which determines the minimum number of GPUs.\n- **Bandwidth**: each decode step reads the weights once, so decode is bandwidth-bound and bandwidth directly sets per-request decode speed.\n- **Interconnect**: TP needs an all-reduce in every layer and only pays off within NVLink; GPUs without NVLink (such as L40S) are only suited to single-GPU or DP.\nReference numbers: H100 SXM 80 GB, 3.35 TB/s, NVLink 900 GB/s; H200 141 GB, 4.8 TB/s, same compute as H100; B200 180 GB, 8 TB/s, FP4 support, NVLink 1.8 TB/s; L40S 48 GB, 864 GB/s, no NVLink; A100 80 GB at 2.0 TB/s, no FP8.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nFor decode-heavy workloads, the H200's advantages over the H100 (1.43× bandwidth, 1.76× memory) are often worth more than the price difference. Blackwell's value lies in FP4 and larger NVLink domains: NVL72 connects 72 GPUs into a single NVLink domain, so EP for large MoEs doesn't have to cross InfiniBand. Picking GPUs by FLOPS alone is a common mistake, since LLM decode is bandwidth-bound most of the time.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Three steps: memory → bandwidth → interconnect.\n- Be able to explain why decode workloads care more about bandwidth than compute.","L3-T3A.11.6":"Beyond GPUs there is a class of specialized inference chips: Cerebras, Groq LPU, Google TPU, AWS Inferentia / Trainium. Their shared idea is to trade on-chip SRAM or higher bandwidth for single-stream decode speed, at the cost of capacity and generality.\n\n**How it works**\nThe LLM decode bottleneck is reading the weights from memory at every step. If the weights sit directly in on-chip SRAM (with far higher bandwidth than HBM), single-stream decode can be much faster.\n- **Cerebras**: a wafer-scale chip with all weights in on-chip SRAM; single-stream decode can exceed 10× that of a GPU. CS-4 was released in 2026-08 (the vendor claims up to 30× the per-user tok/s of GPU solutions). GPT-5.3-Codex-Spark runs on Cerebras with output above 1000 tok/s.\n- **Groq LPU**: a deterministic SRAM architecture; in 2025-12 it signed a non-exclusive inference technology licensing agreement with NVIDIA, and its core team joined NVIDIA.\n- **Google TPU** (Ironwood targets inference) and **AWS Inferentia / Trainium**: accelerators built in-house by cloud providers.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nSRAM capacity is small, so large models need many chips; the software ecosystem and model coverage lag GPUs. But the many sequential calls of agents make single-stream decode speed a new axis of competition, and these chips change the cost structure of ultra-low-latency agents. In interviews they serve as supporting evidence for the argument that \"decode is bandwidth-bound.\"\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Core logic: SRAM bandwidth buys single-stream speed, at the cost of capacity and generality.\n- The NVIDIA–Groq agreement confirms the value of ultra-low-latency inference.","L3-T3A.11.7":"The roofline model uses arithmetic intensity to judge whether a computation is bandwidth-bound or compute-bound. It explains why, in LLM decode, \"adding batch is nearly free,\" which is the economic foundation of continuous batching.\n\n**How it works**\n- Arithmetic intensity = compute / memory traffic (FLOP/byte).\n- One decode step (batch B, N parameters, b bytes per parameter): compute is about `2·N·B` FLOP; the weights are read once, so traffic is about `N·b` bytes; intensity is about `2B / b`, proportional to B.\n- Knee = peak compute / bandwidth: for H100, 989 TFLOPS (BF16) / 3.35 TB/s ≈ 295 FLOP/byte.\n- With BF16 weights (b=2), everything below B ≈ 295 is bandwidth-bound; with weight-only FP8 (b=1, still using BF16 compute), the knee is at B ≈ 150; with W8A8 on FP8 Tensor Cores, peak compute doubles (about 1979 TFLOPS) and the knee returns to B ≈ 295.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nBelow the knee, increasing the batch from 1 to 100 barely changes step time, so throughput grows linearly and cost per token falls linearly; past the knee you are compute-bound, and adding batch only lengthens TPOT. In practice, KV cache reads also consume bandwidth, even exceeding the weights at long context, so **the real knee comes earlier**, which is the fundamental reason long-context, high-concurrency serving is hard. Prefill processes L tokens at once with intensity about `2L/b`, so it is already compute-bound at a few hundred tokens.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Derive the 295 figure on the spot.\n- MoE reads only the active experts per step, but as B grows, the active set tends toward all experts.\n- How to verify: plot measured throughput vs batch; the linear segment is the bandwidth regime, the plateau is the compute regime.","L3-T3A.11.8":"Estimating the per-token price is the foundation of self-hosted inference cost analysis: convert the GPU hourly price into cost per million tokens and compare it with API prices.\n\n**How it works**\n- Cost per million tokens = GPU hourly price × GPU count / (effective throughput tok/s × 3600) × 10^6\n- Effective throughput = peak load-tested throughput within SLO × utilization (average production load / peak, typically 0.3–0.6)\nWorked example: Llama-3.1-70B FP8 on 4×H100 ($3.5/h per GPU), peak 2,500 tok/s within SLO, utilization 0.5, gives about $3.11 per million output tokens; the same setup at utilization 0.2 gives about $7.78; 8B FP8 on a single H100 at a peak of 6,000 tok/s and utilization 0.5 gives about $0.32.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThree caveats that must be stated together:\n1. **Input tokens cost money too**: the formula counts only output; prefill GPU time must be apportioned by the input/output ratio. Agent workloads have an input:output ratio of about 100:1, so prefill may be the main cost, and the prefix caching hit rate determines the real prefill volume, which means the cache hit rate must be a variable in the cost model.\n2. **Utilization is the biggest lever**: on the same hardware, utilization of 0.2 vs 0.6 means a 3× difference in unit price, which is also why APIs or serverless are often cheaper when traffic is unsteady.\n3. **The SLO constrains throughput**: the peak must be throughput that meets the TTFT / TPOT targets (goodput); throughput beyond the knee doesn't count.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Write the formula on the spot and plug in a worked example.\n- Raising utilization: mix online and offline workloads to fill troughs, share GPUs across models, and handle peaks with serverless.","L3-T3A.12.1":"Apple Silicon's unified memory lets the CPU and GPU share the same memory, so a Mac can hold large models that won't fit on a single GPU. Its profile is \"fits very large models, but decode speed is mediocre and prefill is slow.\"\n\n**How it works**\n- **Capacity**: M-series Max / Ultra chips can be configured with 128–512 GB of unified memory, far more than any single data-center GPU.\n- **Bandwidth**: on the order of 400–800 GB/s (M3 Ultra about 819 GB/s), between an L40S and an A100, far below the H100's 3.35 TB/s.\n- Each decode step reads the weights once, so speed ≈ bandwidth / weight bytes, and bandwidth sets the decode ceiling; prefill is compute-bound, and Mac GPU compute is far below data-center GPUs, so prefill is slow.\n- In practice: a 70B 4-bit model on a high-end Mac Studio typically runs at single-digit to low-teens tok/s.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nSuited to single-user local use, not high-concurrency serving. For agent developers, the value of local models is running tool-call and evaluation pipelines end to end at zero cost during development, handling private data, and experimenting with small-model routing. WWDC 2026 had sessions on MLX distributed inference and training, making multi-Mac cluster inference a viable path.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Estimate decode speed with \"bandwidth / weight bytes,\" and explain why a Mac can fit a model but not run it fast.\n- Hands-on exercise: measure prefill and decode curves with MLX and verify that decode tok/s is roughly inversely proportional to weight bytes.","L3-T3A.12.2":"There are four main tools for running LLMs locally on a Mac: MLX / mlx-lm, llama.cpp, Ollama, and LM Studio, each with different trade-offs in performance, ecosystem, and ease of use.\n\n**How it works**\n- **MLX / mlx-lm**: Apple's official framework with Metal kernels and the best performance. After `pip install mlx-lm`, use `mlx_lm.generate` to generate, `mlx_lm.server` to start an OpenAI-compatible server, `mlx_lm.convert -q` to quantize, and `mlx_lm.lora` to fine-tune; mlx-community hosts many pre-quantized models.\n- **llama.cpp**: `llama-server -m model.gguf -ngl 99` (all layers on the GPU); the largest GGUF ecosystem, cross-platform, with Metal backend performance close to MLX.\n- **Ollama**: `ollama run qwen3:8b`, with an OpenAI-compatible API on by default; the easiest option. Since 0.19 (2026-03) it can use an MLX backend on Apple Silicon (preview).\n- **LM Studio**: a GUI app supporting both GGUF and MLX.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nSelection: mlx-lm for performance and fine-tuning; llama.cpp for model-format coverage and cross-platform consistency; Ollama if you just want to get running quickly and hook into an agent framework; LM Studio if you'd rather avoid the command line. All of them suit a single user, not production concurrency. For agent developers, their value is zero-cost local integration testing of tool calls, evaluation pipelines, and private data handling.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- One-sentence distinction: mlx-lm is fastest and supports fine-tuning, llama.cpp has the largest ecosystem, Ollama is the easiest, LM Studio is a GUI.\n- All provide OpenAI-compatible APIs and plug directly into agent frameworks.","L3-T3A.12.3":"Inference on phones and in browsers is severely limited by memory bandwidth, so on-device uses a \"small model + low-bit quantization\" stack and hands hard problems to the cloud through device–cloud collaboration.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Bandwidth limits**: NPUs (Apple Neural Engine, Qualcomm Hexagon, etc.) are designed for low power, but LLM decode is still limited by memory bandwidth; phone LPDDR5X is about 50–100 GB/s, so 10–30 tok/s for a 3B-class model is typical.\n- **Browsers**: WebLLM (MLC) runs 1–8B models via WebGPU, suited to privacy-first lightweight scenarios.\n- **On-device stack**: small models (0.5–4B) + 4-bit quantization + speculative decoding + KV cache quantization + distillation; architectures lean toward GQA / sliding windows / hybrid linear attention to keep KV under control.\n- **Device–cloud collaboration**: try on-device first and escalate to the cloud when uncertain, a common architecture for mobile agents in 2026.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nOn-device brings privacy, offline availability, zero inference cost, and low network latency; the cost is limited model capability. Device–cloud collaboration balances the two, and the key is the routing policy: decide whether to escalate to the cloud based on model confidence or task complexity. Inaccurate routing means either poor quality or high cloud cost.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Use \"bandwidth / weight bytes\" to explain the 10–30 tok/s range for a 3B model on a phone.\n- In an interview, describing device–cloud collaboration as \"routing by confidence / task complexity\" is enough.","L3-T3A.12.4":"OS-built-in models are small on-device models and APIs provided directly in the operating system by phone vendors, so developers can use on-device LLM capabilities without shipping their own model. The main examples are the Apple Foundation Models framework and Gemini Nano on Android.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Apple Foundation Models framework**: since 2025 it provides an on-device model of about 3B parameters with a Swift API, supporting **guided generation** (constrained structured output, essentially constrained decoding) and tool calling; 2026 reports suggest developers can plug in models from mlx-community (unverified).\n- **Gemini Nano**: provided on Android through the **AICore** system service.\n- The system manages, updates, and schedules the model centrally; apps call it through an API without bundling weights in the app package.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nAdvantages: zero distribution cost (no need for every app to ship several GB of weights), system-level power and memory optimization, data never leaves the device, and no inference fees. Limitations: the model is small (about 3B class), with limited complex reasoning and long-context ability; customizability is restricted by the platform; behavior is inconsistent across platforms. Suited to lightweight tasks such as summarization, classification, extraction, and simple tool calls; complex tasks still need device–cloud collaboration to escalate to cloud models.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Remember: Apple's is about 3B with guided generation and tool calling; Android provides Gemini Nano via AICore.\n- Guided generation and server-side constrained decoding are the same kind of technique.","L3-T3A.12.5":"Knowledge distillation trains a small student model to learn the capabilities of a large teacher model. From a deployment perspective, it is one of the main ways to make the model itself smaller and thus cut inference cost. Unlike quantization, distillation changes the parameter count.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Logit distillation** (Hinton 2015): the student fits the teacher's soft distribution via KL divergence; requires the same vocabulary and access to logits, so it is a white-box method.\n- **Sequence-level distillation / SFT on teacher outputs**: fine-tune the student on text generated by the teacher; only output text is needed, so it is black-box and the most common. DeepSeek used R1 to generate about 800k samples and did SFT only on the Qwen2.5 and Llama families, producing distilled models from 1.5B to 70B; the conclusion was that for small models, distilling from a large reasoning model is more effective and cheaper than doing RL themselves.\n- **On-policy distillation (GKD, etc.)**: the student generates its own outputs and the teacher provides a distribution per token, mitigating exposure bias and combining on-policy sampling with dense supervision.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nWith a clearly defined narrow task and plenty of teacher data, a 7B distilled model matching a 70B model on that task is common, cutting inference cost by an order of magnitude. Watch compliance: commercial API terms of use usually prohibit using their outputs to train competing models. On-policy distillation also requires the teacher to provide token-level logprobs.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Three forms of distillation: logit, sequence-level, on-policy; distinguish white-box from black-box.\n- Why distillation beats RL for small models: weak exploration and sparse rewards, while distillation provides dense supervision.","L3-T3A.12.6":"Pruning makes a model smaller by removing unimportant weights. The key distinction: unstructured pruning barely speeds anything up on general-purpose GPUs unless it satisfies 2:4 structured sparsity; structured pruning combined with distillation directly yields a smaller dense model.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Unstructured pruning** (SparseGPT, Wanda): zeroes out 50% of elements by weight importance. But scattered zeros can't be accelerated by general-purpose GPUs, and the matrices are still computed densely.\n- **2:4 structured sparsity**: keep 2 of every 4 weights; natively supported by Tensor Cores since Ampere, with a theoretical 2× compute.\n- **Structured pruning**: remove whole heads, layers, or channels, directly producing a smaller dense model that actually saves money in deployment. NVIDIA's Minitron used \"pruning + distillation\" to get 8B / 4B models from 15B, at 40× less training cost than training small models from scratch; Sheared LLaMA follows a similar idea.\n- Depth pruning (removing layers) is most effective for latency; width pruning is most effective for memory.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nPruning loses capability and almost always needs distillation or continued training to recover. It requires training resources, and off-the-shelf small models are often cheaper, so it comes last among compression options. The theoretical 2× of 2:4 sparsity is usually discounted in real end-to-end inference, because decode is bandwidth-bound.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why unstructured pruning doesn't speed things up: GPUs can't exploit scattered sparsity.\n- Removing layers cuts latency; reducing width saves memory.","L3-T3A.12.7":"The decision order for model compression is: first switch to a smaller off-the-shelf model → quantize → distill → prune, and sign off on your own eval set. The goal is to meet the quality bar at lower cost.\n\n**How it works**\n1. **Switch to an off-the-shelf smaller model**: the lowest cost, much cheaper than pruning yourself; the open-source community usually offers several sizes.\n2. **Quantize**: doesn't change the parameter count but reduces bytes per parameter, with almost no training cost and direct memory and bandwidth gains (FP8, INT4, NVFP4, etc.).\n3. **Distill**: use when you have a clearly defined narrow task and plenty of teacher data; a 7B distilled model matching 70B on a specific task is common; needs training resources and data.\n4. **Prune**: only when you have training resources and none of the available sizes fit, usually with distillation to recover capability.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe order runs from low to high cost and from small to large risk: later steps need more training resources, longer engineering cycles, and harder-to-control quality risk. Every compression must be signed off on **your own eval set**, not on general benchmarks; matching general scores doesn't mean your task won't regress. For example, quantization can be especially sensitive for long generation, JSON tool calls, or math reasoning.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Recite the order and explain why (rising cost and risk).\n- Sign-off method: design an eval set of 100–200 items covering short QA, reasoning, tool calls, and long generation, and report a quality–speed–memory comparison.","L3-T3A.13.1":"Long context is not free capacity but a resource you pay for by length. Understanding its cost structure is the foundation for designing long-context serving and admission control.\n\n**How it works**\nTwo physical facts:\n- **Prefill attention compute grows quadratically with length**: FlashAttention avoids N×N memory, but the compute is still quadratic; a 100k-token prefill on an H100 means a TTFT measured in seconds.\n- **KV cache grows linearly with length and occupies memory**: Llama-3.1-8B uses 128 KB per token, so a 128k context = 16 GB; a 24 GB card can run only 1 such request, and concurrency drops sharply.\n- In addition, each decode step reads the full KV; at long context KV reads exceed weight reads, so TPOT rises with length.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nDesign countermeasures: prefix caching (long documents are often referenced repeatedly, and a hit brings prefill close to zero, which is the key to making long context affordable); chunked prefill to cap the per-step budget so it doesn't wreck other requests' ITL; route very long requests to a dedicated instance pool by length; use FP8 or lower-bit KV with offloading support; use DCP to shard KV across GPUs when one GPU can't hold it; admission control and quotas. API pricing reflects this too: Gemini prices whole requests above 200k at a higher tier; Anthropic from 4.6 onward prices 1M context at the standard rate, but the new tokenizer produces more tokens.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"What happens when a 100k request enters the system?\" Answer in three parts: prefill, KV, decode.\n- FlashAttention addresses memory and memory-access efficiency, not the quadratic compute.","L3-T3A.13.2":"The thinking tokens (reasoning tokens) a reasoning model generates before answering are billed at the output rate and often make output overtake input as the largest cost item. Controlling them is a key cost problem in deploying reasoning models.\n\n**How it works**\n1. **Billed at the output rate**, consistently across all major APIs, and the output rate is usually 4–6× the input rate;\n2. **The count depends on the task and settings**: a few hundred for easy questions, tens of thousands for hard ones; the knobs are OpenAI's `reasoning_effort`, Anthropic's effort (adaptive thinking is recommended from Opus 4.6 onward), and Gemini's thinking budget;\n3. **They consume context and generation time**: 10k thinking tokens at 50 tok/s means 200 seconds of latency;\n4. **Whether they are kept across turns** affects subsequent input cost and caching.\nWorked example: one agent turn with 60k input (95% cache hits), 4k thinking, 600 visible output, on a $2 / $10 model: input about $0.018, output (4000 + 600) × $10 / 10^6 = $0.046.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nCost-control essentials: tier effort by task difficulty (no thinking or low effort for simple tool calls); use non-reasoning models for routing and simple steps; cap thinking and monitor the distribution of `reasoning_tokens`. From an engineering-decision standpoint: training is a one-off cost, while test-time compute is an ongoing cost multiplied by QPS; when volume is high and latency matters, push capability into the weights; when volume is low, value is high, and results are verifiable, test-time scaling is more cost-effective; in between, use adaptive budgets.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Use the worked example to show how thinking tokens overtake input cost.\n- Tier effort by difficulty and monitor reasoning_tokens.","L3-T3A.13.3":"The Batch API and fast mode are two pricing options on commercial APIs that point in opposite directions: the Batch API trades time for money, fast mode trades money for time.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Batch API**: Anthropic, OpenAI, and Gemini all offer asynchronous batch processing at about a 50% discount, usually returning within 24 hours. The provider can schedule these requests into idle periods and process them in large batches, raising GPU utilization, which is why it can discount them. Suited to evaluation, offline labeling, and data generation. The self-hosted counterpart is an offline queue that soaks up the GPUs' overnight idle hours.\n- **Fast mode**: Anthropic offers a mode with significantly faster output for some Opus models, at double the price (e.g. Opus 5.5 standard $4 / $20, fast mode $8 / $40), and it **does not share the prompt cache** with standard mode.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe two are opposite ends of the same latency–cost trade-off, corresponding in self-hosted systems to \"large batches for high throughput and low cost\" versus \"small batches, speculative decoding, and pricier hardware for low latency.\" The choice depends on whether anyone is waiting for the result: always send offline jobs through the Batch API; consider fast mode only for user-facing, interactive agent scenarios sensitive to single-stream speed. The fact that fast mode doesn't share the cache is easy to overlook: switching modes makes every cache lookup miss, so the real cost for long-prefix agents can far exceed 2×.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- The Batch API is about half price and returns within 24 hours.\n- Fast mode costs more and doesn't share the prompt cache.","L3-T3A.13.4":"\"Self-host inference or keep using APIs\" is a common architecture decision question. The interviewer wants to see you ask about constraints first, compare quantitatively, and then propose an incremental plan, not an unverified assertion like \"self-hosting is cheaper and safer.\"\n\n**How it works**\nDimensions of comparison:\n- **Quality ceiling**: APIs offer frontier closed models; self-hosting uses the strongest open models, where the gap has narrowed but persists on frontier tasks.\n- **Unit cost**: APIs are cheap at low volume with no fixed cost; self-hosting has high fixed costs, but at high, steady volume the unit price can be an order of magnitude lower.\n- **Latency**: with APIs it's up to the provider; self-hosting is controllable (quantization, speculative decoding, specialized hardware, same data center).\n- **Data and compliance**: with APIs you must assess cross-border data transfer and retention policies, with options for zero retention or regional deployment (usually about a 10% premium); with self-hosting data stays in your domain, often a hard requirement in finance, healthcare, and government.\n- **Customization**: self-hosting allows arbitrary fine-tuning, multi-LoRA, constrained decoding, and logit intervention.\n- **Operations**: self-hosting means owning autoscaling, upgrades, and GPU failures, a long-term commitment of about 1–2 people; APIs make you dependent on the provider's rate limits and outages, so you need multi-vendor fallback.\n- **Time to market**: immediate with APIs, weeks to months for self-hosting.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nRecommended answer structure: first clarify data compliance, traffic volume and variability, latency SLO, whether fine-tuning is needed, and the team's operational capacity; then propose an incremental plan: \"start with APIs by default + abstract providers behind a gateway + migrate high-volume / sensitive workloads to self-hosting once traffic and compliance cross thresholds.\" Hybrid architectures are the most common form in 2026.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Below 30% utilization, self-hosting is usually more expensive.\n- Hidden costs of APIs: rate limits, price changes, tokenizer changes, data retention terms.","L3-T3A.13.5":"The self-hosting break-even point is the quantitative condition for whether self-hosted inference pays off on pure cost: `T × (p_api − p_self) > F`.\n\n**How it works**\n- `p_api`: the blended API unit price, computed from the actual input/output ratio and cache hit rate;\n- `p_self`: the self-hosted unit price, computed as \"GPU hourly price × GPU count / (peak throughput within SLO × utilization × 3600) × 10^6,\" including utilization;\n- `F`: the monthly fixed cost of self-hosting, including staff, monitoring, and spare capacity;\n- `T`: monthly token volume.\nSelf-hosting only pays off when monthly token volume times the price difference exceeds the fixed cost. Typically this means **steady traffic of at least several billion tokens per month**.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe formula turns \"self-hosting is cheaper\" from intuition into a calculable judgment and exposes two key variables: utilization (dropping from 0.5 to 0.2 makes the self-hosted unit price about 2.5×) and cache hit rate (prefill dominates agent workloads, and the hit rate determines the real input cost). For teams below the break-even point, the reasons to self-host are mainly data compliance, customization, and latency, not saving money. Also, API prices change quickly, so the cost model should be parameterized rather than hard-coded.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Write out the formula and explain each variable.\n- Emphasize \"steady\": traffic variability drags utilization down and pushes p_self up.","L3-T3A.14.1":"Four threads run through the evolution of inference systems in 2025–2026 and can serve as an answer framework for open questions like \"how do you see trends in inference systems?\"\n\n**How it works**\n1. **KV-cache-centric**: prefix caching → tiered offloading → cross-instance sharing → KV-aware routing → KV quantization. Agent workloads, with inputs far larger than outputs and long gaps between calls, have made this the top priority.\n2. **Disaggregation and heterogeneity**: separating prefill and decode, using different GPUs or even different architectures for different phases (GPU + LPU / wafer-scale chips); encoder disaggregation and attention–FFN disaggregation (AFD, aimed at MoE) have also appeared.\n3. **Lower precision by default**: FP8 has become the default, FP4 (NVFP4 / MXFP4) is spreading with Blackwell, and models ship directly in low precision (e.g. gpt-oss ships MoE weights in MXFP4), turning quantization from an after-the-fact task for deployers into part of the model release.\n4. **Speculative decoding as standard**: MTP ships with models, EAGLE / parallel drafting are standard in engines, and schedulers toggle it adaptively by load.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nBehind all four threads is the same physical constraint: decode is limited by memory capacity and bandwidth. KV-centric design and low precision save capacity and bandwidth, disaggregation lets phases with different bottlenecks each use the best-suited resources, and speculative decoding trades spare compute for latency. Being able to map specific techniques onto these threads shows systematic understanding rather than scattered memorization.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Answer trend questions with four phrases: KV-centric, disaggregated and heterogeneous, lower precision by default, speculative decoding as standard.\n- Give one representative technique or event for each thread.","L3-T3A.14.2":"Several key developments in inference engines and orchestration in 2026 make good material for showing your information is current.\n\n**How it works**\n- **vLLM Model Runner V2 (MRV2)**: released in 2026-03, rewriting the execution core, which shows CPU-side overhead has become the main bottleneck for small models / fast GPUs; it became the default for all models from v0.29.0 (2026-09-09). v0.30.0 (09-22) added **Fast Start**: a resident GPU weight-cache daemon that maps weights already in GPU memory directly via CUDA IPC when the engine restarts, shortening cold start.\n- **NVIDIA Dynamo 1.0**: declared production-ready on 2026-03-16 (GTC 2026); the latest as of 09-21 is v1.5.0, which added pluggable worker-selection policies, with KVBM planned for removal in 1.6.0.\n- **llm-d joins the CNCF**: accepted as a CNCF Sandbox project on 2026-03-12, as the Kubernetes-native distributed inference stack moves toward standardization.\n- **P/D practice guide**: published by vLLM on 2026-09-29 with measurements such as \"TTFT 2.2 s vs 0.7 s over PCIe\" and \"p99 ITL 169 → 25–52 ms,\" clarifying where disaggregation applies.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nTogether these show that engines are squeezing CPU overhead and startup time, orchestration is moving toward Kubernetes standardization and production maturity, and P/D disaggregation has gone from a trendy technique to an engineering option with clear conditions for use. Versions change fast, so in interviews it's safer to state the dates and note that official release notes are authoritative.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- MRV2 shows CPU overhead has become a bottleneck.\n- Cite the P/D guide's data to argue \"don't disaggregate without a high-speed interconnect.\"","L3-T3A.14.3":"\"Shrinking KV through model architecture\" means reducing KV cache and long-context cost at model design time, rather than only through caching and quantization at deployment. The core idea is that model architecture is itself an inference optimization.\n\n**How it works**\n- **MLA** (DeepSeek-V3): compresses the KV cache into a low-rank latent, greatly reducing KV per token. Note that MLA's latent isn't split by head, so under TP every GPU must store the full KV; DeepSeek-style deployments therefore run attention with data parallelism and MoE layers with expert parallelism.\n- **Kimi Linear** (2025-10): interleaves KDA (fine-grained gating on Gated DeltaNet) with MLA layer by layer; the authors report up to 75% less KV cache and up to about 6× faster decoding at 1M context. Linear attention layers have state that doesn't grow with length.\n- **DSA** (DeepSeek-V3.2, 2025-12): after a lightning indexer scores tokens, each query attends only to the top 2048 tokens, cutting long-context cost from O(L²) to O(Lk).\n- **DeepSeek-V4** (2026-04): hybrid compressed attention combining CSA and HCA; the paper says at 1M context V4-Pro needs only 27% of V3.2's per-token FLOPs and 10% of its KV cache.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nDeployment-side techniques (prefix caching, KV quantization, offloading) have a ceiling, while model-side compression cuts cost at the root. The costs: engines need dedicated support (a hybrid KV manager so different layer types share a block pool, specialized kernels), and sparse or linear attention may affect long-range retrieval quality, which needs validation.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- List three families of approaches: MLA, hybrid linear attention, sparse attention.\n- Impact of hybrid architectures on serving: engines need a hybrid KV manager.","L3-T3A.14.4":"The inference optimization chapter comes with a set of hands-on exercises, defaulting to a Mac (no large local GPU) and covering the full chain from measuring single-machine bottlenecks to signing off on cost and quality.\n\n**How it works**\n1. **Measure prefill / decode with MLX**: load a 4-bit 7–8B model with mlx-lm, measure prefill time and decode tok/s at different prompt lengths, and compare with 8-bit and BF16; check whether prefill is superlinear and whether decode speed is roughly inversely proportional to weight bytes.\n2. **Scheduler + paged-KV simulator**: a pure-Python discrete-event simulation comparing static, continuous, and continuous + chunked prefill, with preemption when KV runs out; reproduce phenomena such as \"static TTFT explodes with load\" and \"chunked prefill flattens ITL p99 but raises TTFT.\" In measurements with the companion script `serving_scheduler_sim.py`, continuous had an ITL p99 of 192.5 ms, which chunked brought down to 29.9 ms.\n3. **vLLM load testing**: plot throughput–latency curves on a cloud GPU, comparing FP8 KV, prefix caching, and n-gram speculative decoding, within 3 hours of total GPU time.\n4. **Cache break-even experiment**: use an API to send requests with a stable prefix and varying suffix, and measure how timestamps, a concurrent first wave, and changing effort / schema damage the hit rate.\n5. **Quantization sign-off**: 8-bit / 4-bit or FP8 / AWQ, with a quality–speed–memory comparison on a self-built eval set of 100–200 items.\n6. **Constrained decoding experiment** (optional): compare unconstrained-with-retry, JSON schema, and structural tag.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThese exercises turn interview conclusions into numbers you measured yourself, so you can say \"I've measured this,\" which is the key signal separating memorization from practice.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Back up your points with data you measured yourself.\n- The simulator code can serve as material for hand-coding questions.","L3-T3B.1.1":"PagedAttention allocates KV on demand in blocks, greatly increasing concurrency, but when a request needs a new block mid-decode the pool may already be empty. Preemption is this design's safety valve: evict a running request to free memory.\n\n**How it works**\n- Whom to evict: vLLM uses LIFO, evicting the most recently admitted request, which has done the least computation and is cheapest to recompute, and which is also consistent with first-come-first-served; the evicted request goes back to the head of the waiting queue.\n- Recompute: free all blocks, and on resumption prefill \"original prompt + generated tokens\" again as a new prompt. Prefill is compute-intensive and highly parallel, much faster than decoding token by token.\n- Swap: copy blocks to CPU memory and back, at a cost of about `2 × KV bytes ÷ PCIe bandwidth`.\n- vLLM V1 defaults to RECOMPUTE; GPU<>CPU swap has been removed, along with the `--swap-space` and `--preemption-mode` flags; V1's CPU offload (KV connector) is for prefix caching and unrelated to preemption.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nFrequent preemption causes thrashing: in the simulator, shrinking the KV pool to 2,000 blocks produced 594 preemptions, TTFT p99 rose from 0.71 s to 18 s, and throughput fell from 2,287 to 1,395 tok/s. The root cause is \"concurrency × context length\" exceeding KV capacity.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why not evict the longest request: it is the most expensive to recompute and is often nearly finished.\n- Recompute vs swap: compare prefill(L) compute time with the PCIe round-trip time; V1 keeps only recompute to simplify the scheduler.\n- Countermeasures: lower `max_num_seqs`, FP8 KV, cap `max_model_len`, add replicas.","L3-T3B.1.2":"FCFS suffers from head-of-line blocking: a request that will generate 2,000 tokens blocks a string of requests that need only 20. In queueing theory, shortest-job-first (SJF) minimizes average waiting time, but LLM output length isn't known in advance.\n\n**How it works**\n- Learning-to-rank approximate SJF: Fu et al. (NeurIPS 2024) found that predicting exact output length is hard, but predicting the relative order of \"which is longer\" within a batch is much easier; they use a small model for learning to rank and schedule in predicted order. They report 2.8× lower latency for chat and 6.5× higher throughput for synthetic data generation.\n- Priority scheduling: vLLM uses `--scheduling-policy priority`, where lower values go first and ties are broken by arrival time; V1 added it in v0.9.2, and since v0.28.0 priority can be read from an HTTP header. A typical setup gives interactive requests high priority and offline evaluation and batch synthesis low priority.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIf predictions are wrong, SJF degrades and can be worse than FCFS; long requests may starve, so aging (priority rises with waiting time) is needed as a backstop; when priority combines with preemption, high-priority requests repeatedly preempt low-priority ones, causing a lot of recomputation.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why SJF is hard: job length, i.e. output length, is unknown, so only relative order is predicted.\n- Preventing starvation: aging, a cap on maximum wait, and routing very long requests to dedicated replicas.\n- Agent workloads should be scheduled per \"program,\" not per individual call.","L3-T3B.1.3":"When multiple tenants share an inference cluster, you need to ensure one large customer can't crowd out everyone else. VTC (Virtual Token Counter, Sheng et al., OSDI 2024) does fair scheduling by token service rather than by request count.\n\n**How it works**\n- Maintain a counter per client that accumulates the service it has received: a weighted sum of input and output tokens (output is more expensive).\n- Each scheduling decision prioritizes the client with the smallest counter, and it is work-conserving: as long as there are requests, the GPU is never left idle.\n- The paper proves a tight bound of 2× on the service gap between two continuously backlogged clients.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nRate-limiting by request count (RPM) is unreasonable: a 100k-token request and a 100-token request differ in cost by three orders of magnitude. Metering by tokens matches how billing works and is fairer. The cost is that fair scheduling lowers throughput for large tenants, which needs to be aligned with the business on SLAs; it addresses fairness between tenants, not optimal per-request latency (which is SJF's goal).\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why tokens rather than request count: request costs vary enormously.\n- Metering: weight input and output tokens separately, with a higher weight for output.\n- Theoretical guarantee: a tight 2× bound on the service gap between two continuously active clients.","L3-T3B.1.4":"The smaller the model and the larger the batch, the more a decode step takes only a few milliseconds on the GPU, and CPU work (scheduling, assembling inputs and block tables, sampling post-processing, detokenization) becomes the bottleneck, leaving the GPU idle between steps (gaps between kernels on the nsys timeline).\n\n**How it works** (four layers, from simple to advanced)\n1. Multi-process split: vLLM V1 puts scheduling and execution in a separate EngineCore process, and tokenization, detokenization, and HTTP streaming in the API process, communicating over ZeroMQ; together with persistent batches, V1 throughput is up to 1.7× that of V0.\n2. CUDA graphs: record the hundreds of kernel launches in one step into a graph and replay it.\n3. Overlap scheduling: while the GPU runs step t, the CPU is already preparing step t+1. The difficulty is that step t+1's input depends on the token just sampled in step t; SGLang uses \"future token\" placeholders and resolves the dependency on the GPU via CUDA events (the zero-overhead batch scheduler, on by default); vLLM calls it async scheduling, on by default since v0.14.0.\n4. Move input preparation to the GPU: vLLM Model Runner V2, which raised Qwen3-0.6B throughput by 56%.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nScheduling for step t+1 can't see step t's full results, which complicates preemption, speculative decoding, structured outputs, and stop conditions; vLLM v0.11.0's async scheduling once produced garbled output during preemption, fixed only in v0.11.1.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Diagnosing a CPU bottleneck: low GPU utilization, gaps between kernels, and throughput rising with a faster CPU.\n- Less noticeable for large models: each step takes longer on the GPU, so the CPU share is small.","L3-T3B.1.5":"temperature = 0 still gives different results in production. The main cause isn't \"GPU concurrency + floating-point error\" but dynamic batching on the server: kernels choose different tiling and reduction strategies depending on batch size, so the same request gets a different reduction order under different loads, and the results differ slightly.\n\n**How it works**\n- Thinking Machines Lab (Horace He, 2025-09) pointed out that although floating-point addition isn't associative, a typical forward pass has almost no atomic adds, so a single forward pass is deterministic run to run; the problem is that kernels lack batch invariance.\n- Only three kinds of reduction operators need to change: RMSNorm handles each batch element independently on one core; matmuls fix the kernel config and tiling and avoid split-K; attention uses split-KV with a fixed split size (rather than a fixed number of splits).\n- Experiment: 1,000 greedy generations with Qwen3-235B produced 80 distinct outputs, diverging as early as token 103; with batch-invariant kernels they were all identical.\n- Engines: vLLM `VLLM_BATCH_INVARIANT=1` (beta); SGLang `--enable-deterministic-inference`, 34.35% slower on average.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIt matters for reproducible evaluation, regression testing, and RL's on-policy assumption (when sampling and training probabilities match bit for bit, KL can stay at 0). The cost is a throughput drop on the order of 30%–60%; results are reproducible only on the same hardware, version, and parallelism config, and TP's all-reduce also has reduction-order issues.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Setting a random seed isn't enough: the logits themselves change.\n- Why training frameworks are deterministic: batches are fixed, and nondeterminism comes from atomic adds in the backward pass, which can be disabled with a determinism switch.","L3-T3B.1.6":"Concurrency limits for long-context serving are often set by KV capacity (Llama-3.1-70B uses 320 KiB per token, so a 32k sequence needs 10 GiB). KV eviction keeps KV for only some tokens at inference time and permanently discards the rest.\n\n**How it works**\n- StreamingLLM (ICLR 2024): the first few attention sink tokens absorb a large share of attention, and dropping them breaks the model; keeping only the sinks plus a recent window allows stable generation of about 4 million tokens, up to 22.2× faster than sliding-window recomputation.\n- H2O (NeurIPS 2023): a few heavy hitters receive sustained high attention; keep them by cumulative attention plus the recent tokens. At 20% retention, throughput is up to 3× that of FlexGen.\n- SnapKV (NeurIPS 2024): uses attention from an observation window at the end of the prompt to have each head vote on important positions, compressing once after prefill; 3.6× faster generation and 8.2× better memory efficiency at 16k input.\n- PyramidKV: keep more in lower layers and less in higher layers; retaining just 12% matches full KV.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nEviction is irreversible. Needle-in-a-haystack tasks break first; multi-turn conversations and agents are especially risky, since SnapKV selects positions based on the current question, and the tokens needed when the next turn asks about something else may already be gone. Per-token eviction is also hard to reconcile with paged blocks and prefix sharing.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why attention sinks arise: softmax weights sum to 1, so attention with nowhere to go piles onto the initial tokens, which are always visible.\n- Difference from sparse attention: eviction is heuristic and permanent; sparse attention is learned in training, reselects dynamically every step, and keeps the full KV.","L3-T3B.1.7":"KV quantization complements eviction: it keeps the KV for all tokens and only lowers the bit width of each element. FP8 KV is already a standard option, and KIVI (ICML 2024) pushes KV down to 2 bits.\n\n**How it works**\n- KIVI's key is the grouping: keys are quantized in per-channel groups, values in per-token groups.\n- The reason is that large key values concentrate in a few fixed channels. Quantizing per token would let one outlier channel inflate the step size for the whole row and waste the other channels' precision; grouping per channel isolates outliers in their own group.\n- In practice the most recent stretch of tokens is usually kept unquantized to protect the most relevant context.\n- Reported results: peak memory reduced 2.6× (including weights), batch size up to 4× larger, throughput 2.35–3.47×. In a synthetic experiment at 2 bits, per-token key quantization had a relative error of 1.29 versus 0.56 per channel.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nQuantization is less risky than eviction: every token is still there and the error is smooth, so you don't get \"the information the next turn needs is gone,\" and it works more easily with paged blocks and prefix sharing. The costs are dequantization overhead and precision loss; 2 bits requires a dedicated grouping scheme and kernels.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why keys per channel and values per token: their outliers are distributed differently.\n- Production commonly uses FP8 KV; 2 bits is a more aggressive option.\n- Quantization vs eviction: one lowers precision and keeps everything, the other drops tokens irreversibly.","L3-T3B.1.8":"There are many ways to compress KV at inference time, but the default combination in production engines is quite conservative: FP8 KV quantization + model structure (GQA / MLA / sliding window) + tiered offloading (pushing KV down to CPU memory, disk, etc.). Per-token eviction appears mostly in research and specific long-text scenarios.\n\n**How it works**\n- Structural techniques (GQA / MLA / sliding window) are fixed at training time, so KV per token is inherently smaller and quality is ensured by training.\n- FP8 KV keeps all tokens, with smooth error, and has become a standard option.\n- Tiered offloading uses larger, cheaper storage tiers to hold the prefix cache, expanding reusable capacity.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nPer-token eviction conflicts with the engine's core mechanisms: PagedAttention manages memory at block granularity, and dropping scattered tokens fragments blocks; prefix sharing requires complete KV, and after eviction the same prefix no longer has consistent state across requests; high-performance attention kernels assume a contiguous, regular KV layout. Add to that eviction's irreversibility and tendency to lose information in multi-turn and agent settings, and its gains are hard to realize in practice.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When designing long-context serving, lead with structure (GQA/MLA) + FP8 KV + tiered offloading, then discuss eviction as an option for specific scenarios.\n- Be able to explain why eviction is hard to reconcile with paged blocks, prefix caching, and high-performance kernels.\n- Bonus: point out that MLA is ill-suited to TP, so the KV scheme must be considered together with the parallelism strategy.","L3-T3B.1.9":"NSA, MoBA, and DSA are learned sparse attention mechanisms: each query attends to only part of the history. The point most often gotten wrong from the serving side is that they reduce compute and per-step reads, not KV storage.\n\n**How it works**\n- NSA (DeepSeek and Peking University, ACL 2025 best paper): three branches (compression, selection, sliding window) plus gating; the selection branch takes the top 16 blocks, with up to 11.6× faster decoding at 64k.\n- MoBA (Moonshot): selects the top-k blocks by the inner product of the query with each block's mean key; up to 6.5× faster prefill at 1 million tokens, used in Kimi's long-context serving.\n- DSA (DeepSeek-V3.2-Exp): a lightweight lightning indexer scores every historical token, and each query selects 2,048 tokens for actual attention, reducing core attention from O(L²) to O(L·k); the indexer is still O(L²) but cheap.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nvLLM's DSA integration stores the full MLA latent cache (656 bytes per token in FP8), plus an FP8 key cache for the indexer. So concurrency limits based on KV capacity are essentially unchanged; but precisely because KV is complete, prefix caching still works, the opposite of eviction. To compress storage as well, you need sequence-wise compression designs like DeepSeek-V4's CSA / HCA.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- What it saves and what it doesn't: saves compute and reads, not storage.\n- Difference from eviction: it reselects dynamically every step, so a token skipped now can be selected on the next step.\n- An indexer miss can't be recovered, similar to a recall miss in retrieval.","L3-T3B.1.10":"Deploying sparse attention (NSA / MoBA / DSA) in serving isn't simply \"swap the model and it gets faster\"; it comes with clear prerequisites.\n\n**How it works**\n- Gains grow with context: core attention drops from O(L²) to O(L·k) (k = 2,048 in DSA), but the indexer itself is still O(L²), plus extra overhead from selection and scheduling. At short context, L isn't much bigger than k to begin with, so these overheads can cancel out the gains; only in long-context decode do per-step reads and compute drop significantly. This is also what gave DeepSeek the confidence to cut API prices by more than half when V3.2-Exp launched.\n- Dedicated kernels are required: DeepSeek uses a sparse version of FlashMLA, DeepGEMM (for the indexer), and TileLang implementations. Irregular sparse access can't directly reuse ordinary dense attention kernels, so how fast new architectures get deployed depends on engine kernel support.\n- The model must be trained for it: MoBA requires continued training and can't be bolted onto an existing model; DSA uses a dense-attention warm-up to train the indexer to align with the main attention.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nWhen evaluating adoption, look at your workload's context-length distribution: workloads dominated by short conversations may not benefit; long documents and long agent trajectories are where it pays off. Also confirm that your target engine already has the corresponding kernels, or performance may actually get worse.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why it may not pay off at short context: indexer and scheduling overhead.\n- Deployment depends on dedicated kernels + native training of the model.\n- KV storage is unchanged, so the memory-bound concurrency ceiling doesn't rise.","L3-T3B.1.11":"Hybrid models such as Qwen3-Next / Qwen3.5, Kimi Linear, and Nemotron 3 replace most layers with linear attention or Mamba layers whose state size is fixed. KV gets smaller, but mechanisms designed for KV, such as prefix caching, have to be redone, and state checkpointing is the solution.\n\n**How it works**\n- The difficulty: SSM state is updated in place; the state after processing 1,000 tokens can't be rolled back to the state after the first 500, so it can't be sliced by prefix for reuse or rolled back the way KV can. And the state is large (Nemotron-Nano-12B-v2's Mamba state is about 2.57 MiB per sequence, while a 16-token KV block is about 64 KiB).\n- Approach: save state snapshots at block boundaries. SGLang's MambaRadixCache copies the state on a hit, forks a checkpoint on insert, and maintains separate LRUs for Mamba state and KV.\n- vLLM v0.15.0 added block-aligned Mamba prefix caching (`--enable-prefix-caching --mamba-cache-mode align`), giving about a 2× speedup; it can only resume from block boundaries, and finer-grained checkpoint options were added later.\n- Speculative decoding works the same way: prepare one state slot per draft token so it can \"roll back\" when verification fails.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nDenser checkpoints mean higher hit rates, but each one is MiB-scale; block alignment is a compromise between memory and hit rate.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why direct reuse doesn't work: in-place updates, no slicing, no rollback.\n- Why hybrid models keep a few full-attention layers: fixed-size state forgets, and exact retrieval relies on full attention.","L3-T3B.1.12":"In hybrid models, the state that different layer types must keep differs greatly in size: attention-layer KV grows with tokens and is managed in blocks, while Mamba / linear-attention layers keep one fixed-size, relatively large state per sequence. Giving each its own memory pool fixes the ratio, so as load shifts one side runs tight while the other sits idle. Unified memory allocation lets all layer types share a single block pool.\n\n**How it works**\n- vLLM's hybrid memory allocator (v0.9.1, 2025-06): enlarges attention-layer blocks until one page's byte size aligns with a Mamba state page (the blog example uses 672 tokens per block), padding the Mamba pages, so both layer types can take pages from the same pool.\n- The same idea also covers mixes of sliding-window layers + global layers: in 2026 both vLLM and SGLang implemented unified hybrid KV managers; SGLang generalized its radix tree to cover sliding-window, Mamba, and sparse attention together.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nA unified pool avoids the fragmentation and waste of static partitioning and can allocate dynamically with load. The cost is that attention blocks become very large, so prefix-cache granularity gets coarser (hits only at large block boundaries), and padding Mamba pages wastes a little memory. This kind of architecture-aware layout means the engine must understand the model's structure, and new architectures require engine adaptation.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why pages must align: different layers need the same page size to share one pool.\n- Cost: larger blocks make cache granularity coarser.\n- Used together with MambaRadixCache / aligned prefix caching.","L3-T3B.1.13":"Model-free speculative decoding trains no draft model; instead it looks up likely continuations directly in the prompt or past outputs and hands them to the target model for verification. Agent, code-editing, and RL rollout outputs are highly repetitive, which makes them a natural fit.\n\n**How it works**\n- n-gram / prompt lookup: find an n-gram in the prompt matching the last few generated tokens and use the span after it as the guess. vLLM uses `\"method\": \"ngram\"`, with `prompt_lookup_min/max` controlling the match window; v0.18.0 moved it to the GPU and made it compatible with async scheduling.\n- SuffixDecoding (NeurIPS 2025 Spotlight): caches prompts and past outputs in suffix trees (one tree per request plus one globally shared tree), scores candidates by frequency, and adaptively decides how far to guess based on acceptance probability. Up to 5.3× speedup on agent tasks such as SWE-Bench and Text-to-SQL, 2.8× faster than EAGLE-2/3. Integrated in vLLM v0.11.1 (`\"method\": \"suffix\"`, requires arctic-inference).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nCompared with draft models or EAGLE heads, it needs no training, takes no extra memory, and doesn't need updating alongside the target model. Downsides: open-ended writing doesn't repeat, so acceptance is low and verification compute is wasted; at high concurrency the batch already saturates GPU compute, so extra verification tokens are no longer \"free\"; the global tree needs memory control and attention to data isolation across tenants.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why it suits RL rollouts: GRPO samples 8–64 responses per prompt, and they overlap heavily.\n- Gains shrink at high concurrency: the workload shifts from memory-bound to compute-bound.","L3-T3B.2.1":"When serving large MoEs like DeepSeek-V3, experts are spread across dozens or hundreds of GPUs (wide expert parallelism), with only a few experts per GPU, and all the memory saved goes to KV to enlarge the batch.\n\n**How it works**\n- Arithmetic intensity: each token activates only a few experts. If each GPU holds many experts, each expert gets few tokens and expert GEMMs are memory-bound; spreading across more GPUs means fewer weights per GPU, more KV space, larger batches, and more tokens per expert per pass.\n- DP attention: MLA's latent isn't split by head, so with TP every GPU must store a full copy of the KV, wasting memory. DeepSeek-style deployments therefore run attention with data parallelism, each GPU handling different requests and storing its own KV, and then run MoE layers with expert parallelism.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe difficulties: each step's time is set by the busiest GPU, hot experts cause load imbalance (needs EPLB), and there's cross-node all-to-all communication (needs DeepEP, two-batch overlap). The larger the EP group, the larger the blast radius of a single GPU failure, and redundant experts also take memory. This architecture depends on InfiniBand-class networking and hundreds of GPUs.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why attention uses DP rather than TP: the MLA latent can't be split by head.\n- EP vs TP: with very small experts, TP slices too finely; since 2024, large MoEs commonly use EP.\n- Training balances load through routing itself; at inference routing is fixed, so you can only replicate and place experts.","L3-T3B.2.2":"Under wide-EP, each step's time is set by the busiest GPU: when a few experts are especially hot, the GPUs holding them become the bottleneck for the whole EP group. EPLB is DeepSeek's open-source inference-time expert load balancer.\n\n**How it works**\n- Make extra copies of hot experts (redundant experts), then use heuristic bin-packing to distribute the replicas across GPUs so per-GPU load is roughly even.\n- Hierarchical policy: used when the number of nodes divides the number of expert groups. Pack expert groups onto nodes first, replicate within nodes, then assign to GPUs, using group-limited routing to reduce cross-node traffic; suited to prefill with smaller EP sizes.\n- Global policy: ignores grouping and replicates globally; suited to decode with large EP sizes.\n- Load prediction is outside EPLB's scope; the repo suggests a moving average of historical statistics.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nWhen SGLang reproduced DeepSeek's deployment on 96 H100s, EPLB delivered 1.49× speedup for prefill and 2.54× for decode. Costs: redundant experts take extra memory; when the traffic distribution shifts, hot experts drift, and rebalancing requires moving expert weights online.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Load balancing in training vs inference: training changes routing via auxiliary losses or auxiliary-loss-free bias terms; inference routing is fixed, so you can only replicate and place.\n- Why the two policies map to prefill and decode: EP size and cross-node traffic.\n- Why the busiest GPU sets the speed: the EP group must complete all-to-all in sync.","L3-T3B.2.3":"DeepEP is DeepSeek's open-source all-to-all communication library for expert parallelism. It sends tokens to experts (dispatch) and gathers the results back (combine), and is the communication foundation of wide-EP deployments.\n\n**How it works**\n- Normal kernels: target throughput, forwarding \"intra-node NVLink → inter-node RDMA,\" with a controllable number of SMs used; used for training and prefill. On H800, intra-node dispatch / combine reach about 153 / 158 GB/s.\n- Low-latency kernels: RDMA only and CUDA-graph compatible, using a hook-based approach that overlaps communication with computation without occupying SMs; used for decode. Dispatch for 128 tokens ranges from 77 µs at EP8 to 194 µs at EP256.\n- Precision: FP8 for dispatch, BF16 for combine.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIf communication kernels occupy SMs, computation loses that compute and the overlap gain is cancelled; the low-latency kernels being pure RDMA and SM-free is precisely what makes two-batch overlap work. Version note: DeepEP's main branch has been rewritten as V2.5 (a unified `EPBuffer`, an NCCL backend, and removal of V1 and the NVSHMEM dependency); the normal / low-latency kernel pair is the V1 design (e.g. v1.2.1), so cite the version when referencing it.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Division of labor between the two kernel sets: throughput-oriented for training / prefill, low-latency for decode.\n- Why communication must not occupy SMs.\n- Dispatch uses FP8 to cut traffic; combine uses BF16 to preserve precision.","L3-T3B.2.4":"Under wide-EP, each MoE layer has an all-to-all before and after it (dispatch, combine), which across nodes can take as long as the computation; executed serially, the GPU sits idle during communication. Two-batch overlap splits a batch into two micro-batches that alternate: while one computes, the other communicates.\n\n**How it works**\n- DeepSeek: in prefill (EP32), the two micro-batches must balance attention compute, so one prompt may be split across both; decode splits attention into two further steps to form a five-stage pipeline, with RDMA all-to-all that uses no SMs.\n- SGLang TBO (2025-05): describes a layer's execution as \"operations + yield points,\" e.g. attention → yield → dispatch → MLP → yield → combine, with the two micro-batches alternating at yield points; as a bonus, peak memory is halved. It reports 27%–35% improvement for prefill and 25.5% for decode at 256 tokens per GPU.\n- vLLM DBO (dual batch overlap): added in v0.11.0 and enabled with `--enable-dbo`, with a token-count threshold for activation; two worker threads switch at yield points in the MoE kernel, requiring an EP deployment with DP > 1 and full CUDA graphs.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nWhen the batch is too small, each half is too small after splitting, and computation can't hide communication, or it even gets slower; the two halves' compute must be balanced; and the communication library must support asynchronous operation without occupying SMs.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- The training counterpart: DualPipe overlaps forward and backward computation with all-to-all, essentially the same idea.\n- Why a token-count threshold is needed.","L3-T3B.2.5":"P/D disaggregation splits instances by phase; Attention–FFN disaggregation (AFD) goes further and splits by layer type: attention and FFN / experts go into different GPU pools, each scaled independently, even on different GPU models.\n\n**How it works**\n- In decode the two layer types have different bottlenecks: attention cost grows linearly with context length and batch, mostly reading KV, so it is memory-bound and consumes KV memory; MoE expert layers need a large enough batch to be efficient, so they are compute-bound and consume weight memory.\n- On the same GPU, their demands on memory, bandwidth, and compute pull against each other, making it hard to saturate both. Splitting them into two pools lets each choose its own parallelism and scale, with a pipeline shuttling micro-batches between the pools to keep both busy.\n- GPUs can be chosen to fit: high-bandwidth GPUs for memory-intensive attention, compute-heavy GPUs for compute-intensive experts.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nEvery layer has to pass activations across pools once, so network bandwidth and latency are hard constraints and it only works with high-speed interconnects; the ratio between the two pools must adjust with load; and deployment and failure handling get more complex. So far it appears mostly in large companies' in-house systems and papers, with open-source engines still catching up.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why it only makes sense for decode: in prefill both attention and FFN are compute-intensive with similar needs, so splitting them gains nothing.\n- Difference from P/D disaggregation: splitting by phase vs splitting by layer type.\n- Representative implementations: ByteDance's MegaScale-Infer, StepFun's Step-3.","L3-T3B.2.6":"MegaScale-Infer and Step-3 are two representative implementations of Attention–FFN disaggregation (AFD): the former is system-oriented, the latter is a co-design of model and system.\n\n**How it works**\n- MegaScale-Infer (ByteDance, 2025-04): \"disaggregated expert parallelism,\" placing attention and FFN modules in different GPU pools that each choose their own parallelism and scale, with support for heterogeneous hardware; a ping-pong pipeline shuttles multiple micro-batches between the two pools to keep both busy; a custom M2N communication library avoids GPU→CPU copies and synchronization. It reports up to 1.9× higher per-GPU throughput; in a heterogeneous configuration with H20 running attention and L40S running FFN, throughput per unit cost improves 1.7×.\n- Step-3 (StepFun, 2025-07): MFA attention on the model side and AFD on the system side, co-designed; 321B total parameters, 38B active. On Hopper with 4k context, FP8, and TPOT ≤ 50 ms, per-GPU decode throughput is 4,039 token/s, versus 2,324 for DeepSeek-V3 under the same conditions.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe heterogeneous configuration shows AFD can choose GPUs by bottleneck: attention is memory-bound, so use GPUs with relatively ample bandwidth; FFN is compute-bound, so use compute-heavy GPUs. Step-3 shows the attention structure itself can be designed for disaggregated deployment. The shared cost is passing activations across pools at every layer, which depends on a high-speed network.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- What the ping-pong pipeline does: hides cross-pool communication and keeps both pools busy.\n- Be able to cite the heterogeneous-GPU gains and the conditions behind them.\n- Model–system co-design is a trend.","L3-T3B.2.7":"The router in front of multiple inference replicas decides who gets each request. LLM request costs differ by orders of magnitude (100 tokens vs 100k tokens), so round-robin by request count is badly uneven; and if requests from the same session or with the same system prompt land on different replicas, the prefix cache is entirely wasted.\n\n**How it works**\n- Multi-metric scoring: choose a replica by a weighted combination of queue depth, KV utilization, prefix hits, in-flight tokens (the LLM version of \"least connections\"), and so on.\n- NVIDIA Dynamo's KV routing: cost = prefix-hit weight × number of prefill blocks to compute + estimated decode load; with the weight at 0 it reduces to pure load balancing; temperature can be used for softmax sampling.\n- Add randomness to avoid herding: power of two choices or softmax sampling over cost, so that not all requests rush to the same best replica.\n- Admission control: cap the queue, and under overload return 429 quickly or degrade to a smaller model rather than letting requests queue for tens of seconds and then time out.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nLooking only at load throws away the cache; looking only at the cache overwhelms hot replicas. Metrics lag (you see the load from a few hundred milliseconds ago), so multiple router instances may herd onto the same idle replica at once; overly strong prefix affinity overloads the replica holding a popular system prompt; and session affinity conflicts with failover.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Multi-turn agent sessions: session affinity first; under overload or failure, pick the next-best replica by prefix similarity.\n- Why a fast 429 is preferable.","L3-T3B.2.8":"The Endpoint Picker (EPP) is a plugin framework defined by the Gateway API Inference Extension (GAIE, a Kubernetes SIG standard). llm-d implements its InferencePool, letting the gateway choose backend replicas based on LLM-specific state instead of a plain Service's round-robin.\n\n**How it works**\n- Three steps: filter (exclude ineligible endpoints, e.g. model mismatch or overload) → score → select (by default the highest total score).\n- Scorers include: queue depth (preferring short queues), KV utilization, prefix hits, number of running requests, token load (total input + output tokens the endpoint is processing), predicted latency headroom, session affinity, and LoRA affinity.\n- GAIE defines CRDs such as InferencePool so the gateway can pick endpoints by model, priority, and KV state; it is implemented by llm-d and by several gateways including kgateway and Envoy.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nEngines (vLLM / SGLang) solve single-instance efficiency; the orchestration layer solves routing, autoscaling, the P/D ratio, and KV sharing. Without an orchestration layer, Nginx round-robin plus session affinity can work, but cache hit rates under agent workloads will look bad. LoRA affinity sends multi-tenant adapter requests to replicas that already have that adapter loaded, avoiding repeated loading. The cost is that the metrics scoring relies on lag, so randomness is needed to avoid herding.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- The three steps and the main scorers.\n- \"Pick by in-flight tokens\" is the LLM version of least connections.\n- llm-d was accepted as a CNCF Sandbox project in 2026-03.","L3-T3B.2.9":"Power of two choices is a classic load-balancing idea: for each incoming request, randomly pick two replicas and send the request to the less loaded of the two.\n\n**How it works**\n- There's no need to query the real-time state of every replica each time; only two candidates are examined, so overhead is tiny.\n- Theory and practice both show it performs almost as well as \"globally choose the idlest replica\"; compared with purely random assignment, the maximum load drops significantly (the classic result is a drop from order log n / log log n to order log log n).\n- In LLM routing, \"load\" can be queue depth, in-flight tokens, or a composite cost that factors in prefix hits.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nWith multiple router instances, if every router deterministically picks the \"current best\" replica, the few hundred milliseconds of metric lag make them herd onto the same idle replica at once and overwhelm it. Randomness breaks this synchronization while keeping most of the benefit of load-based selection. The cost is no guarantee of the global optimum; when prefix-cache affinity matters, the two random candidates may both lack the cache, so it should be combined with prefix scoring (e.g. filter by prefix first, then pick two among the candidates). A similar idea is the cost-based softmax sampling in Dynamo's KV routing.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- What it solves: querying global state is expensive, and multiple routers herd.\n- Why \"two\" is enough: the marginal gain is largest going from 1 to 2.\n- Same goal as softmax temperature sampling: add randomness to avoid herding.","L3-T3B.2.10":"The inference system overview DeepSeek published on the last day of its open-source week (2025-03-01) is the template for large-scale MoE inference with PD disaggregation + wide-EP; when asked in an interview to \"design an inference system serving a 671B MoE,\" you can follow its structure directly.\n\n**How it works**\n- P/D disaggregation: prefill uses 4-node units, EP32 / DP32, with 9 routed experts + 1 shared expert per GPU, plus 32 redundant experts; decode uses 18-node units, EP144 / DP144, with 2 routed experts + 1 shared expert per GPU.\n- Precision: FP8 for matmuls and dispatch, BF16 for MLA computation and combine.\n- Overlap and balancing: dual micro-batches hide all-to-all, and EPLB balances expert load; disk-level prefix caching achieved a 56.3% input-token hit rate over 24 hours.\n- 24-hour data: an average of 226.75 H800 nodes, daily cost of $87,072; 608 billion input and 168 billion output tokens; a theoretical cost-profit margin of 545% (DeepSeek itself notes actual revenue is far lower).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThree \"whys\": prefill needs large EP to saturate compute, and decode needs even larger EP to spread out weights and free up KV; attention uses DP because the MLA latent can't be split by head; dual micro-batches because cross-node all-to-all is too slow. Limitations: it needs InfiniBand-class networking and hundreds of GPUs; with only a few GPUs, use TP and a conventional deployment. SGLang's reproduction on 96 H100s achieved 5× the output throughput of pure TP.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to explain why prefill and decode use different EP sizes.\n- Point out proactively that the margin is a theoretical figure.","L3-T3B.3.1":"Pipeline parallelism (PP) splits layers into p stages on p GPUs, with micro-batches flowing through in sequence. A stage can only start after the previous one finishes, and the backward pass flows back from the last stage, so at the start and end some GPUs are always waiting; this idle time is the pipeline bubble.\n\n**How it works**\n- GPipe: each stage first completes the forward pass for all m micro-batches, then all backward passes. The bubble is `(p−1)/(m+p−1)` of total time, which the paper says is negligible when m ≥ 4p; each stage must hold m sets of activations at once.\n- 1F1B (PipeDream-Flush): stage s first does p−s−1 forward passes (warmup), then alternates one forward with one backward. The bubble is exactly the same as GPipe, but at most p sets of activations are stored; 1F1B saves memory, not time.\n- The Megatron paper's alternative formulation: bubble time ÷ ideal compute time = `(p−1)/m`; in interviews, be clear about the denominator.\n- Simulation (p = 4, m = 8): both take a total time of 33, with peak activations of 8 per stage versus [4, 3, 2, 1] per stage respectively.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIncreasing m reduces the bubble, but m is limited by the global batch and memory; the first and last stages also hold the embedding and output layers, so load is inherently uneven; and in RL training micro-batches get longer, making the bubble much larger than the formula suggests.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Is 1F1B faster than GPipe? No, it only saves activation memory.\n- Why PP suits cross-node setups: activations pass only at stage boundaries, so communication is small; TP's two all-reduces per layer need NVLink.","L3-T3B.3.2":"1F1B only saves memory without shrinking the bubble. Interleaved 1F1B and Zero Bubble are the two routes that actually compress it.\n\n**How it works**\n- Interleaved 1F1B (Megatron, SC 2021): each GPU handles v non-adjacent chunks of layers (virtual stages), and the bubble becomes `(1/v)·(p−1)/m`, at the cost of multiplying inter-stage communication by v. The paper reports over 10% higher throughput and 52% of peak compute when training a 1T-parameter model on 3,072 GPUs.\n- Zero Bubble (Sea AI Lab, ICLR 2024): splits the backward pass into B (gradient with respect to inputs) and W (gradient with respect to weights). B must be sent to the previous stage and is on the critical path; W waits on nothing and can be deferred to fill bubbles. Under similar memory limits, throughput is up to 23% higher than 1F1B, and up to 31% with relaxed memory.\n- ZB-V: each GPU holds two chunks of layers arranged in a V shape, achieving zero bubble with 1× activation memory; PyTorch 2.6's `torch.distributed.pipelining` has added a ZBV schedule.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nInterleaving trades more communication for a smaller bubble, suiting cases with ample bandwidth; Zero Bubble adds no communication, but deferring W means holding intermediates longer and requires framework support for splitting B / W, making it more complex to implement. In simulation, a greedy schedule that splits B / W reduced total time from 33 to 30 with the same activation cap.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why W can be deferred: it is not on the inter-stage dependency chain.\n- The cost of interleaving: communication multiplied by v.\n- DualPipe builds on Zero Bubble's split to add bidirectional pipelining.","L3-T3B.3.3":"DualPipe is the pipeline schedule used in DeepSeek-V3 training: building on Zero Bubble, it runs a bidirectional pipeline and hides MoE all-to-all communication inside computation.\n\n**How it works**\n- Each chunk is split into four parts: attention, all-to-all dispatch, MLP, and all-to-all combine; the backward pass is split, as in Zero Bubble, into input and weight parts.\n- Micro-batches are fed in from both ends of the pipeline at once, so the computation and communication of a paired forward and backward chunk interleave, and both expert-parallel all-to-all and PP communication can be fully hidden.\n- The bubble is `(PP/2−1)(F&B+B−3W)` (F&B denotes a mutually overlapped pair of forward and backward chunks), versus `(PP−1)(F+B)` for 1F1B; each GPU holds PP+1 sets of activations. DualPipeV is a V-shaped version obtained by \"cutting in half,\" reducing the device count to PP/2.\n- DeepSeek-V3 training configuration: 16-way PP, 64-way EP across 8 nodes, ZeRO-1 data parallelism, deliberately without TP.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe cost is storing two copies of the parameters on each GPU. DeepSeek considers this acceptable because EP is large and each GPU holds few parameters. It shows that in large MoE training, cross-node all-to-all is the main bottleneck, and schedule design must account for communication.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Essentially the same as two-batch overlap in inference: two streams of work hide each other's communication.\n- Cost: 2× parameter memory.\n- Be able to compare the bubble formulas for 1F1B / ZB1P / DualPipe.","L3-T3B.3.4":"Megatron sequence parallelism (SP, NVIDIA, MLSys 2023) complements tensor parallelism (TP): TP only splits the matmuls, so every GPU in the TP group stores full copies of the LayerNorm and dropout activations; SP splits those along the sequence dimension.\n\n**How it works**\n- In non-matmul regions (LayerNorm, dropout), each GPU holds only 1/t of the sequence (t is the TP degree).\n- Before entering TP matmuls, an all-gather reassembles the full sequence; after the matmul, a reduce-scatter replaces the original all-reduce and directly produces the split result; the backward pass does the reverse.\n- A ring all-reduce is already equivalent to reduce-scatter + all-gather, so communication volume doesn't increase, while this activation memory is divided by the TP degree.\n- Combined with selective recomputation (recomputing only the parts of attention that take a lot of memory but are cheap to compute): activation memory drops to about 1/5, and the extra overhead of recomputation falls by over 90%; a 530B model on 2,240 A100s went from 42.1% MFU with full recomputation to 54.2%.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIt's an almost \"free\" memory optimization. But SP is always tied to TP, its scale is limited by the TP degree (usually no more than the 8 GPUs in a node), and it can't be used on its own to handle very long sequences; real long context relies on context parallelism such as Ulysses and Ring Attention.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why communication doesn't increase: all-reduce is decomposed into reduce-scatter + all-gather.\n- Difference from context parallelism: SP does not split the attention computation itself.","L3-T3B.3.5":"DeepSpeed-Ulysses (Microsoft) is a form of context parallelism: it shards along the sequence dimension and uses all-to-all before and after the attention computation to switch between sequence sharding and head sharding.\n\n**How it works**\n- Normally each GPU holds all attention heads for N/P tokens.\n- Before attention, one all-to-all switches to each GPU holding a subset of heads over the full sequence; standard attention runs locally (FlashAttention can be used directly), and another all-to-all switches back to sequence sharding.\n- Communication per link is about `4Nh/P` (3Nh for QKV, Nh for output), which stays constant when sequence length and GPU count grow proportionally; the paper reports 2.5× faster training with 4× longer sequences.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe advantages are that attention itself is untouched and communication scales. Limitations: the degree of parallelism can't exceed the number of attention heads, and with GQA the cap is the number of KV heads. The USP paper's example: Llama3-8B has only 8 KV heads, so the maximum degree is 8; with MQA's single KV head it can't be used. Both it and TP split the head dimension, so they conflict. All-to-all is bandwidth-hungry and suits intra-node NVLink, so USP uses Ulysses within a node and Ring across nodes.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- What each of the two all-to-alls does.\n- Why the parallelism cap is the number of KV heads.\n- Compared with Ring Attention: Ulysses is limited by head count; Ring isn't, but needs large enough chunks.","L3-T3B.3.6":"Ring Attention (UC Berkeley, ICLR 2024) is another form of context parallelism: each GPU keeps its own segment of queries and passes key / value chunks around a ring, computing as they arrive. USP (Unified Sequence Parallelism) combines it with Ulysses into a 2D scheme.\n\n**How it works**\n- For each incoming KV chunk, accumulate with online softmax: maintain each row's running max and denominator, and when a new chunk arrives rescale the existing result before adding; this is the same chunk-merging trick as FlashAttention, and the result matches a single GPU exactly.\n- Transferring the next chunk fully overlaps with computing the current one, so sequence length can scale with the number of devices.\n- Overlap condition: chunk length c must be at least on the order of \"per-GPU compute ÷ interconnect bandwidth,\" or computation can't hide communication.\n- Under a causal mask, early chunks have little computation and later chunks a lot, so zigzag / striped sharding is needed to balance load.\n- USP: Ulysses within a node (all-to-all over NVLink), Ring across nodes; Llama3-8B reached 47% MFU on two 8×A800 machines with 208K sequences.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nRing is not limited by the number of attention heads and scales well; but chunks must be large enough and load must be balanced, and with document masks, masked-out chunks can skip computation, making load even more uneven.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why the result is exact: chunk merging with online softmax.\n- Why zigzag is needed: the causal mask causes load imbalance.\n- USP's hierarchical idea: all-to-all where bandwidth is high, a ring across nodes.","L3-T3B.3.7":"In its long-context training stage, Llama 3 used a form of context parallelism (CP) simpler than Ring Attention: instead of passing KV around a ring, it all-gathers the KV and then computes.\n\n**How it works**\n- Split the sequence into 2×CP segments; rank i takes segment i and segment 2×CP−1−i, pairing the head with the tail.\n- Under a causal mask, early segments need less attention computation and later ones more; head-to-tail pairing gives each rank roughly the same amount of work, balancing load (the same goal as Ring's zigzag sharding).\n- When computing attention, all-gather K and V from all ranks, then compute against the local queries.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nWhy all-gather rather than ring passing: it makes non-standard masks like document masks much easier to support. When training on packed documents, each token may only see preceding tokens within the same document, giving an irregular mask shape that ring-based chunk scheduling handles poorly. And KV is small relative to Q (especially with GQA), so the exposed all-gather latency is small. The cost is that each rank must temporarily hold the full KV, using more memory than ring schemes and limiting scalable sequence length. This reflects the industry's preference for simple and reliable.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why head-to-tail pairing: to balance causal-mask computation.\n- Why all-gather instead of Ring: document masks are easy to support and exposed latency is small.\n- Llama 3 405B overall parallelism: TP=8, PP=16, CP (for long context), DP.","L3-T3B.3.8":"Every form of parallelism in distributed training introduces communication, and almost every systems optimization does the same thing: make communication and computation run at the same time rather than taking turns.\n\n**How it works**\n- DDP gradient bucketing: the backward pass runs from the last layer forward, so DDP buckets parameters roughly in reverse order (`bucket_cap_mb` defaults to 25 MiB), and as soon as a bucket's gradients are ready it launches an asynchronous all-reduce while backward continues; `gradient_as_bucket_view` avoids a copy.\n- FSDP prefetching: each layer needs to all-gather its full parameters before computing. In FSDP1, `backward_prefetch` prefetches ahead by default and `forward_prefetch` is off by default; FSDP2 (`fully_shard`) shards per parameter and prefetches implicitly on a separate CUDA stream.\n- Async TP: splits \"all-gather / reduce-scatter + matmul\" into small pipelined chunks, transferring chunk k+1 while computing chunk k; Llama3-70B's forward pass is about 20% faster and end to end about 8% (64 H100s).\n- EP: dual micro-batches / DualPipe.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nAn often-overlooked detail: communication kernels themselves occupy SMs. DeepSeek-V3 saturated InfiniBand and NVLink bandwidth using only 20 SMs (10 communication channels), building custom communication kernels for the purpose. Costs: prefetched parameters and communication buffers take extra memory; different streams compete for SMs and bandwidth, so individual kernels may slow down; and the root cause of an NCCL timeout may lie on another rank, making debugging harder.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why DDP buckets in reverse order.\n- Communication also occupies SMs; overlap only pays off if it uses few SMs.","L3-T3B.3.9":"Variable-length packing concatenates several short samples into one sequence, eliminating the compute wasted on pad tokens when padding to the longest sample; but simply concatenating them lets later samples \"see\" earlier ones and keeps position IDs counting upward, so it must be paired with document masking.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Document masking**: the attention mask becomes a block-diagonal causal mask, where each token sees only earlier tokens within the same sample. FlashAttention's variable-length interface supports this directly via `cu_seqlens` (the cumulative start/end positions of each sample) without building an N×N mask, and masked-out blocks aren't computed.\n- **Position ID reset**: each sample's position ids start from 0. Hugging Face's `DataCollatorWithFlattening` (since Transformers 4.44) does exactly this; the blog reports up to 2× throughput and 20% less memory on FLAN, with validation loss matching the padded version.\n- **Loss normalization**: after packing, the number of samples per batch varies, and averaging per token weights things differently from averaging per sample; with gradient accumulation, the denominator should be the total token count across the entire accumulation window.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nZhao et al. (ACL 2024) found that an ordinary causal mask lets the model be distracted by unrelated preceding documents; Llama 3 pretraining also used a cross-document attention mask and noted it is especially important in continued pretraining on very long sequences. The costs: bin-packing preprocessing (greedy is enough), kernels must support variable length, and don't pack during evaluation.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Two must-dos for packing: document masking + position id reset.\n- Follow-up \"how is the loss computed\": mind per-token vs per-sample averaging, and the denominator under gradient accumulation.","L3-T3B.3.10":"The systems challenges of MoE training all stem from not knowing in advance how many tokens each expert will get: you have to handle capacity overflow, load imbalance, and cross-GPU all-to-all communication.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Capacity factor** (GShard, Switch Transformer): expert capacity = tokens per batch ÷ number of experts × capacity factor; overflowing tokens are dropped and only pass through the residual. A larger factor drops fewer tokens but wastes more memory and padding.\n- **Dropless**: MegaBlocks uses block-sparse matmuls to avoid dropping tokens (reportedly up to 40% faster than Tutel); today grouped GEMM is common, e.g. Megatron-Core's `--moe-grouped-gemm`, with no capacity factor set by default.\n- **Auxiliary-loss-free balancing** (DeepSeek-V3): add a bias to routing scores that is **used only to select the top-k, while gating weights still use the original scores**; at each step, overloaded experts' bias decreases by γ and underloaded ones' increases by γ (γ = 0.001, set to 0 for the last 500B tokens), while keeping a sequence-level auxiliary loss with α = 0.0001.\n- **Node-limited routing**: each token is sent to at most 4 nodes. NVLink (160 GB/s) is about 3.2× faster than IB (50 GB/s), so after one IB hop a token can be fanned out to multiple experts within the node, with DualPipe overlapping all-to-all and computation.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nTraditional auxiliary losses interfere with the main task; token-dropping schemes behave differently at inference if capacity is set differently. Failure modes include routing collapse and load spikes that cause memory peaks or all-to-all long tails.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why the bias is used only for expert selection: it doesn't change the gate output values, avoiding effects on gradients.\n- Be able to work out the bandwidth arithmetic behind node-limited routing.","L3-T3B.3.11":"In training across tens of thousands of GPUs, failures are routine, and the system's goal is to minimize the time lost per failure: write checkpoints fast and often, recover quickly, and isolate the broken parts.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Sense of scale**: Llama 3 405B pretraining saw 419 unexpected interruptions in 54 days, about 78% hardware-related, with GPU issues accounting for 58.7%, roughly one every 3 hours, yet it still achieved over 90% effective training time.\n- **Young-Daly interval**: optimal interval ≈ √(2 × time to write one checkpoint × mean time between failures); with a 1-minute write and 186 minutes between failures, that's about 19 minutes.\n- **Async checkpointing**: PyTorch DCP's `async_save` first copies to CPU memory, then a background thread writes to disk and returns a Future; for a 7B model the stall dropped from 148.8 seconds to 6.3 seconds.\n- **In-memory checkpointing**: GEMINI (SOSP 2023) writes to each node's CPU memory, recovering over 13× faster than from remote storage.\n- **Localized fault tolerance**: TorchFT uses a Lighthouse to coordinate multiple replica groups, committing or discarding each step like a transaction; a broken replica group pulls weights peer-to-peer from healthy replicas.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe faster you can write, the more often you can write, and the less progress each failure costs. Spot instances in the cloud work the same way: expected loss is roughly half the interval times the number of preemptions; too short an interval wastes I/O, too long loses progress.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to estimate the interval on the spot with the Young-Daly formula.\n- The cost of async checkpointing: extra CPU memory, and parameters must not change until the copy completes.","L3-T3B.4.1":"A colocated architecture has the same set of GPUs alternate between rollout (inference) and training so no GPU sits idle. It is the most common form of RL post-training system, represented by HybridFlow / verl (ByteDance and HKU, EuroSys 2025).\n\n**How it works**\n- One RL iteration = generate rollouts → score → update the policy → hand the new weights to the inference engine; rollout often dominates, and the inference engine and training framework are two separate software stacks.\n- HybridFlow uses a single controller to orchestrate dataflow between multiple models, with multi-controller SPMD execution inside each model.\n- Training shards with FSDP / Megatron, generation shards with vLLM / SGLang TP, and the 3D-HybridEngine reshards the actor weights between the two layouts with \"zero memory redundancy.\"\n- The inference engine works with vLLM sleep mode: during training, inference weights are offloaded to CPU (level 1) or simply discarded (level 2), and woken up again before generation.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe paper reports 1.53–20.57× higher throughput, with phase-transition overhead up to 71.2% lower than DeepSpeed-Chat and up to 89.1% lower than OpenRLHF. Pros: no wasted GPUs and a simple implementation; con: rollout and training can only run serially. Colocation suits small models where the two phases take similar time; when rollouts are very long or asynchrony is needed, move to a disaggregated architecture.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why weights must be resharded: training and inference have different optimal parallel layouts.\n- The difference between the two sleep-mode levels.","L3-T3B.4.2":"A disaggregated architecture puts the rollout pool and the training pool on two separate sets of GPUs, each scaling independently and able to run in parallel or even asynchronously, at the cost of tuning the ratio between them and solving weight synchronization.\n\n**How it works**\n- OpenRLHF is built on Ray + vLLM; after training, it syncs weights to the inference engine via NCCL broadcast or same-node CUDA IPC. It also offers a fully colocated option and supports async mode since v0.8.0 (2025-05).\n- vLLM's docs provide a pluggable weight-transfer engine (multi-GPU NCCL, same-GPU IPC) and pause / resume generation interfaces.\n- Synchronization at trillion-parameter scale is itself an engineering challenge: Moonshot's open-source checkpoint-engine reports updating Kimi-K2 (1T parameters) weights across thousands of GPUs in about 20 seconds, with the flow \"copy into GPU memory → broadcast into CUDA IPC buffers → inference engine reloads.\"\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nWhen rollouts are long, as with reasoning models and multi-turn agent interactions, colocation's serial waiting is too costly; disaggregation lets generation and training overlap. Costs: a poorly tuned pool ratio leaves one side idle; each update must transfer the full weights, putting network and reload time on the critical path; and with asynchrony the data becomes off-policy and needs correction.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Choosing colocated vs disaggregated: consider model size, rollout length, and whether asynchrony is needed.\n- Where NCCL broadcast vs CUDA IPC apply (cross-node / same-node).","L3-T3B.4.3":"Async RL means rollouts don't wait for training and training doesn't wait for the slowest rollout: the current policy is trained on data generated by a slightly older policy. The cost is that the data becomes off-policy, so you need to cap how stale it can be and correct for it.\n\n**How it works**\n- **AReaL** (Tsinghua and Ant Group, NeurIPS 2025): rollout workers generate continuously (and can be interrupted to swap in new weights), while training workers update continuously; a cap η bounds how many versions a sample can lag, and η = 0 is synchronous.\n- **Decoupled PPO objective**: sample with the behavior policy π_behav, importance-weight by π_prox / π_behav, and compute trust-region clipping relative to a newer proximal policy π_prox, so older samples can still train stably.\n- The abstract reports up to 2.77× speedup with linear scaling to 512 GPUs.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nSynchronous RL waits for the slowest rollout each round, leaving GPUs idle much of the time. Too much staleness destabilizes training, and async systems are harder to debug and reproduce. The companion script's numbers show why correction is necessary: as the KL between sampling and training distributions grows from 0.006 to 0.59, the relative bias without correction rises from 0.09 to 0.95; full importance sampling is unbiased but its variance rises from 0.077 to 1.15; truncating at C = 2 gives a bias of 0.50 with variance of only 0.031.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- What η means, and the degenerate case η = 0.\n- Decoupled PPO: which policy the importance weight and the clipping baseline are each relative to.\n- Bias–variance trade-off: truncated IS trades a little bias for stability.","L3-T3B.4.4":"Partial rollout is Kimi k1.5's answer to long responses slowing down a whole rollout round: each round gets only a fixed output-token budget, and unfinished trajectories are saved and continued in the next round.\n\n**How it works**\n- Each rollout round has a fixed output-token budget; trajectories exceeding it go into a replay buffer and continue generating in the next round, with rollout workers running asynchronously.\n- Training (Megatron) and inference (vLLM) are deployed as sidecars, and switching from training to inference takes under 1 minute.\n- A related over-sampling approach: APRIL sends extra requests, stops once the target count is reached, and recycles the unfinished parts into the next round (integrated into slime), reporting an average 22.5% and up to 44% higher rollout throughput.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nReasoning models' response lengths vary enormously, and a few very long responses determine the time for the whole round. Partial rollout spreads the long tail across rounds so GPUs no longer sit idle. Costs: a single response spans multiple policy versions, making it off-policy data that needs importance-sampling correction; and if over-sampling discards unfinished responses instead of recycling them, it systematically favors short responses and may teach the model to \"write shorter.\"\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Partial rollout vs \"discarding slow samples\": the latter introduces length bias.\n- Data spanning versions needs off-policy correction.","L3-T3B.4.5":"In-flight weight updates are PipelineRL's approach (ServiceNow, 2025-09): the inference engine swaps in new weights partway through generation and keeps going, without waiting for the current response to finish before syncing.\n\n**How it works**\n- Whenever training produces new weights, they are pushed to the inference engines that are generating; the engines load them mid-generation, keep the existing KV, and generate subsequent tokens with the new policy.\n- As a result, the first and second halves of the same response come from different policy versions.\n- The paper reports about 2× faster learning on 128 H100s.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nTraditional synchronous updates wait for the whole rollout batch to finish, and async schemes (such as AReaL) have samples that lag by several versions overall. In-flight updates keep data as fresh as possible: most tokens are close to the latest policy, staleness is lower, and the inference engine doesn't have to stop and wait. The cost is that each trajectory mixes multiple policies, making probability-ratio computation and importance correction more complex and the system harder to debug and reproduce; and the KV for the old prefix was computed with old weights, so it isn't fully consistent with the new ones.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Compared with partial rollout: both let one response span multiple policy versions, but partial rollout continues across rounds, while in-flight swaps weights mid-round.\n- Follow-up: how to compute logprobs over mixed versions: recompute on the training side and apply per-token importance correction.","L3-T3B.4.6":"Training–inference mismatch is when the rollout engine (vLLM / SGLang) and the training framework (FSDP / Megatron) load the same weights yet compute different probabilities for the same token, turning nominally on-policy RL into off-policy RL and, in severe cases, crashing training.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Sources**: different kernels (fused kernels, different reduction orders); different precision (inference often uses FP8 / INT8 / BF16 kernels, training uses BF16 + FP32 accumulation); different MoE routing (tiny logit differences make top-k pick different experts, amplifying the error).\n- **Monitoring**: for the same batch of rollouts, recompute logprobs with the training framework and compare them with those recorded during rollout; track the mean absolute token-level difference, the P99 and max of the importance ratio, and differences bucketed by sequence length. A thickening tail in the ratio distribution often precedes a crash.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nA blog post by Feng Yao et al. notes that cases even arise where the sampler's probability is 1 and the trainer's is near 0; in DAPO training of Qwen2.5-32B, the maximum probability gap between the two sides reached 1.0 with INT8 rollouts (about 0.4 with BF16). Returning the true sampling probabilities or switching lm_head to FP32 doesn't fix it. Corrections include TIS / MIS, MoE routing replay, FP16, and bitwise-consistent kernels, each with its own cost.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Three sources: kernels, precision, MoE routing.\n- Be specific about monitoring metrics: logprob gap, importance-ratio tail, length buckets.","L3-T3B.4.7":"TIS / MIS are the most direct algorithmic fixes for training–inference mismatch: since the sampling distribution differs from the training distribution, weight by the ratio of their probabilities and limit extreme ratios.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Truncated importance sampling (TIS)**: proposed by Feng Yao, Liyuan Liu, et al. in 2025-08, it multiplies the gradient by `min(π_train / π_infer, C)`, where C is a hyperparameter; the blog experiments used 2 and 8, and verl's corresponding parameter defaults to 2.0.\n- **Masked importance sampling (MIS)**: samples whose ratio exceeds C are dropped outright (multiplied by an indicator function) rather than truncated; AReaL also uses dropping.\n- verl's rollout correction module offers token-level / sequence-level and truncation / masking variants as selectable presets.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIn DAPO experiments on Qwen2.5-32B, TIS helps clearly when the gap between the two sides is large (e.g. INT8 rollouts) and not at all when it's small. Full importance sampling is unbiased but high-variance; truncation introduces bias in exchange for stability. In the companion script, truncating at C = 2 gives a bias of 0.50 and variance of only 0.031, while full IS has variance of 1.15. MIS is more conservative by dropping samples, but wastes data and may introduce selection bias against certain kinds of samples.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- The difference between truncation and masking, and the cost of each.\n- The choice of C reflects the bias–variance trade-off.\n- Correction only mitigates; it can't replace narrowing the gap itself (FP16, routing replay, consistent kernels).","L3-T3B.4.8":"MoE routing replay (R3, Peking University and Xiaomi, arXiv 2510.11370) specifically targets training–inference mismatch in MoE models: tiny logit differences between inference and training make top-k select different experts, multiplying the error.\n\n**How it works**\n- During rollout, the inference engine records the top-k experts selected for each token at each layer.\n- During training, instead of reselecting experts from the training-side logits, the inference-time expert choices are reused, guaranteeing both sides follow the same computation path.\n- The gradient through the training-side router logits is kept, so the router can still learn.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe paper reports a significant reduction in KL between the two sides, outperforming GSPO and TIS. vLLM's 2026 vime framework reports routing replay reducing the logprob gap between the two sides on Qwen3-30B-A3B from about 0.019 to about 0.013. Compared with after-the-fact weighting like TIS, R3 eliminates routing differences, the largest source of error, at the root. The costs are that the inference side must additionally record and return per-layer top-k indices (storage and communication overhead), and the training side must support externally specified routing; it fixes only routing, not the residual differences from kernels and precision.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why mismatch is worse in MoE than in dense models: discrete top-k selection turns small continuous errors into different computation paths.\n- R3 and TIS can be combined.","L3-T3B.4.9":"These are the two routes to eliminating training–inference mismatch at the numerical level: switch to FP16 with its longer mantissa, or have training and inference use the exact same batch-invariant kernels for bitwise consistency.\n\n**How it works**\n- **FP16 instead of BF16**: a paper by Sea AI Lab and NUS (arXiv 2510.26788) points out that FP16 has 10 mantissa bits versus BF16's 7; switching both training and inference to FP16 (with loss scaling) nearly eliminates the mismatch with just a few lines of code.\n- **Bitwise consistency**: both sides use the same batch-invariant kernels so results don't change with batch composition. vLLM and TorchTitan demonstrated bitwise-consistent on-policy RL on Qwen3-1.7B in 2025-11; TorchTitan's TitanRL in 2026-08 has training and vLLM generation share the same model code and kernels.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nFP16 is cheap, but its dynamic range is narrower than BF16, so loss scaling is needed to prevent overflow / underflow. Bitwise consistency is the most thorough solution; in the demo it was 2.4× slower than the non-bitwise-consistent version, because reduction order must be fixed and some fusion and split optimizations given up. TIS and R3 are after-the-fact or partial corrections; these two routes make \"on-policy\" actually true.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Remember the mantissa bits: FP16 has 10, BF16 has 7, a difference of 3.\n- Why bitwise consistency is slow: batch invariance requires a fixed splitting scheme (e.g. fixed split size), sacrificing parallelism.","L3-T3B.4.10":"Long-tail rollouts arise because response lengths from reasoning models and agents vary enormously: within a batch, a few very long responses determine the time for the whole rollout round while the other GPUs sit idle. It is the most common efficiency problem in RL systems.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Partial rollout**: a fixed token budget per round, with very long responses carried over to the next round.\n- **Over-sample and stop when enough**: APRIL (AMD, CMU, LMSYS, UCLA) sends extra requests, stops once the target count is reached, and recycles unfinished parts into the next round (integrated into slime), improving rollout throughput by 22.5% on average and up to 44%.\n- **Tail batching**: RollPacker concentrates prompts prone to long tails into a few long rounds.\n- **Engine-level optimization**: Seer (Moonshot and Tsinghua, OSDI 2026) splits the scheduling of long responses, uses context-aware scheduling, and applies adaptive grouped speculative decoding to responses in the same group, achieving up to 2.04× throughput and 72%–94% lower tail latency.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe key trap: if over-sampling simply discards the slowest responses without recycling them, it **systematically favors short responses**, and the model may learn to \"write shorter.\" The fundamental fix is an async architecture so training doesn't wait for the slowest response, but that comes at the cost of off-policy correction.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why discarding slow samples is biased: length correlates with difficulty and correctness.\n- Be able to list the layers: scheduling (partial / over-sampling), batch organization (RollPacker), engine (Seer), architecture (async).","L3-T3B.4.11":"Tail batching is the core idea of RollPacker (arXiv 2509.21009): rather than letting every round be held up by a few very long responses, gather the prompts likely to produce long tails and process them together in a few dedicated \"long rounds.\"\n\n**How it works**\n- Identify prompts likely to produce very long responses (long-tail prompts).\n- Process them together in a few long rounds; most other rounds contain only short responses and move quickly.\n- The whole process keeps synchronous training semantics: each round still trains on data generated by the current policy, introducing no off-policy data.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe paper reports 2.03–2.56× speedup over veRL. Compared with partial rollout and async RL, its biggest advantage is that it preserves on-policy semantics, needs no importance-sampling correction, and behaves algorithmically the same as the synchronous baseline; compared with \"over-sample and discard slow samples,\" long responses aren't thrown away, so there's no bias toward short responses. The costs are needing fairly accurate prediction of which prompts will produce long tails, and that training on long prompts is concentrated in time, so the data order differs from uniform sampling.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- The essential difference between RollPacker and partial rollout: the former reorders prompts and stays synchronous; the latter truncates and continues across policy versions.\n- Why \"synchronous training semantics\" matters: no off-policy correction needed, easier to reproduce and compare.","L3-T3B.5.1":"Agent workloads differ greatly from chat workloads: one task is dozens of sequential LLM calls interleaved with tool calls of unpredictable duration, and this shapes every technical choice in an agent serving system.\n\n**Key characteristics**\n- Each call's input is \"the same ever-growing context + a bit of new content,\" with an input:output ratio often on the order of 100:1 (an empirical figure from Manus).\n- First-hand data from vLLM's AgentX trajectory benchmark: a median of 43 turns per session, a median input of 142k tokens, and a prefix hit rate above 96%.\n- So the core metrics are **prefix cache hit rate** and **end-to-end task completion time**, not per-call TTFT.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nOptimizing per request (e.g. driving down TTFT, default eviction policies) misses the big picture: KV evicted during tool calls forces recomputing the whole context; the scheduler re-queues every call, so long tasks get repeatedly cut in line. Supporting techniques come in two layers: the context-layout layer (stable prefix, append-only, deterministic serialization, logit masks instead of adding/removing tools) and the engine-infrastructure layer (KV handling during tool calls, program-level scheduling, speculative tool execution, sandbox snapshots). For example, vLLM × Mooncake Store raised the cache hit rate on SWE-bench Pro agent trajectories from 1.7% to 92.2%, with 3.8× throughput.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain that an input:output ratio of 100:1 means prefill and caching are the main problem.\n- Metrics must shift from the call level to the task level.","L3-T3B.5.2":"When a request pauses on a tool call, there are three options for its KV cache: keep it on the GPU, discard and recompute it, or swap it out to CPU. InferCept (UCSD, ICML 2024) proposes choosing with a unified waste metric.\n\n**How it works**\n- Traditional engines treat every external call as the end of generation and re-prefill the whole context when the tool returns; InferCept measured this recomputation at 37%–40% of total model forward time.\n- **Min-waste policy**: measure \"wasted memory × time.\" Waste from keeping = tool duration × context tokens × memory per token; waste from discarding comes from recomputation slowing down the whole batch. Each paused request defaults to whichever is smaller, and the limited swap bandwidth per round is allocated in decreasing order of waste.\n- Combined with layer-wise pipelined transfers (overlapping swap with compute), budgeted chunked transfers, and chunked recomputation using idle compute. Reported throughput gains are 1.6–2×.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nNumbers from the companion script: with 2k context and a 50 ms calculator call, keeping wastes 0.012 GiB·s versus 3.2 for recomputing, so keep it; with the same 2k but a 30-second wait, keeping rises to 7.3, so discard; with 128k and a 30-second wait, keeping is 469, discarding 205, and swapping only 21, so it should get swap bandwidth first. Costs: tool durations are hard to predict; swapping depends on CPU memory and PCIe bandwidth, and multiple GPUs swapping at once compete for it.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Keep for short tools, discard or swap for long ones, and prioritize swapping for long contexts.\n- \"Treat it as the end of the request and recompute\" is often the worst choice.","L3-T3B.5.3":"With prefix caching, discarding KV becomes cheap: when the tool returns, as long as the prefix blocks are still cached, only the new content needs computing. The problem is that plain LRU doesn't know the request will be back soon, so during tens of seconds of tool wait the blocks may already be evicted. TTL pinning and prefetching are designed for exactly this.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Continuum** (UC Berkeley et al., arXiv 2511.02230): when a request ends on a tool call, pin its KV on the GPU for a TTL; the TTL is set by \"probability the tool returns within τ × recomputation and re-queuing saved − cost of occupied memory,\" with the probability taken from the historical tool-duration distribution; it also schedules first-come-first-served per program. On real SWE-agent trajectories, average task completion time improved by more than 8×.\n- **KVFlow** (UCSD and AWS, NeurIPS 2025): models multi-agent workflows as a step graph, decides eviction by \"how many steps until next execution,\" and prefetches KV from CPU back to GPU before an agent runs; up to 1.83× (single workflow) and 2.19× (multiple workflows) speedup over SGLang's hierarchical cache.\n- **Product counterparts**: Dynamo's `nvext.cache_control` ttl; TensorRT-LLM's `TokenRangeRetentionConfig`; Anthropic prompt caching's default 5-minute TTL with an optional 1 hour (2× write price).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nA long TTL ties up memory, a short one pins for nothing; pinning too much keeps new requests out, so there must be an unpinning fallback.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- How to estimate tool duration: track historical distributions per tool name, with a conservative default for cold start.\n- At the application layer: use the 1-hour TTL for long waits, or persist to disk to free resources.","L3-T3B.5.4":"TokenCake (Peking University, arXiv 2510.18586) is a KV memory-management scheme for multi-agent scenarios. Its core idea is to actively move idle KV out of the way during function calls and to prioritize agents on the critical path.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Swap out during calls**: once an agent issues a function (tool) call, its KV is idle while waiting, so TokenCake swaps it out to CPU, freeing GPU memory for other requests.\n- **Predictive swap-in**: based on predicted function-call duration, it swaps the KV back to the GPU before the call returns, avoiding a wait for the transfer on return.\n- **Critical-path reservation**: in multi-agent workflows, it reserves memory for agents on the critical path, so they aren't slowed by memory contention and don't in turn slow down the whole task.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIt reports up to 47.06% lower end-to-end latency under memory pressure. It belongs to the same family of \"what to do with KV during tool calls\" as InferCept (choosing among keep / discard / swap by a waste metric), Continuum (TTL pinning), and KVFlow (prefetching by workflow step); the difference is combining duration prediction with critical-path awareness. The cost is dependence on the accuracy of call-duration prediction: predicting too early wastes memory, too late still means waiting for swap-in; swapping is also limited by PCIe bandwidth.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to lay out InferCept, Continuum, KVFlow, and TokenCake in a single comparison table and explain each one's focus.\n- Critical-path reservation reflects a program-level (rather than request-level) perspective.","L3-T3B.5.5":"Autellix (UC Berkeley, Google DeepMind, Shanghai Jiao Tong University, arXiv 2502.13965) treats the whole agent program, rather than an individual LLM call, as the unit of scheduling, addressing head-of-line blocking at both the call level and the program level.\n\n**How it works**\n- Intercept each program's LLM calls and record cumulative program-level service.\n- **PLAS** (single-threaded programs): serve the program with the least cumulative service first, i.e. classic \"least attained service\" scheduling, which approximates shortest-job-first when job lengths are unknown.\n- **ATLAS** (multi-threaded / DAG programs): take the maximum cumulative service across a program's threads, approximating the critical path without explicitly tracking dependencies.\n- Both are discretized into multilevel feedback queues, with priority boosted when the ratio of waiting time to service time gets too high, to prevent starvation.\n- With multiple engines, short requests (≤ 2,048 tokens) go to the least-loaded engine, while long requests stay pinned to their program's engine to preserve the cache.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nCompared with vLLM, program throughput improves 4–15× at the same latency. Costs: the application must pass a program ID to the engine (the OpenAI-compatible API has no such field, so a custom header or extension field is commonly used); favoring short tasks slows long ones, requiring starvation prevention and SLA tiers; and across engines, cache affinity and load balancing pull against each other.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- PLAS vs ATLAS: they differ in what cumulative service is aggregated over (a single thread vs the max across threads).\n- What your own agent platform can do: attach a session / task ID to requests and monitor task-level completion time.","L3-T3B.5.6":"Parrot (Microsoft Research and Shanghai Jiao Tong University, OSDI 2024) introduced semantic variables: explicitly marking input and output placeholders in prompts so the server understands the data dependencies between multiple LLM calls.\n\n**How it works**\n- The application declares certain fragments of a prompt template as semantic variables (e.g. the output of one step as the input to the next).\n- The server uses them to build a dependency graph across calls.\n- With the dependency graph, the server can: chain dependent calls directly on the server, saving client round-trips; infer whether each request should prioritize latency or throughput based on the overall goal; and discover and share common prefixes.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe paper reports an 11.7× improvement in a MetaGPT multi-agent scenario. With traditional APIs, the server sees only independent requests and knows neither which requests are waiting on others nor the end-to-end goal; Parrot exposes the application's structure to the engine and is a form of program-level scheduling. The cost is that applications must adopt its programming interface, which is incompatible with the standard OpenAI-compatible API, making migration expensive; and the dependency graph covers only declared variables, so dynamic control flow still requires client involvement.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Compared with Autellix: Autellix needs only a program ID and no dependency information; Parrot requires explicit data-dependency declarations in exchange for server-side chained execution.\n- Value: fewer round-trips, end-to-end goal awareness, prefix sharing.","L3-T3B.5.7":"SGLang (NeurIPS 2024) started out as an LM program runtime: designed for programs with \"multiple generation calls + control flow,\" not just a single-request inference engine.\n\n**How it works**\n- The frontend is a DSL embedded in Python, letting one program contain multiple `gen` calls, branches, parallel forks, and other control flow.\n- The frontend always sends the full prompt to the runtime and doesn't manage caching itself.\n- The runtime uses RadixAttention: it organizes all requests' KV into a radix tree, automatically performs longest-prefix matching and reuse, and manages memory with an eviction policy.\n- The paper reports up to 6.4× throughput.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nMulti-turn chat, few-shot, agent loops, and tree search naturally have many shared prefixes, and RadixAttention lets applications reuse them without explicit declarations. Compared with Parrot's semantic variables, SGLang doesn't require applications to expose a dependency graph; it gets reuse from \"send the full prompt + automatic prefix matching,\" a simpler interface; but it has weaker awareness of cross-call scheduling goals (such as task-level priority), which is exactly what program-level scheduling work like Autellix and ThunderAgent adds. SGLang is also a mainstream open-source inference engine, with overlap scheduling, tool parsers, and more.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- RadixAttention's data structure and where its gains come from.\n- The design trade-off of \"frontend sends the full prompt, runtime reuses automatically.\"","L3-T3B.5.8":"ThunderAgent (Georgia Tech, CMU, UIUC, Together AI, ICML 2026) further extends the notion of a \"program\" in program-level scheduling: it covers not only LLM calls and KV but also system state and tool resources.\n\n**How it works**\n- An agent program is treated as a whole that occupies several kinds of resources: KV cache on the GPU, system state, and tool-side resources (disk, ports, etc.).\n- Two components work together: a program-aware scheduler that queues by whole program rather than individual call, and a tool resource manager that manages and reclaims the resources needed for tool execution.\n- It applies to online agent serving as well as to agentic RL rollouts (which are essentially large numbers of concurrent agent programs).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIt reports 1.5–3.6× serving throughput and 1.8–3.9× for RL rollouts. Earlier work (Autellix, Parrot) focused mainly on LLM-side scheduling, but if tool-side resources (such as sandbox disk and ports) leak or are contended, tasks can still stall or fail. Managing both sides together avoids mismatches like \"GPUs are free but tool resources are exhausted\" or vice versa. The costs are higher system complexity, the need for deep integration with the tool execution environment, and reliance on applications to provide program attribution.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Difference from Autellix: the scheduling scope extends from LLM calls to tool resources.\n- Be able to say it serves both inference and RL rollout scenarios.","L3-T3B.5.9":"Speculative tool execution carries the idea of speculative decoding over to the tool layer: before the large model has decided its next step, use a cheap method to guess which tool it will call and execute it in advance; if the guess is right, use the result directly, overlapping LLM generation with tool execution.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Speculative Actions** (Columbia University, ICLR 2026 oral): a fast speculator predicts the slow actor's next action and issues the call ahead of time, caching the result keyed by call arguments; the result is used only if the actor's actual call matches exactly, so it is lossless. It reports up to 55% next-action prediction accuracy and up to 20% lower end-to-end latency.\n- **PASTE** (Microsoft Research et al., arXiv 2603.18897): exploits recurring control-flow and argument-passing patterns in tool calls to predict and execute concrete calls while the LLM is generating, keeping results isolated until the LLM confirms them; the latest version reports a 43.5% reduction in average task completion time.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nAgent latency comes from generation and tool execution alternating serially, each waiting on the other. The safety prerequisite is a \"safety envelope\": only speculate on side effects that are idempotent, reversible, or confined to a sandbox, and have a rollback or compensation path ready. Tools with side effects such as placing orders, sending email, or writing to databases must never be speculated; wrong guesses also waste tool fees and quota.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"Use only on exact match\" guarantees losslessness, analogous to the verification step in speculative decoding.\n- The safety boundary is a must-mention: only speculate on idempotent / sandboxed operations.","L3-T3B.5.10":"Parallel and streaming tool calls are the two most common ways to reduce agent latency: one cuts the number of serial turns, the other lets a tool start as soon as its arguments have been generated.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Parallel tool calls**: in one turn the model issues several independent calls, the client executes them concurrently, and all results are returned together in the next message. OpenAI uses `parallel_tool_calls: false` to restrict to zero or one call; Anthropic uses `tool_choice.disable_parallel_tool_use`. Note that vLLM's `parallel_tool_calls=false` keeps only the first call after the fact; it is not a decode-time constraint.\n- **Streaming argument parsing**: vLLM enables it with `--enable-auto-tool-choice --tool-call-parser`; the hermes parser re-parses all text at each step and diffs against what has been sent, emitting the tool name first and then incremental arguments; SGLang uses incremental partial-JSON parsing, and when `tool_choice` is required, uses XGrammar constraints to guarantee validity. Anthropic's fine-grained streaming sets `eager_input_streaming: true` per tool, with arguments not buffered or JSON-validated by the server.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nCutting turns can bring 2–5× end-to-end improvement. Risks: parallelized calls that actually have dependencies will fail, and side-effecting tools need idempotency and retry considerations; streamed arguments may be partial or invalid JSON (e.g. truncated by `max_tokens`), so the client must handle it. Before optimizing, use traces to break down latency by segment, and look at p95 rather than the mean.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"How to optimize a slow chain of 3 tools\": trace breakdown → parallelize independent calls → merge tools → cache the prefix → tool-side caching and timeouts.\n- Be able to point out the pitfall that vLLM's disabling of parallel calls is an after-the-fact filter.","L3-T3B.5.11":"Agent code sandboxes, RL environments, and tree-search branches all need to quickly clone many copies from a given state. microVM snapshots use on-demand memory page loading and copy-on-write to bring restore and fork down from seconds to milliseconds.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Firecracker** (AWS, NSDI 2020): the VMM starts in about 8 CPU milliseconds and reaches guest init in no more than 125 ms, and a 1 vCPU / 128 MiB VM has no more than 5 MiB of management overhead. On snapshot restore, the memory file is mapped with `MAP_PRIVATE`, pages are loaded on demand, and writes go through copy-on-write.\n- **The uniqueness problem with fork**: when one snapshot is restored multiple times, random seeds, IDs, and tokens are all duplicated; the official docs warn that it's unsafe not to restore uniqueness, and the mitigation is VMGenID.\n- **Research frontier**: DeltaBox uses a layered copy-on-write file system + incremental dumps + forking from a frozen template process, with 14 ms checkpoints and 5 ms rollbacks; Branching Policy Optimization snapshots the sandbox at high-entropy steps and forks K actions, scoring 3.6–6.1 points higher than GRPO / RLOO at equal compute.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nBooting a sandbox with dependencies installed from scratch takes seconds to tens of seconds, and one agentic RL batch may need hundreds of environments starting from the same initial state. Costs: snapshot files are as large as memory, so storage and transfer are expensive; the first few seconds after restore are slower due to page faults; and GPU state snapshots are still immature. On the isolation spectrum, containers are fast but share the kernel, gVisor is a user-space kernel, and microVMs provide kernel-level isolation.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Copy-on-write + on-demand loading is the fundamental reason it's fast.\n- After a fork, regenerate random numbers, session tokens, and network connections.","L3-T3B.5.12":"REAP (ASPLOS 2021) exposed the hidden cost of on-demand snapshot loading: restoring from a snapshot starts quickly, but memory pages load only on fault, so once the function begins running it triggers frequent page faults and actually runs slower.\n\n**How it works**\n- On snapshot restore, the memory file is only mapped (e.g. Firecracker's `MAP_PRIVATE`), not actually read in; each access to an unloaded page triggers a page fault that reads it from storage.\n- REAP found that functions started from snapshots took 95% longer to execute because of page faults.\n- Solution: on the first execution, record the memory pages the function actually touches (the working set), then on later restores prefetch the whole working set into memory in one batch, avoiding page-by-page faults.\n- Result: 3.7× lower cold-start latency.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThis shows that \"restore time\" isn't just how fast the restore call returns but the total time from restore until the work is actually done. For agent sandboxes and RL environments, a slow first few seconds after restore directly lengthens every task or rollout. The trade-off of prefetching the working set: prefetch too much and it degenerates into a full load, losing the benefit of on-demand loading; when the working set varies with input, the recorded page set may be inaccurate and needs periodic updating.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Distinguish \"fast snapshot restore\" from \"fast execution after restore.\"\n- Recording the working set + batch prefetching is a general idea, similar to OS read-ahead.","L3-T3B.5.13":"E2B and Modal are the most common hosted sandbox products for agent code execution, and both expose snapshot / restore capabilities through APIs.\n\n**Mechanisms and numbers**\n- **E2B**: each sandbox is a Firecracker microVM, providing kernel-level isolation; pause / resume preserves the file system and memory, including running processes. Pausing takes about 4 seconds per GiB of memory and resuming about 1 second; one snapshot can spawn multiple new sandboxes, which makes forking easy.\n- **Modal**: sandboxes support file-system, directory, and memory snapshots (memory snapshots are alpha); function-level memory snapshots make functions with heavy initialization start 3–10× faster. For isolation, Modal uses gVisor (a user-space kernel), and it supports GPU snapshots, making it suitable for code execution that needs GPUs.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nRunning your own Firecracker cluster means dealing with a pile of issues: images, networking, snapshot storage, uniqueness, and more; hosted products package all of that. When choosing, consider: isolation strength (microVM kernel-level vs gVisor user-space kernel vs containers sharing the kernel), whether you need GPUs, pause/resume latency and billing, and whether snapshots can be forked. The engineering essentials stay the same: no network or an egress allowlist, CPU / memory / time limits, no secrets inside the sandbox, and one sandbox per session, destroyed after use.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to explain the isolation difference: E2B uses Firecracker, Modal uses gVisor.\n- Snapshots for agentic RL: create environments in bulk from the same initial state.","L3-T3B.6.1":"The main recent shift in inference engines is treating CPU overhead as a first-class problem: with small models and large batches, a GPU step takes only a few milliseconds, and CPU-side scheduling, input assembly, and detokenization leave the GPU idling between steps.\n\n**Key developments**\n- **vLLM V1** (alpha in 2025-01): the scheduler and model execution moved into a separate EngineCore process, with tokenization, detokenization, and HTTP streaming in the API process, communicating over ZeroMQ; it introduced persistent batches that update only what changes each step, claiming up to 1.7× the throughput of V0. v0.11.0 (2025-10) removed the V0 engine.\n- **Async scheduling**: while the GPU computes step t, the CPU is already preparing step t+1; added as experimental `--async-scheduling` in v0.10.0 (2025-07) and on by default from v0.14.0 (2026-01). SGLang's equivalent is called the zero-overhead batch scheduler.\n- **Model Runner V2**: prepares inputs directly on the GPU, eliminating per-step CPU–GPU synchronization, with 56% higher throughput on Qwen3-0.6B; it became the default for dense models in v0.25.0 and for all models in v0.29.0 (2026-09).\n\n**Trade-offs**\nOverlap means step t+1 can't see step t's full results, complicating preemption, speculative decoding, structured outputs, and stop conditions; async scheduling in v0.11.0 could produce garbled output during preemption, fixed only in v0.11.1, so run correctness regression tests before going live.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- How to diagnose a CPU bottleneck: low GPU utilization and gaps between kernels on the nsys timeline.\n- Why it's less noticeable for large models: each GPU step takes longer, so the CPU share is small.","L3-T3B.6.2":"Progress in MoE and RL systems in 2025–2026 concentrated on two threads: open-sourcing the toolchain for large-scale expert parallelism, and training–inference mismatch becoming a recognized problem in RL systems.\n\n**MoE systems**\n- 2025-02: DeepSeek's open-source week released DeepEP (an expert-parallel communication library), DualPipe (a bidirectional pipeline schedule that hides all-to-all inside computation), and EPLB (expert load balancing).\n- 2025-03: DeepSeek published its inference system: PD disaggregation + EP144 decode, about 14.8k tokens/s of decode per node; 2025-05: SGLang reproduced it on 96 H100s at 22.3k tokens/s of decode per node.\n\n**RL systems**\n- 2025-08: Feng Yao et al. proposed truncated importance sampling (TIS).\n- 2025-09: Moonshot open-sourced checkpoint-engine (about 20 seconds to update trillion-parameter weights); long-tail / async rollout work such as APRIL, RollPacker, and PipelineRL appeared in a cluster.\n- 2025-10: the R3 routing replay paper and the \"eliminate training–inference mismatch with FP16\" paper were published.\n- 2025-11: vLLM and TorchTitan demonstrated bitwise-consistent on-policy RL (2.4× slower); Seer was published.\n- 2026-07 to 09: TorchTitan launched TitanRL, with training and vLLM generation sharing the same model code and kernels.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to string the fixes together by time and approach: after-the-fact weighting (TIS) → eliminating routing differences (R3) → eliminating numerical differences (FP16, bitwise consistency).\n- The open-source toolchain moved large-scale EP deployment from in-house builds to open, reproducible setups.","L3-T3B.6.3":"Recent progress in agent serving marks inference engines starting to offer dedicated interfaces and first-hand data for agent workloads, rather than optimizing only for single chat requests.\n\n**Key developments**\n- **NVIDIA Dynamo 1.0** (2026-03): supports agent hints (requests can carry priority and expected output length), TTL-based prefix pinning (`nvext.cache_control`, where the router pins matching prefix nodes on the corresponding worker for a period), and KV retention for long sessions.\n- **Continuum** (2025-11): proposed setting KV TTLs from the tool-duration distribution.\n- **ThunderAgent** (2026-02, ICML 2026): program- and tool-resource-aware scheduling.\n- **vLLM × Mooncake Store** (2026-05): raised the cache hit rate on SWE-bench Pro agent trajectories from 1.7% to 92.2% with 3.8× throughput, showing the value of a distributed KV pool for multi-turn agents.\n- **vLLM AgentX** (2026-07 to 09): published a benchmark analysis of agent trajectories: a median of 43 turns per session, a median input of 142k tokens, and a prefix hit rate above 96%.\n\n**Why it matters**\nThese data confirm that agent workloads are \"long context + high prefix reuse + many sequential turns,\" and engine-level interfaces like TTLs, priorities, and program IDs are designed around exactly these characteristics.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- How your own agent platform can use them: attach a session / task ID and priority to requests, use long TTLs for long waits, and monitor task-level completion time and prefix hit rate.\n- Be able to cite AgentX's workload numbers to support your argument.","L3-T3B.6.4":"This topic comes with six Python scripts in `code/ch55/`, each with its own assertions; run them with `python3 <script name>`, and no GPU is needed on a Mac.\n\n**Scripts and exercises**\n- `serving_scheduler_sim.py`: scheduling simulation. Vary `token_budget` (128 / 512 / 2048) to see TTFT and ITL p99 trade off against each other; implement swap-style preemption and compare it with recompute; add an SJF policy.\n- `pipeline_schedules.py`: pipeline schedules. Verify the bubble formula; implement interleaved 1F1B, where bubble/ideal is about (p−1)/(v·m); make backward 3× forward to see the gain from zero-bubble.\n- `kv_cache_budget.py`: KV budget. Compute the concurrency limit for any model at 128k context; you should be able to compute KV bytes per token in your head.\n- `batch_invariance_demo.py`: vary the split threshold to observe at which step outputs diverge; write a \"fixed split size\" split-KV version and verify it is batch-invariant.\n- `rl_offpolicy_correction.py`: off-policy correction. Add a mask estimator to compare bias and variance, and sweep C to plot the trade-off curve.\n- `tool_call_kv_policy.py`: KV policy for tool calls. Switch tool durations to a long-tailed distribution, decide by expected waste, and implement a TTL pinned at P90.\n\n**Going further**\nOn a GPU with 24 GB or more, run an 8B model with vLLM, toggle chunked prefill, prefix caching, `--async-scheduling`, and `VLLM_BATCH_INVARIANT=1`, and use `vllm bench serve` to record throughput, TTFT, ITL, and preemption counts in a one-page report you can use as an interview portfolio piece.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Answering with numbers you ran yourself is more convincing than reciting numbers from papers."});
